      "text": "\u9009\u9879 C or B?\n\nWait, let me re-read the question. The question is asking what role does the statement about the relative therapeutic success play in the critic's argument.\n\nThe critic's argument is that the theories are poor in certain aspects (inelegant, not dispelling mystery) but this is not important because they have therapeutic success. So the success is presented as a reason to overlook their flaws. So the success is countering the objections against them. That would be option C: overriding some considerations against the theories.\n\nOption D says \"disprove evidence against these theories\". Maybe \"disprove\" is too strong. The success doesn't disprove the flaws (they are indeed inelegant), but it dismisses their importance. So C is better. The considerations against are the flaws, so the success overrides those considerations. So C is correct. B says it illustrates the most important aspect of scientific theories. The critic says that the importance of the theories isn't in being elegant or explanatory, but in their therapeutic success. So the most important aspect is effectiveness, so the success is illustrating what the critic thinks is important. So B could also be a contender.\n\nHmm. Let me think again. The question is asking what role does the success statement play in the argument. The structure is:\n\nPremise 1: Theories have flaws (inelegant, don't dispel mystery).\n\nPremise 2: But this is not important.\n\nPremise 3: They produce the right results (therapeutic success over rivals).\n\nConclusion: Therefore, the flaws aren't important because of the success.\n\nThe success is used to counter the criticism (the flaws are not important because of the success). So the role is to override the considerations against (the flaws). So option C.\n\nOption B's phrasing is about the most important aspect of scientific theories. The critic is saying that the most important aspect is therapeutic success (as opposed to elegance or explanatory power). The success statement is used to illustrate what the critic considers important. So B says it illustrates the most important aspect of scientific theories. The argument is that the theories are poor in terms of being good theories (scientifically, perhaps), but their success makes that unimportant. Thus, the critic is prioritizing therapeutic over scientific qualities. So the success is showing that the important thing is effectiveness, not the scientific aspects. So B could be correct.\n\nHmm, now I'm confused between B and C. Let me see the options again.\n\nOption C: It is used to override some considerations against these theories.\n\nThe considerations against are the flaws (inelegant, not dispelling mystery). The success is presented as a reason why those considerations (that the theories are poor) don't matter. So the success is overriding the considerations against the theories. So C is correct.\n\nOption B: Illustrate what the critic thinks is the most important aspect of scientific theories. The critic says that the theories are poor in being scientific (since they are inelegant etc.), but their success is what matters. So the most important aspect for the critic is not the scientific aspects but the therapeutic success. Thus, the success is used to illustrate that the most important aspect is not scientific merit but practical success. So B would be correct as well? Wait, but the question is about the role in the critic's argument. The argument is structured as: even though they have flaws (considerations against), but because of their success, those flaws are not important. The success is used to counter the negative points. So the role is to override the considerations against. So C is the answer.\n\nB is about illustrating the most important aspect. The critic's point is that the flaws are not important because of the success. So the success shows that what's important is the therapeutic results. Thus, the success is illustrating that the important aspect is the therapeutic success. So B is also possible.\n\nWait, the question is about the role of the statement. The statement is that the theories have greater therapeutic success. The role of this statement is to counter the previous criticism. The previous criticism was that they are poor theories (because of inelegance and not dispelling mystery). The success is presented as the reason why the previous flaws are not important. So the success is used to override those considerations against the theories. Therefore, C is correct.\n\nB is more about the importance of the aspect (therapeutic success being important), but the statement is used to counter the criticism, so C is better. So I think C is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe critic acknowledges that popular psychological theories have flaws (inelegant and fail to dispel mysteries) but argues these flaws are unimportant because the theories achieve therapeutic success. The role of the therapeutic success claim is to counter the criticisms against the theories by showing their practical effectiveness outweighs their theoretical shortcomings. \n\n**Option C** is correct because the therapeutic success is used to \"override some considerations against these theories\" (i.e., the flaws mentioned). The success does not disprove the flaws (as in D) but dismisses their significance, making them less relevant. Option B incorrectly frames the success as illustrating \"the most important aspect of scientific theories,\" but the critic emphasizes therapeutic results over scientific merit rather than defining what makes a scientific theory important. Thus, the therapeutic success primarily serves to counter objections, aligning with **C**.\n\n**Answer: C**",
