      "text": "\u9009\u9879 C.\n\nWait, let me re-express the tea dealer's argument as a syllogism.\n\nThe dealer says:\n\nAll authentic Yunshan tea is produced in Yunshan (Premise 1).\n\nThis tea is produced in Yunshan (Premise 2).\n\nTherefore, this tea is authentic Yunshan tea (Conclusion).\n\nThis is the fallacy of affirming the consequent. The form is:\n\nIf A (authentic), then B (from Yunshan).\n\nTherefore, if B then A.\n\nWhich is invalid.\n\nNow, looking for the same in options:\n\nOption C says:\n\nAll drivers (A) have a license (B).\n\nXiao Zheng has B.\n\nTherefore, he is A (driver).\n\nSame structure as dealer's argument: All A are B \u2192 B \u2192 A.\n\nTherefore, this is the same fallacy. So the answer should be C.\n\nBut earlier I was confused because I thought option A was valid, but the structure of option C matches exactly.\n\nOption D's structure is different because it's about two conditions. Even though it's a fallacy, it's a different type. The dealer's fallacy is about a single necessary condition being treated as sufficient, which is exactly what option C is doing.\n\nSo the correct answer is C.\n\nBut wait, the dealer's argument's conclusion is that the tea is authentic because it's from Yunshan. The correct option must mirror that structure.\n\nYes, option C does that.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe tea dealer's argument uses the flawed logic of affirming the consequent. The dealer assumes that because the tea is from Yunshan (a necessary condition for being authentic Yunshan tea), it must therefore be authentic. This structure follows the form: \"All A (authentic tea) are B (from Yunshan). This item is B, so it must be A.\"\n\n### Analysis of Options:\n- **Option A**: \n  - Structure: All Class 3 students are diligent. Xiao Li is in Class 3, so Xiao Li is diligent. \n  - This is **valid** if the premise \"all Class 3 students are diligent\" is true. It does not commit the same fallacy.\n\n- **Option B**: \n  - Structure: Flying brand cars are made in Country X. A car is not a Flying brand, so it is not made in Country X. \n  - This is the fallacy of the inverse (denying the antecedent), not the same as the dealer\u2019s reasoning.\n\n- **Option C**: \n  - Structure: All drivers must have a license. Xiao Zheng has a license, so Xiao Zheng is a driver. \n  - This matches the dealer\u2019s fallacy: \"All A (drivers) have B (license). This item has B, so it must be A (driver).\"\n\n- **Option D**: \n  - Structure: A good doctor needs skills and ethics. Xiao Chen has both, so he is a good doctor. \n  - This assumes necessary conditions (skills/ethics) are sufficient, which is a different type of fallacy (treating necessary conditions as sufficient for multiple criteria).\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe dealer\u2019s reasoning is best mirrored by **Option C**, as it incorrectly infers sufficiency from necessity using a single condition (location/license).\n\n**Answer: C**",
