      "text": " Wait, but in this arrangement, P is in all three, so P is in three, so \"only one\" would not apply, but M is in two, and P is in three. So P is in three, so he is not in only one. So \"P or M is a member of only one\" would require at least one of them is in one. But in this case, P is in three and M in two. So neither is in only one. Thus, this would be a counterexample where option C is false. Therefore, C might not hold. So perhaps my initial thought was wrong.\n\nWait, so the question is: can we have a scenario where neither P nor M is in only one committee? If yes, then C is not necessarily true. So let's try.\n\nLet me try to make a case where both P and M are in two committees each, and another member is in three.\n\nWait, but the triple is someone else. Let's say the triple is F.\n\nWait, can F be the triple? Let's see:\n\nTriple is F. Then F is in all three subcommittees.\n\nNow, F and G cannot be together, so G cannot be in any of the three with F.\n\nSub1: F, H, I (but H and I can't be together! Wait, that's a problem. Because H and I can't be in same subcommittee. So Sub1 can't have both H and I.\n\nSo maybe Sub1: F, H, M.\n\nSub2: F, G, P (since F is allowed here, but G can't be with F. Wait, Sub2 has F and G together? That's not allowed. So that's invalid.\n\nSo F can't be in a committee with G. So G has to be in committees without F.\n\nLet me try again with triple as F:\n\nSub1: F, H, M.\n\nSub2: F, I, P.\n\nSub3: F, G, (needs someone else). But Sub3 has F and G together? No, can't have them. So that's invalid. So Sub3 can't have F and G.\n\nWait, so if triple is F, then in each committee he must be present, but G can't be in any committee with F. So G must be in committees without F. But since each committee has three people, and F is in all three committees, the other two members in each committee must not include G when F is there.\n\nWait, so Sub1: F, H, M.\n\nSub2: F, I, P.\n\nSub3: F, (needs two more). But G can't be with F, so the other two in Sub3 must be non-F and non-G. The remaining available are H, I, M, P, but already in previous committees. Wait, but let's see:\n\nSub3 must have F plus two others. The available people besides G are H, I, M, P. But each must be in at least one committee. Let's see, H is in Sub1, I in Sub2, M in Sub1, P in Sub2. So Sub3 could be F and, say, H and I? But H and I can't be together. So that's bad. F, H, and P? Then P is in Sub2 and Sub3 (so two).Sub3 would be F, H, P. Then:\n\nSub1: F, H, M.\n\nSub2: F, I, P.\n\nSub3: F, H, P.\n\nWait, but now H is in Sub1 and Sub3 (two). I is in Sub2 only. M is in Sub1 only. P is in two. G is left. He must be in at least one committee, but can't be with F. So where can G go? Since all committees have F except none, because F is in all. So G can't be in any committee, which violates the requirement that every member is in at least one. So that's a problem. Hence, this arrangement is invalid. So making F the triple is problematic.\n\nAlternatively, maybe triple is someone else. Let me try triple as M.\n\nTriple is M. So M is in all three committees.\n\nNow, let's construct the subcommittees:\n\nSub1: M, F, H.\n\nSub2: M, G, I (But H and I can't be together. H is in Sub1, so I is in Sub2. So that's okay.\n\nSub3: M, I, P. Wait, but I is in Sub2 and Sub3. H and I are not together here.\n\nWait, let's check assignments:\n\nSub1: M, F, H.\n\nSub2: M, G, I.\n\nSub3: M, P, and someone else. Let's see who's left? F, G, H, I are already in some. Wait, in Sub3, we need to include someone else besides M and P. Let's say H? But H is in Sub1 and Sub3. But H and I are not together here, which is okay. So Sub3: M, H, P. Wait, but then H is in Sub1 and Sub3 (two times), and P is in Sub3 and maybe another? Let's see:\n\nWait, let's track all:\n\nM is in all three.\n\nF is in Sub1 (only).\n\nG is in Sub2 (only).\n\nH is in Sub1 and Sub3 (two).\n\nI is in Sub2 (only).\n\nP is in Sub3 (only). Wait, but then P is only in one. So that's a problem for our attempt to have both P and M in more than one. Wait, in this scenario, P is only in Sub3. So P is in one committee. So this doesn't satisfy the case where neither P nor M is in only one. Since M is in three, and P is in one. Thus, option C would be true here because P is in only one.\n\nHmm, maybe this approach isn't working. Let's see if we can get both P and M in two committees each.\n\nLet me try with the triple being H. Let's see:\n\nTriple is H, so in all three.\n\nSub1: H, F, M.\n\nSub2: H, G, P.\n\nSub3: H, I, and someone else. The remaining available are M, P, but let's choose M again. So Sub3: H, I, M. Now:\n\nH:3.\n\nF:1 (Sub1).\n\nG:1 (Sub2).\n\nI:1 (Sub3).\n\nM:2 (Sub1 and Sub3).\n\nP:1 (Sub2).\n\nHere, both P and M are each in one or two: M is in two, P in one. So option C says \"P or M is a member of only one\", which is true here because P is only in one. So that still holds.\n\nWait, but to test option C, we need to see if there's a scenario where neither is in only one. Let's try another arrangement.\n\nSuppose the triple is P. So P is in all three.\n\nSub1: P, F, H.\n\nSub2: P, G, M (since F and G can't be together).\n\nSub3: P, I, and someone else. Since H can't be with I, so maybe H is okay here. Wait, Sub3 needs three members. P is there, so plus two more. Let's choose I and M. So Sub3: P, I, M.\n\nNow assignments:\n\nP:3.\n\nF:1 (Sub1).\n\nH:1 (Sub1).\n\nG:1 (Sub2).\n\nM:2 (Sub2 and Sub3).\n\nI:1 (Sub3).\n\nHere, P is in three, M is in two, so neither P nor M is in only one. Wait, but I is in Sub3 only, so that's okay. But in this case, P is in three, M is in two, so the statement \"P or M is a member of only one\" is false because neither is in only one. Therefore, this is a counterexample where option C is not true. Therefore, option C is not necessarily true.\n\nHmm, so my previous thought was wrong. So in that scenario, option C would be false. Therefore, C might not hold. So maybe the correct answer is D?\n\nOption D: One member happens to be a member of two sub-committees.\n\nWait, but from earlier, we know that one member is in three, one is in two, and the rest are in one. So \"one member\" (the one in two) plus the triple is also a member of two? Wait, the triple is in three, so they are also in two. But the question says \"one member\" (at least one) must be in two? Wait, the question says \"one member happens to be a member of two sub-committees.\" Since the structure requires that apart from the triple member (who is in all three), there is exactly one other member who is in two committees, so yes, there must be at least one member in two. So D is saying exactly that. So D must be true.\n\nWait, but let me confirm. The total slots are 9. The triple member takes 3, so remaining 6 slots for five members. Each of those five must be in at least one, so the minimal total is 5, so one must be in two (with 5+1=6). Therefore, exactly one of the remaining five must be in two committees. So there is exactly one member (apart from the triple) who is in two. So the total of members in two committees is one. So \"one member happens to be a member of two\" must be true. Therefore, option D must be true.\n\nBut let me check the options again:\n\nOptions:\n\nA) One of f or g is a member of three sub-committees.\n\nB) One of h or i is a member of three sub-committees.\n\nC) P or m is a member of only one sub-committee.\n\nD) One member happens to be a member of the two sub-committees.\n\nWait, D says \"two\", but the structure requires that one is in three, one in two, and the rest in one. So there is exactly one member (the one in two) who is a member of two. So D must be true. So the correct answer is D.\n\nWait, but in the scenario above where the triple was P and another was in two (M in two), then D is true because M is in two. So yes, D is always true.\n\nBut let me check another scenario where the triple is H and M is in two. Then D holds. If the triple is someone else, like F? Wait, earlier when I tried making F the triple, it didn't work, but let's try again. Suppose the triple is M.\n\nTriple is M:\n\nSub1: M, F, H.\n\nSub2: M, G, I (but H and I can't be together. Wait Sub2 has G and I, which is okay. H is in Sub1.\n\nSub3: M, I, P (but H is not with I here. Wait H is in Sub1, so okay). Then:\n\nM is in three.\n\nF:1.\n\nG:1.\n\nH:1.\n\nI:2 (Sub2 and Sub3).\n\nP:1 (Sub3).\n\nThus, I is in two. So D holds.\n\nAnother scenario where triple is G? Wait, can G be a triple? Let's see:\n\nTriple is G. But F can't be with G, so F must be in committees without G. But G is in all three committees, so F cannot be in any committee. Which is impossible because F must be in at least one. So G can't be the triple.\n\nTherefore, the triple can't be F or G if there's any way to have it? Wait, let me see:\n\nSuppose triple is F:\n\nThen F is in all three. G must be in committees not with F. Since F is in all committees, G can't be in any, which violates the requirement. So F cannot be the triple. Similarly, G can't be the triple.\n\nTherefore, the triple must be H, I, M, or P.\n\nWait, that's an important point. So the triple can't be F or G because they can't be in the same committee as each other, but since the triple is in all committees, that would require F and G to not be in any committee together, but they could be in different committees. Wait, actually, if F is the triple, then every committee includes F, so G can't be in any committee, which violates the requirement that G must be in at least one. So F and G can't be triples. Similarly for H and I: if one of them is the triple, then the other can't be in any committee with them. Let's see, if H is the triple, then I can't be with H in any committee, but I must be in at least one committee. So I could be in other committees with non-H members. That's okay. Similarly, if I is the triple, H can't be with I. So H would have to be in committees without I. But possible.\n\nTherefore, the triple can be H, I, M, or P, but not F or G.\n\nTherefore, options A and B are about F/G or H/I being triple. Since the triple can be M or P, then A and B can be false. So only D must hold.\n\nSo the answer is D.\n\nWait, but let me confirm again. The question says \"one member happens to be a member of two sub-committees\"\u2014so exactly one? Or at least one?\n\nThe problem says \"one member happens to be a member of two\"\u2014the wording suggests that there exists at least one, but given the structure, there must be exactly one (since the total required is that one is in three, one in two, and the rest in one). Thus, there exists at least one, so D must be true. So yes, D is correct.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{D}\n</think>\n\nGiven a street comprehensive management committee with 6 members (F, G, H, I, M, and P) and three sub-committees each consisting of three members, we need to determine which option must be true based on the constraints provided. The constraints are: one member serves on all three sub-committees, F and G cannot be on the same sub-committee, and H and I cannot be on the same sub-committee.\n\nKey points:\n1. Each sub-committee has 3 members, totaling 9 member slots.\n2. One member must be on all three sub-committees (3 slots).\n3. The remaining 5 members must occupy 6 slots (since 9 - 3 = 6).\n4. Each of the remaining 5 members must be on at least one sub-committee.\n5. This results in one member being on two sub-committees and the rest on one each.\n\nAnalysis of options:\n- **Option A**: One of F or G must be on three sub-committees. This is not necessarily true as the triple member could be H, I, M, or P.\n- **Option B**: One of H or I must be on three sub-committees. This is also not necessarily true as the triple member could be F, G, M, or P (though F and G can't be triples due to constraints).\n- **Option C**: P or M must be on only one sub-committee. This is not necessarily true as shown in scenarios where both could be on more committees.\n- **Option D**: One member must be on two sub-committees. This is necessarily true because the structure requires one member (other than the triple) to be on two sub-committees to fulfill the total slots.\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{D}",
