      "text": " The correct answer is B. Wait, let me double-check.\n\nWait, for Xiao Li to have made a call (to Xiao Zhao), he must have answered at least one call from someone else in the department, per the third statement's contrapositive. The only call Xiao Li might have received is from Xiao Wang, since Xiao Wang called everyone. Therefore, if Xiao Li didn't answer Xiao Wang's call, then he didn't answer any calls from others (since Xiao Zhao didn't call him, and others in the department, if any, might not have called him either). Thus, if Xiao Li didn't answer Xiao Wang's call, then he would have never answered any calls from others, which would contradict the fact that he made a call. Therefore, Xiao Li must have answered at least one call, which must have been from Xiao Wang. Therefore, Xiao Li must have answered Xiao Wang's call. Therefore, option B is correct.\n\nLet me check the other options again.\n\nOption C: Someone in the department once called Xiao Wang. Since Xiao Wang is part of the department, and Xiao Wang called everyone, including himself, but the question is if someone else called him. The problem states that Xiao Wang called everyone, but others might have called him. The only other callers are Xiao Li and Xiao Zhao. Xiao Zhao never called anyone, so he didn't call Xiao Wang. Xiao Li only called Xiao Zhao, so didn't call Xiao Wang. Therefore, unless there are other people in the department, no one called Xiao Wang except Xiao Wang himself. But the problem says \"the mobile phone usage of the administrative department\", so if the department has only these three, then the only person who called Xiao Wang is himself. Since the question is whether someone (other than Xiao Wang?) called him. The option says \"someone in the administration department\"\u2014which could include Xiao Wang calling himself, but that's a bit odd. But likely, the option C is suggesting that another person called Xiao Wang. Since Xiao Li and Xiao Zhao didn't, then if the department only has three people, then C is false. But maybe the department has more people? The problem doesn't say. Since the problem only mentions three people, we have to assume the minimal case. Therefore, option C is not necessarily true. So C is incorrect.\n\nOption D: Xiao Wang answered the phone from everyone in the department. Wait, Xiao Wang received calls from whom? The problem states that Xiao Wang called everyone, but others might have called him. Since Xiao Zhao never called anyone, so he didn't call Xiao Wang. Xiao Li only called Xiao Zhao, so didn't call Xiao Wang. So the only calls Xiao Wang received would be from himself? That's impossible. So no one else called him. Therefore, Xiao Wang didn't receive any calls from others in the department, so he can't have answered calls from everyone in the department, unless \"everyone\" includes himself. But that doesn't make sense. So D is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nBased on the given information:\n\n1. **Xiao Wang called everyone in the administrative department**, including Xiao Li and Xiao Zhao.  \n2. **Xiao Li called Xiao Zhao**, but Xiao Zhao never made any calls.  \n3. **People who never answered calls from others in the department have never called anyone**.  \n\n### Analysis of Options:\n- **A) Xiao Zhao never answered a phone call from Xiao Li**:  \n  Xiao Li called Xiao Zhao, but whether Xiao Zhao answered is unspecified. The third statement does not require Xiao Zhao to have answered calls since he did not call anyone. Thus, **this cannot be inferred**.  \n\n- **B) Xiao Li once answered a phone call from Xiao Wang**:  \n  Xiao Wang called everyone in the department, including Xiao Li. If Xiao Li had **not** answered Xiao Wang\u2019s call, he would have never answered any calls (since Xiao Zhao did not call him). However, Xiao Li made a call (to Xiao Zhao), so by the third statement\u2019s contrapositive, he must have answered at least one call. The only call Xiao Li could have answered is from Xiao Wang. **This must be true**.  \n\n- **C) Someone in the department once called Xiao Wang**:  \n  Xiao Zhao never called anyone, and Xiao Li only called Xiao Zhao. Unless there are other unnamed members, no one else called Xiao Wang. The problem specifies only Xiao Wang, Xiao Li, and Xiao Zhao, so **this cannot be inferred**.  \n\n- **D) Xiao Wang answered the phone from everyone in the department**:  \n  Xiao Wang called everyone, but there is no evidence others called him. Xiao Zhao and Xiao Li did not call him. Thus, **this is false**.  \n\n### Conclusion:\nThe only logically valid inference is **B**.  \n\n**Answer: B**",
