ory can use body data. The example given is height data, so this might be true, but the passage's first sentence is broader: about finding appropriate quantities in quantitative research. So the author is not just talking about economic history but quantitative historical research in general, using height as an example in economic history. So B is more specific than the passage's scope.\n\nC) How to select parameters: the first sentence says part of the work is to find appropriate quantities. The rest of the text uses height as an example of such a quantity. So the main idea is about selecting parameters (C), with the height example illustrating that process.\n\nD) Height data freezes socio-economic situations: this is presented as the reason why height is a useful quantity. The author mentions this to support the idea that height is a good parameter. The main point is the broader concept of selecting appropriate quantities (C), with D being a supporting reason for why height is a good example.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer would be C. However, the last sentence of the passage says: \"Height is equivalent to freezing the socio-economic conditions of the past...\" So the author's main point might be to explain that height data serves this freezing function, making D correct. Alternatively, the author's main point is about the importance of selecting such quantities (C), with D being an example of what makes a good quantity.\n\nLooking back at the question: \"which of the following options is what the author mainly wants to explain through this passage?\"\n\nThe first sentence is the thesis: The work of quantitative historical research is to find appropriate quantities. The rest explains that height is an example of such a quantity because it freezes socio-economic conditions. So the main point is C, which is about selecting parameters. The example of height is to illustrate this process. So the answer is C.\n\nWait, but option D is presented as the reason why height is a good parameter. So if the question is asking what the author mainly wants to explain, perhaps the author's main point is that height is a useful measure (D), which is an example of selecting appropriate parameters (C). The question is whether the main point is the general principle (C) or the specific example (D). Since the first sentence sets up the general idea (selecting parameters) and the rest explains with an example, the answer should be C. But maybe the author is using the example to highlight that height data is a good measure, so the main point is D. Let me check the question again.\n\nThe question says: \"what the author mainly wants to explain through this passage?\"\n\nThe first sentence introduces the topic: part of the work is finding appropriate quantities. The rest of the paragraph discusses height as an example of such a quantity. So the main idea is about selecting parameters (C). The example of height illustrates how to do that. So the correct answer should be C. However, the last sentence emphasizes that height freezes the socio-economic conditions, which might make D the main point. But the passage's structure starts with the broader concept and uses height as an example. So the main point is C. Alternatively, maybe the author is arguing that height is a good measure (D), making that the main point. The first sentence is the setup, but the author's focus might be on the example. Hmm, this is a bit ambiguous. Let me see the options again.\n\nOption C: How to select appropriate parameters in quantitative historical research.\n\nThe passage does not elaborate on the \"how\"\u2014it just gives an example (height) of a good parameter. The first sentence states that it's part of the work, but the rest doesn't explain the process of selection, just gives an example. So maybe C is not correct because the passage doesn't explain the method of selection, just provides an example of a good parameter.\n\nOption D states that height data freezes the