h in general. So B might be too narrow.\n\nOption D is directly stated in the last sentence, so that's a strong candidate.\n\nThe question is whether the main point is to explain that height freezes socio-economic conditions (D), which is the reason why it's a good parameter. Since the author spends the paragraph explaining why height is a good quantity (because it freezes the conditions), the main point is to show that height serves this purpose. So the answer is D.\n\nAlternatively, the first sentence is the thesis, so the main point is about selecting parameters (C), with the rest being an example. The question is about what the author wants to explain. If the author is illustrating the process of selecting parameters by using height as an example, then C is correct. But if the focus is on the example's specific utility (height freezing conditions), then D is correct.\n\nThe first sentence: \"Part of the work of quantitative historical research is to find appropriate quantities to reflect the past.\" The rest of the paragraph is about how height is such a quantity. So the author is explaining that height is an example of an appropriate quantity. The main idea is to explain that choosing such quantities (like height) is part of the work, so the main point is C. But the question is which option the author mainly wants to explain. The author might be explaining that height is a good parameter (D) as an example of the process (C). Since the question is about the main point, I think D is the answer because the passage emphasizes the freezing aspect as the reason why height is useful. The first sentence introduces the general idea, but the rest is about why height is a good example, so the author's main point is to explain that height data freezes the socio-economic situation. Hence, D is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe passage begins by stating that a key part of quantitative historical research is identifying appropriate quantities to reflect the past. It then uses height data as an example, emphasizing that average height reflects past socio-economic conditions (specifically living standards during adolescence) and \"freezes\" these conditions. While the first sentence introduces the broader concept of selecting parameters (Option C), the subsequent explanation focuses on height\u2019s role in capturing socio-economic contexts, culminating in the explicit statement: \"Height is equivalent to freezing the socio-economic situation of the past.\" This makes Option D the central point the author emphasizes through the example. \n\n**Answer: D**",
