      "text": " D.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The question is asking \"which one probably will not attend the meeting in the end?\" So the answer would be the pair that cannot attend in any valid scenario, or the one that must not attend?\n\nWait, need to see which pair cannot attend in the possible valid scenarios.\n\nWait in the case when Zhao is out, then Zhao is out, and Sun is out, so Zhao and Sun (option D) are both out. So in that scenario, D is a pair that are both out.\n\nAlternatively in case 1a (Li and Wang in) then Li is in, so the pair Li and Zhao (option C) would have Zhao out, but Li in, so that pair isn't both out. Similarly, in case 1b where both Li and Wang are out, then the pair Li and Zhao (C) would have Zhao out and Li out, so that pair is both out. Wait, but the question is asking which pair \"probably will not attend\", so which pair is necessarily excluded in all scenarios?\n\nWait, the problem might have only one possible scenario. Let me see.\n\nWait when Zhao is out, there are two possibilities: case 1a and 1b.\n\nCase 1a: Li and Wang are in. So the attendees are Zhang, Zhou, Li, Wang. So the people not attending are Zhao, Sun, and... no, wait, Sun is also out. Wait Zhao, Sun, and the others? Wait total attendees would be four: Zhang, Zhou, Li, Wang. So the people not attending are Zhao, Sun, and... no, that's all six. Wait no, the six are Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun, Zhou. So in this case, Zhao is out, Sun is out, but Li and Wang are in. So the ones not attending are Zhao and Sun. So in this case, the pair Zhao and Sun (option D) are both out, and Li and Wang are in. So the pair D would both be out here.\n\nIn case 1b, where Li and Wang are both out, then the attendees are Zhang and Zhou. So the ones not attending are Zhao, Sun, Li, Wang. So here, Zhao and Sun are out (so D is out), and also Li and Wang are out (so B is out, C is out because Li and Zhao are both out?), etc.\n\nWait in case 1b, Li and Wang are both out, so:\n\nThe absentees are Zhao, Sun, Li, Wang. So the pairs that are both out would include Zhao and Sun (D), and also Zhao and Li (C), since both are out.\n\nBut the question is which pair \"probably will not attend\". Since Zhao is always out, and Sun is also always out when Zhao is out (due to constraint 6), thus the pair Zhao and Sun (D) must both be out in any valid scenario.\n\nWhereas for example, Zhao and Li (C): in case 1a, Zhao is out and Li is in, so that pair isn't both out. So C is possible to have one in and one out. So C is not necessarily both out.\n\nOption B (Wang and Li) can be both out in case 1b, but in case 1a they are both in. So they can be both in or both out, so not necessarily both out.\n\nOption A (Zhang and Wang): in case 1a, Zhang is in and Wang is in, so that pair is both in. In case 1b, Zhang is in and Wang is out. So the pair A (both out) would only occur if both are out, which is not the case here. So A is not a pair that must be both out.\n\nTherefore, the only pair that must be both out in every valid scenario is D (Zhao and Sun). Because whenever Zhao is out, Sun must be out, and Zhao cannot be in (as we saw earlier), so Zhao is always out, and thus Sun is always out. So D is the correct answer.\n\nWait but let me confirm once more.\n\nIs there any way Zhao could attend? Earlier, we saw that if Zhao attends, it leads to a contradiction. Let me recheck that.\n\nCase where Zhao is in:\n\nThen Zhang must be out (constraint 1).\n\nLi must be out (constraint 2).\n\nConstraint 3: at least one of Zhang or Wang must attend. Since Zhang is out, Wang must attend.\n\nBut then, Wang is in, so by constraint 5, Li must also be in. But Li is out because Zhao is in. Contradiction. Therefore Zhao cannot be in. So Zhao is definitely out, so Sun must be out. So D's pair is always out. Therefore the answer is D.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{D}\n</think>\n\nGiven the context and constraints, we need to determine which pair of people will not attend the meeting. The key steps are as follows:\n\n1. **Constraints Analysis**:\n   - **Constraint 1**: Only one of Zhang or Zhao can attend.\n   - **Constraint 2**: Only one of Li or Zhao can attend.\n   - **Constraint 3**: At least one of Zhang or Wang must attend.\n   - **Constraint 4**: Exactly two of Zhang, Sun, or Zhou must attend.\n   - **Constraint 5**: Wang and Li must both attend or both not attend.\n   - **Constraint 6**: If Zhao does not attend, then Sun cannot attend.\n\n2. **Considering Zhao's Attendance**:\n   - If Zhao attends, then Zhang and Li must be excluded (Constraints 1 and 2). \n   - This leads to a contradiction because Constraint 3 requires Wang to attend (since Zhang is excluded), but Constraint 5 then requires Li to attend, which contradicts Constraint 2. Thus, Zhao cannot attend.\n\n3. **Zhao Excluded**:\n   - Zhao is excluded, so by Constraint 6, Sun must also be excluded.\n   - This leaves Zhang and Zhou to satisfy Constraint 4 (exactly two of Zhang, Sun, Zhou must attend). Since Sun is excluded, Zhang and Zhou must attend.\n   - Constraint 5 allows Wang and Li to either both attend or both not attend.\n\n4. **Possible Scenarios**:\n   - **Scenario 1a**: Wang and Li both attend. Attendees: Zhang, Zhou, Wang, Li. Excluded: Zhao, Sun. Pair D (Zhao and Sun) is excluded.\n   - **Scenario 1b**: Wang and Li both exclude. Attendees: Zhang, Zhou. Excluded: Zhao, Sun, Wang, Li. Pair D (Zhao and Sun) is excluded.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - Zhao and Sun are always excluded regardless of other attendees. Other pairs (B, C, A) can vary depending on scenarios.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{D}\\).",
