      "text": " So I think C is correct because all third graders are registered here, so some (in fact all) of the registered students are third-grade. D: the \"some first-grade are not migrant workers' kids\" \u2014 since the problem says \"some first-grade are migrant's kids,\" but doesn't say that the rest are or aren't. Wait, but unless there's a total number, we can't assume. Hmm, but actually, in logic, if you have \"some S are P,\" that doesn't let you conclude \"some S are not P,\" unless there's some overlap or other info. But here, the problem states that the children of migrant workers are not registered here. But first-grade students can have some who are migrant's kids (so not registered here), and others who are not migrant's kids (so maybe registered here or not). But the problem doesn't state that first-grade students must have any registered here. Wait, but the problem only says about third-graders that all are registered here. So first-graders who are not migrant's kids might have their registration elsewhere, but the problem doesn't say. But the question is about the existence of some first-grade students who are not migrant's kids. Since the problem says \"some are,\" that means that there is at least one who is a migrant's kid. But the rest could be all or some non-migrant's kids. Wait, but if all first-grade students were migrant's kids, then \"some are\" is still true, so it's possible. Therefore, the statement \"some first-grade students are not children of migrant workers\" would be false in that scenario. So we can't conclude D for sure. Therefore, the only one we can be certain of is C. So the answer is C. D might not be necessarily true. So yeah, C is correct. A is incorrect because some second-grade students are registered (so they can't be migrant's kids), but the others (the unregistered ones) could be migrant's kids. So A says \"all second-year students are not children of migrant workers,\" which is false. B is wrong because third graders are all registered, so migrant's kids can't be in third grade. So B is wrong. D can't be concluded for sure because maybe all first-graders except those some are also migrant's kids. Wait but the problem says \"some first-grade students are children of migrant workers\" \u2014 that leaves open the possibility that there are others who are not. But you can't conclude \"some are not\" unless you have more info. Therefore, the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nC) Some students with household registration in this city are third-grade students  \n**Explanation:**  \nThe context states that **all third-grade students are registered in S City**. Since every third-grader is registered there, this directly implies that **some** (in fact, all) third-grade students are part of the city's registered population. Thus, option C is valid.  \n\n- **A** is incorrect because while some second-grade students are registered (and thus not migrant workers' children), others might not be registered (and could be migrant workers' children).  \n- **B** is false because third-grade students are all registered in the city, but migrant workers' children are explicitly stated to lack such registration.  \n- **D** cannot be definitively concluded because the context only confirms that *some* first-grade students are migrant workers' children. It does not guarantee that others are not.  \n\nThe only logically certain conclusion is **C**.  \n\n**Answer:** C",
