messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nParents who consistently laud their children for every attempt to accomplish something, whether successful or not, actually erode the youngsters' sense of self-esteem. Children require commendation for their achievements, but if uniformly praised for both what they have accomplished and what they have merely attempted, they will eventually discount all words of commendation. In effect, such children never hear any praise at all.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following most accurately expresses the overall conclusion of the argument?\n# Options:\nA) Children will develop low self-esteem if their parents do not praise them when they succeed.\nB) Children's self-esteem will suffer if their parents uniformly praise their attempts to accomplish things regardless of their success or failure.\nC) Parents should praise their children for their achievements.\nD) Children whose actions are praised undeservedly eventually learn to discount all words of praise.", '<think>\nOkay, let\'s tackle this logical reasoning problem.The question is asking for the overall conclusion of the argument presented in the context.The options are A, B, C, or D.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the context thoroughly.The main point seems to be about how parents\' constant praise, even for unsuccessful attempts, affects children\'s self-esteem.The key points from the context are:.\n\n1.Parents who consistently praise every attempt (successful or not) erode the children\'s self-esteem.\n2.Children need praise for achievements but if they get praised for both accomplishments and mere attempts, they\'ll eventually ignore all praise.\n3.The result is that such children "never hear any praise at all," meaning the praise becomes ineffective.\n\nThe question is asking for the conclusion.In logical arguments, the conclusion is the main point that the author is trying to make, supported by premises.The premises here are the reasons given for why the conclusion is true.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Children will develop low self-esteem if their parents do not praise them when they succeed.\n- Hmm, the context does say children need commendation for achievements, but the conclusion seems more about the effect of too much praise.This option is about not praising for success leading to low self-esteem.But the argument\'s focus is on over-praising leading to discounting praise, not under-praising.So maybe not A.\n\nB) Children\'s self-esteem will suffer if their parents uniformly praise their attempts to accomplish things regardless of their success or failure.\n- This directly mentions the uniform praise for attempts regardless of outcome leading to self-esteem issues.The first sentence of the context states that such praise erodes self-esteem, so this seems like a strong candidate..\n\nC) Parents should praise their children for their achievements.\n- The argument does say children require commendation for achievements, but this is presented as a premise supporting the conclusion, not the conclusion itself.The conclusion is more about the negative outcome of over-praising, so C might be a premise.\n\nD) Children whose actions are praised undeservedly eventually learn to discount all words of praise.\n- This is stated in the context as "they will eventually discount all words of commendation."But the conclusion is the main point.The conclusion is that their self-esteem is eroded because of this.The discounting is a step towards the conclusion.The conclusion is about self-esteem, not just the discounting.However, D is a restatement of part of the argument.The consistent praising of children\'s attempts, regardless of success or failure, actually boosts their self-esteem by helping them feel valued and encouraged.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
