an's action would have caused the result. So the illegal act is not the sole cause but part of the chain, but the result was inevitable anyway. Hence, it fits the definition.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the answer is C.\n\nWait, in C, the actor was speeding (illegal) and couldn't brake in time. If they hadn't been speeding, could they have braked in time? If yes, then the result was avoidable by lawful action, so it doesn't fit. If even without speeding, they couldn't have braked in time, then it would fit.\n\nThe problem states \"too late to brake\", which might imply that the driver was speeding, so the braking distance was too long. Therefore, without speeding, maybe he could have stopped. Hence, the result was avoidable by following the law. So C doesn't qualify.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be B.\n\nBut I'm still a bit uncertain because sometimes in these questions, the key is that the result was inevitable despite lawful actions. So B's scenario fits that, because even if B was sober, the pedestrian's sudden rush would still lead to the injury.\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **B**.\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe definition of \"obligatory alternative action\" requires that the actor's illegal act caused the result, but the result would still have occurred even if they had followed the law.\n\n- **Option A** is invalid because the driver acted lawfully (no illegal act).  \n- **Option C** involves speeding (illegal), but the scenario implies that braking in time would have been possible if the driver had obeyed the speed limit (avoidable result).  \n- **Option D** involves an illegal act (overloading) but the result was avoided, so it does not fit.  \n- **Option B** fits the definition: B drove drunk (illegal act), and pedestrian Wang, also drunk, suddenly rushed out. Even if B had driven sober (lawfully), the pedestrian\u2019s sudden action might have made the injury unavoidable. Thus, the result was inevitable regardless of B\u2019s compliance with the law, fulfilling the criteria of obligatory alternative action.  \n\n**Answer:** B",
