      "text": " D is about fat content comparison, which the passage implies. Wait, the passage says meat's fat interferes with absorption, but doesn't directly compare to fish. So maybe D is an inference. Alternatively, maybe B is not correct because it's about protein digestibility, not tyrosine absorption. Let me clarify:\n\nThe problem with meat is the fat interfering with tyrosine absorption. This means that even though meat has tyrosine, because of the fat, the body can't absorb it as well. So, the protein in meat is present but the tyrosine part isn't effectively utilized. So the issue isn't the digestibility of the protein itself (since protein digestion is broken down into amino acids), but the absorption of tyrosine. So, the protein in fish may not necessarily be easier to digest, but the tyrosine in them isn't blocked by fat, so the tyrosine is absorbed better. Therefore, the correct answer between B and D is which one can be inferred.\n\nOption B says \"protein in fish is easy to digest and absorb\". The passage doesn't mention the digestibility of the protein, only that the absorption of tyrosine is hindered by fat. So the absorption here refers to tyrosine's entry into the brain. Therefore, the problem with meat's fat is about the tyrosine absorption, not the protein's digestibility. Therefore, B is not necessarily correct because it's talking about protein digestibility, which isn't mentioned. \n\nLooking at D: \"There is more fat in meat than in fish\". The passage says meat has fat that interferes with tyrosine absorption, but it doesn't explicitly say fish have less fat. However, since fish are listed as a good source of tyrosine (and thus their tyrosine is absorbed well), the implication is that fish have lower fat content. So D can be inferred as a comparative statement. Therefore, D is the correct answer.\n\nWait, but maybe the answer is B? Let me see the options again.\n\nWait, the question is which can be inferred. The passage says that \"Birds and fish contain the most tyrosine, but not all foods containing tyrosine excite the brain. Meat contains tyrosine, but fat interferes with its absorption.\"\n\nSo the fat in meat is the problem. So, perhaps fish, since they are mentioned as a good source of tyrosine (and presumably their tyrosine is absorbed well), would have less fat. Hence, D is correct. \n\nAlternatively, B says \"protein in fish is easy to digest and absorb\". The problem with meat's fat is about absorption of tyrosine, not the protein. So the protein in fish may not necessarily be easier to digest, but the tyrosine's absorption is better. So B is not necessarily correct because the passage doesn't talk about digestibility of the protein itself, only the absorption of tyrosine. So D is the better answer.\n</think>\n\n**Answer: D) There is more fat in meat than in fish.**\n\n**Explanation:**  \nThe context states that while meat contains tyrosine, its fat interferes with absorption. Since fish are highlighted as a good source of tyrosine (implying effective absorption), the implication is that fish have less fat than meat. This comparison allows the inference that **meat has more fat than fish**, directly supporting option D.  \n\n- **Option A** is incorrect because the context does not link protein content to intellectual development, only to immediate alertness.  \n- **Option B** is misleading because the passage discusses tyrosine absorption hindered by fat, not the digestibility of protein itself.  \n- **Option C** is false since fish contain tyrosine, not dopamine (the brain produces dopamine from tyrosine).  \n\nThus, the logical inference is **D**.",
