dden City, the Forbidden City;.\n\nthe fourth candidate: the Temple of Heaven, Tiananmen Square;.\n\nthe fifth candidate: Tiananmen Square, the Forbidden City;.\n\nthe sixth candidate: the Forbidden City, Tiananmen Square, the Forbidden City.".\n\nAh, okay, so each candidate\'s answers are listed, but some have fewer entries.Wait, that can\'t be.Each candidate must have answered all three questions.So perhaps there\'s a mistake in the way the answers are listed here.For example, the second candidate might have three answers, but maybe they are all Tiananmen Square except two?Or maybe the problem is that the answers are written with some missing?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second candidate\'s answers are Tiananmen Square for all three questions, but it\'s written as "Tiananmen Square, Tiananmen Square" with the third missing, which is an error.Alternatively, maybe the second candidate only answered two questions?But the problem states that each question must be answered, so that can\'t be.Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that the second candidate\'s answers are Tiananmen Square for the first two questions, and maybe the third question\'s answer is omitted?That can\'t be.Maybe there\'s a formatting issue here.Let me think differently.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second candidate\'s answers are Tiananmen Square for all three questions, but the problem lists only two entries because of a typo.Similarly, the third candidate has two answers listed, but actually three?Wait, the third is "the Forbidden City, the Forbidden City" – perhaps they answered the first two questions as Forbidden City and the third as something else?But the problem states each candidate must have three answers.Alternatively, maybe the problem is written such that each candidate\'s answers are listed for each question, but in some cases, they repeated the same answer for all three questions.Let me try to re-express each candidate\'s answers properly, assuming that each has three answers:.\n\nCandidate 1: Q1: Temple of Heaven, Q2: Temple of Heaven, Q3: Tiananmen Square.\n\nCandidate 2: All three questions answered Tiananmen Square?But the problem lists two entries.Wait, maybe it\'s a mistake and they meant all three as Tiananmen Square.Alternatively, maybe the second candidate\'s answers are Tiananmen Square for the first two questions and another answer?But the problem only lists two entries.Hmm, maybe the problem\'s formatting is off.Let me check the original problem again.\n\nOriginal problem\'s context:.\n\n"the first candidate: Temple of Heaven, Temple of Heaven, Tiananmen Square;.\n\nthe second candidate: Tiananmen Square, Tiananmen Square;.\n\nthe third candidate: the Forbidden City, the Forbidden City;.\n\nthe fourth candidate: the Temple of Heaven, Tiananmen Square;.\n\nthe fifth candidate: Tiananmen Square, the Forbidden City;.\n\nthe sixth candidate: the Forbidden City, Tiananmen Square, the Forbidden City.".\n\nWait, so for the second candidate, the answer list is two entries.That can\'t be.Each candidate must have three answers.So perhaps there\'s a mistake here.Alternatively, maybe the second candidate\'s third answer is the same as the first two?Like Tiananmen Square for all three, but written as two.Let me assume that maybe the problem had a formatting error, and perhaps the second candidate\'s answers are Tiananmen Square for all three questions.Similarly, the third candidate\'s answers are the Forbidden City for all three?But their listed answers are two entries.Wait, perhaps the problem is that the candidates answered each question with one answer, but some candidates repeated the same answer for multiple questions, so the problem lists them concisely.For example, the first candidate\'s answers are Temple of Heaven for Q1 and Q2, and Tiananmen for Q3.The second candidate answered Tiananmen for Q1 and Q2, and maybe something else for Q3, but the problem only lists two?Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is structured such that each candidate\'s answers are listed per question, but some have typos.Let me try to proceed by assuming that the second candidate\'s third answer is the same as the first two.So:.\n\nCandidate 2: All three answers are Tiananmen Square.\n\nCandidate 3: All three answers are the Forbidden City.\n\nCandidate 4: Temple of Heaven for Q1, Tiananmen for Q2, and perhaps another answer for Q3?Wait, the problem lists "the Temple of Heaven, Tiananmen Square" – that\'s two answers.So maybe they answered Q1 and Q2, but what about Q3?Hmm, perhaps the problem has an error here.Alternatively, maybe the fourth candidate only answered two questions, but that can\'t be since each must answer all three.Maybe the problem\'s formatting is missing some entries.Alternatively, perhaps the fourth candidate\'s answers are Temple of Heaven for Q1, Tiananmen for Q2, and maybe the same as Q2 for Q3, but that\'s speculation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem lists the answers in a way that if they repeat, they just list the first two and the third is same?Not sure.Alternatively, maybe the problem is that the candidates can leave some questions blank?But the problem states "each examinee has answered at least one of the questions correctly", implying they answered all, but maybe they can leave some blank?Wait, the problem says "requiring candidates to choose the name of a scenic spot as the only answer to each question."So they have to choose an answer for each question.So they can\'t leave any blank.Therefore, each candidate has three answers, one per question.\n\nGiven that, perhaps the problem\'s presentation has some typos.Let me proceed by making an assumption based on the given data.Let me try to parse each candidate\'s answers as follows:.\n\nCandidate 1: Q1: Temple of Heaven; Q2: Temple of Heaven; Q3: Tiananmen Square.\n\nCandidate 2: Q1: Tiananmen Square; Q2: Tiananmen Square; Q3: ?Maybe the third answer is also Tiananmen Square, since it\'s listed as two entries but maybe it\'s a formatting error.Alternatively, perhaps the third answer is different?But the problem only lists two.Hmm.\n\nWait, looking at the fifth candidate: "Tiananmen Square, the Forbidden City" – that\'s two answers, so maybe they have Tiananmen for Q1 and Forbidden for Q2, and what about Q3?The problem didn\'t list the third answer.This is really problematic.Alternatively, perhaps the candidates are only required to answer two questions?But the problem says three questions.This is confusing.Maybe the problem is presented in a way that lists the answers for each question, but in some cases, the candidate has the same answer for multiple questions, so they just list the answers once with a count?Like "Tiananmen Square (x3)" but written as "Tiananmen Square, Tiananmen Square".\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is that each candidate\'s answers are listed in the order of the three questions, and the number of answers given corresponds to the number of questions.But the second candidate\'s entry has two answers, implying maybe they didn\'t answer the third question?But that can\'t be.Maybe it\'s a mistake and they meant three answers.Let me proceed with the assumption that maybe there was an error in the problem\'s formatting and that each candidate has three answers, but some have duplicates.Let me try to list them as:.\n\nCandidate 1: [Temp, Temp, Tian].\n\nCandidate 2: [Tian, Tian, Tian] (assuming third is same).\n\nCandidate 3: [Forb, Forb, Forb].\n\nCandidate 4: [Temp, Tian, ???]Hmm, problem lists two answers, so maybe third is missing.Maybe it\'s Tian again?Or Forb?Not sure.\n\nWait, the fourth candidate\'s answers are listed as "Temple of Heaven, Tiananmen Square".That\'s two answers, so maybe they answered Q1: Temp, Q2: Tian, and Q3: something else?But the problem doesn\'t list it.This is a problem.Alternatively, maybe the third answer is same as the second?So Temp, Tian, Tian?Or Temp, Tian, Forb?Not sure.\n\nThis is a critical issue because without knowing all the answers, we can\'t proceed.Maybe the problem\'s original Chinese had different formatting, and in translation, it got messed up.Alternatively, maybe the candidates answered each question with one answer, but the problem lists their answers for each question in a way that\'s abbreviated.For example, if a candidate answers the same for all three questions, it\'s written once.But that\'s just a guess.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem lists the answers for each question in order.Let me try to see:.\n\nLooking at the sixth candidate: "Forbidden City, Tiananmen Square, Forbidden City".That\'s three answers, so probably Q1: Forb, Q2: Tian, Q3: Forb.\n\nThe fifth candidate: "Tiananmen Square, the Forbidden City" – that\'s two answers, so maybe Q1: Tian, Q2: Forb, Q3: ???Missing third answer.\n\nHmm.This is a problem.Maybe I need to proceed by assuming that the second candidate\'s third answer is Tiananmen Square, third candidate\'s third answer is Forbidden City, fourth candidate\'s third answer is maybe Tiananmen Square again, and fifth candidate\'s third answer is something else, but since we can\'t know, maybe there\'s a different approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem lists the answers as a list for each candidate, with the order corresponding to the three questions, but some candidates have fewer answers because they repeated answers?That doesn\'t make sense.Alternatively, maybe the problem is in Chinese and the translation has formatting issues, but the key is that each candidate has three answers, one per question.Let me try to assume that the second candidate\'s third answer is Tiananmen Square, the third candidate\'s third answer is Forbidden City, fourth candidate\'s third answer is, say, the Forbidden City?Or perhaps the problem is that the fourth candidate\'s answers are Temple of Heaven for Q1, Tiananmen for Q2, and maybe the third is missing?But without that, it\'s hard.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is that each candidate\'s answers are listed in the order of the questions, and the numbers correspond.So:.\n\nLet me try to parse again, perhaps the candidates\' answers are:.\n\n1.Candidate 1: [Temp, Temp, Tian].\n\n2.Candidate 2: [Tian, Tian, ?](maybe third is Tian?).\n\n3.Candidate 3: [Forb, Forb, ?](third is Forb?).\n\n4.Candidate 4: [Temp, Tian, ?](third missing?Maybe Forb?).\n\n5.Candidate 5: [Tian, Forb, ?](third missing?).\n\n6.Candidate 6: [Forb, Tian, Forb].\n\nThis is the best I can do.Let\'s proceed with that assumption, even if it\'s a bit shaky, because otherwise I can\'t move forward.\n\nNow, the key is that each candidate got at least one correct answer.So, the correct answers (the three questions\' correct answers) must be such that for each candidate, at least one of their three answers matches the correct one for that question.\n\nOur goal is to find the correct order (Q1, Q2, Q3) from the options A-D.\n\nLet me look at the options:.\n\nA) Temple of heaven, imperial palace, temple of heaven.\n\nWait, the options use different names.The problem mentions Temple of Heaven, Tiananmen Square, and the Forbidden City.But the options use "Imperial Palace" instead of "Forbidden City"?Wait, perhaps "Imperial Palace" is another name for the Forbidden City?Let me check:.\n\nThe Forbidden City is also known as the Imperial Palace in some contexts.So, maybe in the options, "Imperial Palace" refers to the Forbidden City.So in the options:.\n\nOption A: Temple of Heaven, Imperial Palace (Forbidden City), Temple of Heaven.\n\nOption B: Imperial Palace (Forbidden City), Tiananmen Square, Tiananmen Square.\n\nOption C: Tiananmen Square, Imperial Palace (Forbidden City), Temple of Heaven.\n\nOption D: Temple of Heaven, Temple of Heaven, Imperial Palace (Forbidden City).\n\nSo, we can treat "Imperial Palace" as the Forbidden City.So let me note that.\n\nNow, let\'s analyze each option to see if it satisfies the condition that every candidate has at least one correct answer.\n\nLet me consider each option one by one.\n\nStarting with Option C: Tiananmen Square, Imperial Palace, Temple of Heaven.So the correct answers are:.\n\nQ1: Tiananmen Square.\n\nQ2: Imperial Palace (Forbidden City).\n\nQ3: Temple of Heaven.\n\nNow let\'s check each candidate:.\n\nCandidate 1\'s answers: Q1: Temp, Q2: Temp, Q3: Tian.\n\nSo comparing to correct answers:.\n\nQ1: Temp vs Tiananmen – wrong.\n\nQ2: Temp vs Forbidden – wrong.\n\nQ3: Tian vs Temple – wrong.Wait, that\'s a problem.Candidate 1 would have all answers wrong, which contradicts the condition that each has at least one correct.Therefore, Option C can\'t be correct.\n\nWait, so Option C is invalid because Candidate 1 would have zero correct answers.Hmm, so Option C is out.\n\nNext, Option D: Temple of Heaven, Temple of Heaven, Imperial Palace (Forbidden City).\n\nSo correct answers:.\n\nQ1: Temp.\n\nQ2: Temp.\n\nQ3: Forb.\n\nNow check each candidate:.\n\nCandidate 1: Q1 Temp (correct), Q2 Temp (correct), Q3 Tian (wrong).So at least one correct.Good.\n\nCandidate 2: All three answers are Tiananmen.Comparing:.\n\nQ1: Tian vs Temp – wrong.\n\nQ2: Tian vs Temp – wrong.\n\nQ3: Tian vs Forb – wrong.Oh, so Candidate 2 would have all wrong answers.That\'s bad.So Option D is invalid.\n\nMoving to Option B: Imperial Palace (Forbidden), Tiananmen Square, Tiananmen Square.\n\nCorrect answers:.\n\nQ1: Forbidden City.\n\nQ2: Tiananmen.\n\nQ3: Tiananmen.\n\nCheck candidates:.\n\nCandidate 1: Q1 Temp vs Forb – wrong.Q2 Temp vs Tian – wrong.Q3 Tian vs Tian – correct.So at least one correct.Good.\n\nCandidate 2: All Tiananmen:.\n\nQ1: Tian vs Forb – wrong.\n\nQ2: Tian vs Tian – correct.\n\nQ3: Tian vs Tian – correct.So they have two correct answers.Good.\n\nCandidate 3: All Forbidden:.\n\nQ1: Forb vs Forb – correct.\n\nQ2: Forb vs Tian – wrong.\n\nQ3: Forb vs Tian – wrong.So at least one correct.Good.\n\nCandidate 4: Temp (Q1), Tian (Q2), and let\'s assume third is Forb (if I assumed earlier that their third answer was