      "text": " D.\n\nWait, no. Let me parse the options again carefully.\n\nOption A says \"allow others to obtain a benefit such as the one that this artist has obtained in the past.\" The artist obtained a subsidy. Repaying allows others to obtain it. So that matches A's wording.\n\nOption D says the benefits' value exceeds the cost to the person. The passage doesn't discuss the value comparison but rather states that the returned money is welcome to help others. The argument is about the benefit to others, not whether the benefits outweigh the artist's cost. The artist's cost is repaying, which they can do because they succeeded. The moral obligation is based on the fact that repaying helps others, not that the benefits are greater than the cost.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A. The argument is that repaying allows others to get the same kind of benefit (subsidies) that this artist got. Hence, A.\n\nHmm, I'm confused now. Let me check the passage again. The passage says the artist should repay because the money returned will be \"welcome as a source of support for other artists deserving of public subsidies.\" So the key reason is that the repayment helps others get subsidies. The argument is that by repaying, the artist allows others to receive subsidies, which is similar to the benefit they received. So A is saying exactly that: allowing others to get a benefit like the one the artist had.\n\nOption D's language is about the general benefits exceeding the cost. The passage doesn't mention cost-benefit analysis, just that the money helps others. Therefore, A is correct.\n\nI think I initially thought D was right due to the phrasing, but upon reanalysis, A is the better choice. The argument is about the cyclical benefit, allowing others to get the subsidy the artist had. So the answer is A.\n</think>\n\nThe passage argues that an artist who successfully repays a public subsidy is morally obligated to do so because the returned funds will support other deserving artists. This reasoning hinges on the idea that repaying enables others to receive subsidies, mirroring the benefit the original artist obtained. \n\n**Option A** states that acting this way allows others to obtain a similar benefit to what the artist received. This aligns with the passage\u2019s emphasis on using repaid funds to aid future artists, directly tying the artist\u2019s repayment to others gaining the same opportunity. \n\n**Option D**, while plausible, focuses on comparing the \"general benefits\" to the \"cost\" of repaying. The passage does not explicitly frame the decision as a cost-benefit analysis but instead stresses the moral imperative to support others through the subsidy system. \n\nThus, **A** is the most accurate choice because it directly reflects the passage\u2019s argument about perpetuating the subsidy system to benefit others as the artist was benefited.\n\n**Answer: A**",
