n to the terrain.So B is also correct?But "chose" might be an issue.The first sentence of the context mentions the traditional theory which says they "chose" to walk upright.Wait, in the first sentence, it\'s the traditional theory\'s view: "due to climate change, human ancestors were forced to leave the arboreal environment and chose to walk upright on the ground due to climate change."Wait, that\'s a bit confusing.The traditional theory is presented here as saying that climate change forced them out of the trees and then they chose to walk upright (maybe because of the new terrain?Or maybe the traditional theory is different, and the context is adding another reason).Wait, perhaps the context is combining both the traditional theory and another factor.Let me parse the first sentence again:.\n\n"The traditional theory believes that due to climate change, human ancestors were forced to leave the arboreal environment and chose to walk upright on the ground due to climate change.".\n\nWait, the structure here is a bit unclear.Maybe it\'s saying that the traditional theory says climate change caused them to leave the trees, and then they chose to walk upright (maybe because of the new environment).But the wording is awkward.Alternatively, maybe it\'s saying that both leaving the trees and choosing upright walking were due to climate change.The second part of the context then adds that the terrain (from geological changes) attracted them to rocky areas, which required upright walking.So the context is presenting an additional reason (the terrain) beyond the traditional climate change theory..\n\nIn that case, the question\'s options would need to be considered based on the entire context.The main point is that the environment (rocky terrain and canyons) required upright walking, so the upright walking evolved because of that environment, which was formed by geological changes.Therefore, the geological changes (volcanic and tectonic) led to the terrain, which in turn led to the upright walking.So the geological changes are an underlying cause.Thus, option C says "changes in geological conditions changed the way human ancestors walked" — that\'s a valid conclusion because the geological changes caused the terrain, which required upright walking, leading to the change in walking..\n\nAlternatively, option B says they chose upright walking to adapt to the terrain.The context\'s wording says "this environment requires early humans to be upright the ability to walk and climb, so human ancestors eventually evolved.." So the requirement of the environment led to the evolution.So the upright walking was an adaptation to the terrain.Therefore, B\'s statement could be seen as correct, even if "chose" is not the best term.Since the question is about what can be derived from the information given, maybe B is the intended answer because it directly connects upright walking to adapting to the terrain.The geological changes are part of the reason why the terrain exists, but the immediate cause for upright walking is the terrain\'s requirements..\n\nHmm, this is tricky.Let me check the options again:.\n\nOption B: "Human ancestors chose to walk upright in order to better adapt to the terrain environment.".\n\nThe context says the environment required them to have that ability, so they evolved.The "chose" might be a way of expressing the evolutionary adaptation as a choice, but in reality, it\'s natural selection.However, in the context\'s first sentence, the traditional theory uses "chose," so maybe in the context\'s perspective, the answer expects B as correct.Alternatively, the question might consider B as the correct answer because it\'s about adapting to the terrain, which is the main point of the added info in the context beyond the traditional theory..\n\nAlternatively, the key is that the terrain (geological changes) required upright walking, so the geological changes caused the change in walking.So C would be correct.But the question is which can be derived.Let me think again: the process is geological changes → terrain formed → terrain requires upright walking → evolution.So the geological changes are a cause, but the direct reason for upright walking is the terrain.The question asks which can be derived.Both B and C might seem plausible..\n\nWait, the question is from the given information.The context says "the terrain was formed by volcanic eruptions and tectonic plate movement during the Pliocene.Human ancestors were attracted by rocky terrain and canyons.. this environment requires early humans to be upright.. so evolved.." So the main point is that the environment (due to geological changes) required upright walking, so the change in walking was due to the terrain (which was formed by geological changes).Therefore, the geological changes led to the terrain which led to the walking change.Thus, the changes in geological conditions (the cause of the terrain) did change the way they walked.Therefore, C is correct..\n\nAlternatively, B is also correct in that the terrain required them to walk upright.But the wording of B uses "chose," which might not be accurate.The context uses "required" and "evolved," so "chose" is not the right term.So B\'s wording is problematic..\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B if the "chose" is acceptable as a way to express the adaptation, but if it\'s a strict terminology issue, then maybe C. Alternatively, the question might expect B because the main point is the terrain\'s role.Let me check the options again..\n\nOption C says "Changes in geological conditions changed the way human ancestors walked."The geological conditions (volcanic and tectonic) caused the terrain, which required upright walking.So the geological changes are the root cause.Therefore, C is correct..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is B.The question is from the context\'s perspective.The context says "this environment requires early humans to be upright the ability to walk and climb, so human ancestors eventually evolved to walk upright on two legs."So the reason for evolving was the environment\'s requirements.Therefore, the upright walking was to adapt to the terrain environment.Therefore, B is correct if "chose" is interpreted as an evolutionary adaptation (even though "chose" is not precise).The question might consider B as correct because it\'s about adapting to the terrain..\n\nHmm.This is a tough one.Let\'s see the options again:.\n\nA) The destruction of the arboreal environment has forced people to walk into mountain valleys.\n\nThe context says they were forced out of the trees due to climate change and were attracted to the rocky areas.So the destruction (of trees) caused them to leave, but the attraction to the rocky areas (mountain valleys/canyons) was the pull.So A says destruction forced them into the valleys.The "forced" part might be accurate, but the context says they were "attracted" by the terrain.So maybe A is incorrect because it\'s not the destruction that forced them into the valleys, but the destruction made them leave the trees, and then they went to the valleys because they were good.So A\'s wording might not be precise..\n\nB) Human ancestors chose to walk upright in order to better adapt to the terrain environment.\n\nThe upright walking was an adaptation to the terrain, so even if "chose" is not perfect, this is the main point..\n\nC) Changes in geological conditions changed the way human ancestors walked.\n\nYes, because the geological changes created the terrain that required upright walking..\n\nD) Canyon areas provide more abundant food resources.\n\nThe context mentions trapping prey, not more food.So D is not directly supported..\n\nSo between B and C. The question is which can be derived..\n\nThe context\'s conclusion is that the environment (rocky terrain, canyons) required upright walking, so the upright walking evolved because of that environment.The environment\'s existence is due to geological changes.So the geological changes are the underlying cause, but the direct reason is the terrain..\n\nThe question asks which can be derived..\n\nOption B is about adapting to the terrain.The context directly states that the terrain required upright walking, so B is a valid conclusion..\n\nThe geological changes caused the terrain, so C is the correct answer.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 24%|##3       | 141/600 [1:31:20<4:27:40, 34.99s/it]
