laims \"may rain\" means \"it will rain,\" conflating possibility with certainty. This is **incorrect** because \"may\" does not guarantee the event.\n  \n- **Person C** states that \"may rain\" means \"it is not inevitable that it won\u2019t rain,\" which translates to the logical equivalence \\( \\Diamond P \\leftrightarrow \\neg \\square \\neg P \\). This is **correct** in modal logic.\n\n### Conclusion:\nA and C are logically correct, while B is incorrect. \n\n**Answer:** A) A and C are correct, B is incorrect. \n\n\\boxed{A}",
