nt. Let me double-check. The problem says \"only the national is the world.\" In formal logic, \"Only A is B\" is equivalent to \"All B are A.\" So World is a subset of National. Therefore, if something is World, it must be National. The contrapositive is if not National, then not World, so that's B. So B is correct. So the answer should be B.\n\nWait, but the question says \"Based on this, which of the following options is correct?\" So the options are:\n\nA) National \u2192 World\n\nB) \u00acNational \u2192 \u00acWorld\n\nC) \"As long as the nation is, it is the world\" which is National \u2192 World\n\nD) \"Only the world is national\" which would be the reverse.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B. So the correct option is B.\n\nWait but the original context says \"only the national is the world.\" So that's \"World \u2192 National\". The contrapositive is B. So B is correct. Therefore, the answer is B.\n\nBut let me check again. The user might have a different interpretation. Let me see the original Chinese context. The user's translation might be slightly off. The original says: \"which has once again proved that this judgment is that only the national is the world.\" So \"only the national is the world\" is the judgment. That is, the judgment is \"Only national things are world things.\" Therefore, the correct logical form is \"If something is world, then it is national.\" The contrapositive is B. So yes, B is correct. Therefore, the correct answer is B.\n\nWait the user's options are:\n\nA) It's national, it's world.\n\nB) If it is not national, it is not world.\n\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world.\n\nD) Only the world is national.\n\nSo yes, B is the contrapositive. So the answer is B.\n\nWait, but the original problem might be in Chinese, so maybe the translation nuances matter. For instance, \"only the national is the world\" could be interpreted as \"Only what is national can be world,\" which would mean that to be world, you must be national. So that's the same as B. So I think B is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is a bit tricky. Let's see the options again. The problem says the judgment is \"only the national is the world,\" so that is the original statement. The question is, which of the options is correct based on this. The options are:\n\nA) National \u2192 World\n\nB) \u00acNational \u2192 \u00acWorld\n\nC) National \u2192 World\n\nD) World \u2192 National\n\nWait, actually, the original statement \"only the national is the world\" is equivalent to \"All world things are national,\" which is World \u2192 National. So that would be equivalent to D? Wait, D says \"Only the world is national.\" That's not the same.\n\nOption D is phrased as \"Only the world is national,\" which would mean that only things that are world are national? That seems opposite. The original is that to be world, you must be national. So World \u2192 National.\n\nOption D is \"Only the world is national\" which would mean National \u2192 World? Because \"only A is B\" is \"B \u2192 A\". So \"Only the world is national\" would mean National \u2192 World. Which is same as A and C. So D is incorrect.\n\nSo the correct logical form is B. So the correct answer is B. Therefore, I think the answer is B.\n\nWait another way. Let me think of examples. Suppose something is world literature. According to the statement, it must be national. So if something is not national, it can't be world literature. That's exactly what B says. So B is correct. A says if it's national, then it's world. But the original statement doesn't say that being national guarantees being world. So A is too strong. The original only states a necessary condition, not a sufficient one. Hence B is correct.\n\nTherefore the correct answer is B.\n\nWait but wait the options given are A, B, C, D. In the problem statement, the user might have a different phrasing. Let me read again:\n\nThe context says: \"only the national is the world.\" So the judgment is \"only the national is the world.\" The options are:\n\nA) It's national, it's world.\n\nB) If it is not national, it is not world.\n\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world.\n\nD) Only the world is national.\n\nSo the answer is B because \"only national is world\" is equivalent to \"if world then national\", whose contrapositive is B. So yes, B is the correct answer. So I think the correct option is B. Therefore, the final answer is B.\n\nWait, but the user's options might be in a different order. But according to the problem, the options are as above. So yes, B is correct. So I think the answer is B. But let me check once more.\n\nOriginal: \"only the national is the world\" \u2192 for anything to be world, it must be national. So World \u2192 National.\n\nContrapositive: \u00acNational \u2192 \u00acWorld \u2192 B is correct.\n\nA is National \u2192 World, which is the converse and not necessarily true.\n\nC is same as A.\n\nD is the opposite.\n\nThus, correct answer is B. Therefore, I choose B.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{B}\n</think>\n\nThe context states that Mo Yan's works have proven the judgment that \"only the national is the world.\" This means that for something to be considered world literature, it must first be national. \n\nTo analyze the options:\n\n1. **Option A**: \"It's national, it's world\" (National \u2192 World) implies that being national guarantees being world, which is incorrect because the original statement only states that national is a necessary condition, not a sufficient one.\n2. **Option B**: \"If it is not national, it is not world\" (\u00acNational \u2192 \u00acWorld) is the contrapositive of the original statement \"World \u2192 National,\" making it correct.\n3. **Option C**: \"As long as the nation is, it is the world\" (National \u2192 World) is the same as Option A and incorrect for the same reason.\n4. **Option D**: \"Only the world is national\" reverses the original statement and is incorrect.\n\nThe original judgment \"only the national is the world\" translates to \"World \u2192 National,\" whose contrapositive is \"\u00acNational \u2192 \u00acWorld,\" corresponding to Option B.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
