= H - h, so H_CERT - H = -h). So (H_CERT - H) = -h <0. So H_CERT + (T - H) <50 \u2192 H_CERT + (T - H) = (H - h) + (T - H) = T - h <50. Since h is small (like 1), T would have to be less than 50 + h. But T is 100, so this is impossible.\n\nWait, maybe my approach here is wrong. Let me try again with T=100.\n\nIf I want to maximize C (the number of non-HSD certs), then C can be as high as possible without causing total certs to exceed 49.\n\nTotal certs = (H - h) + C < 50.\n\nSuppose we want to see if it's possible for H to be, say, 60 (so non-HSD is 40). Then H_CERT = 59 (assuming h=1). Then (59) + C <50 \u2192 C < -9. Impossible. So H can't be 60.\n\nIf H is 50, then H_CERT is 49 (h=1). Then C must be <1. So C=0. So total certs 49. That works. In this case, the non-HSD proportion is 50%, so \"large proportion\" might not be the case. The question is whether \"a large proportion\" means more than half or just a significant portion.\n\nBut the problem says \"most of the factory workers have not earned the certificate\", so certs are less than 50%. So if H is 50, and certs are 49, then non-HSD have 0 certs. So non-HSD workers are 50%, so \"a large proportion\" (i.e., more than half) would not hold here. So in this case, A would not be true. But does the premises allow H=50?\n\nWait in this scenario, H=50, so the non-HSD workers are 50. The HSD workers have 49 certs. So the total certs are 49. So the majority (51 workers) don't have certs, which is okay. Also, the HSD workers who didn't get the cert are 1, which fits the condition.\n\nIn this case, the proportion of workers without HSD is exactly half. So is \"a large proportion\" of the workers not HSD? If \"large\" is more than half, then it's not true, but maybe \"large\" is just significant, like more than a third. The question is from the options given, which uses \"large proportion\". Since in this case, it's exactly 50%, which might not be considered \"large\". However, maybe the problem requires it to be true in all cases, so even if there exists a scenario where A is not true, then A is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but in this scenario where H=50, the premises are satisfied, but A would not hold (since non-HSD are 50%, not a large proportion). Therefore, A doesn't have to be true. Hmm, so my previous conclusion was wrong. So now I'm confused.\n\nWait, but let me check again. Let me see what happens if H is 40.\n\nThen H_CERT = 39 (h=1). So C can be up to 10 (since 39+10=49). Therefore, certs are 49. The non-HSD workers are 60 (which is 60% of total). So in that case, non-HSD are a large proportion (60% is a large proportion). So in such a case, A is true. But when H is 50, A is not true. Since the problem says \"if the statements are true, which must be true\", then A doesn't have to be true because there's a possible scenario (H=50) where A is false but premises are still true.\n\nTherefore, A might not be the correct answer.\n\nHmm, so maybe I made a mistake earlier. Let me re-examine the options.\n\nOption C: \"Most of the workers who have earned the certificate are high school graduates.\" Let's see in the H=50 case: certs are 49 (all from HSD). So yes, most (all) are HSD. So in that case, C holds. In the H=20 case where C=30 (non-HSD certs), then certs are 19 (HSD) +30 (non)=49. So 19/49\u224838.8% HSD, so most are non-HSD. So C would be false. But can that scenario be possible without violating the premises?\n\nWait, in that scenario, H=20, so non-HSD is 80. The HSD with certs are 19, which is nearly all (since 19/20 is 95%). The non-HSD certs are 30. The problem states that \"the high school graduates who haven't earned the certificate are ones who have evening obligations.\" So that holds (the 1 HSD without the cert has obligations). The total certs are 49 <50 (so most don't have it). So the premises are satisfied. But in that case, C is false. Therefore, C doesn't have to be true.\n\nHmm, so maybe none of the options except A or C are possible? Wait, let's think again.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the H=50 case. Let me see:\n\nIf H=50, then H_CERT = 49 (assuming h=1). The non-HSD certs would have to be 0 (since 49 + C <50 \u2192 C <1). So C=0. So certs are all from HSD. So the certs are 49. Then the proportion of certs that are HSD is 100%, so C holds. So in this case, C is true. However, in another case like H=30, C=20, then certs would be 29+20=49. So 29/49\u224859% HSD, so C holds (since most are HSD). But when H=20 and C=30, then certs are 19+30=49, so non-HSD certs are 30, which is more than half. So in that scenario, C is false. But is that scenario allowed?\n\nWait, the problem says that the eight week training course is what HSD graduates have to take. But does it say anything about non-HSD graduates? The problem states \"the high school graduates who haven't earned the certificate are ones who have evening obligations\" but says nothing about non-graduates. So non-graduates could have taken the course or not. The problem doesn't restrict them from taking it. Therefore, in the scenario where H=20 and C=30 (non-HSD certs), that is possible.\n\nTherefore, since there exists a scenario where C is false but the premises are true, then C isn't necessarily true. So neither A nor C must be true?\n\nWait, but in the H=50 case, A is not true but C is true. In the H=20 case, A is true (non-HSD are 80%) and C is false. Hmm. So the options are A and C could be true or false depending on scenario. So the question is which must be true in all scenarios?\n\nWait, let me think again. The problem says \"most of the factory workers have not earned the certificate\". So certs <50% of total.\n\nIf H is high, say H=80, then certs would be nearly all HSD, which would be 79 certs. That's way over 50% of 100, so that violates the premise. So H must be low enough so that even if all HSD except a few get certs, plus some non-HSD, the total certs are still under 50%.\n\nSo the maximum possible certs is when non-HSD certs are as high as possible. Let me see the maximum H can be.\n\nLet me do some algebra.\n\nLet total workers T=100.\n\nTotal certs C_total = (H - h) + C_non-HSD <50.\n\nTo maximize H, set h as small as possible (h=1) and C_non-HSD as large as possible (C_non-HSD = (T - H), but that would mean all non-HSD have certs. Wait, but even then:\n\nC_total = (H -1) + (100 - H) = 99 <50? No, 99 is way over. So that can't happen.\n\nWait, so perhaps to maximize H, we need to set C_non-HSD to as much as possible without exceeding 49.\n\nSo:\n\n(H -1) + C_non-HSD <50 \u2192 C_non-HSD <50 - (H -1) = 51 - H.\n\nBut also, C_non-HSD can't exceed the number of non-HSD workers, which is 100 - H.\n\nTherefore, 51 - H must be greater than the non-HSD certs. Wait, but to maximize H, we can set C_non-HSD as large as possible under the constraint.\n\nThe maximum possible C_non-HSD is min( (51 - H), (100 - H) )\n\nWait, since 51 - H must be positive (otherwise C_non-HSD can't be negative). So 51 - H >0 \u2192 H <51.\n\nTherefore, the maximum H can be is 50 (since H must be an integer).\n\nThus, H can be up to 50, in which case non-HSD are 50.\n\nIf H=50, then C_non-HSD < 51 -50=1, so C_non-HSD must be 0. So certs are 49 (all HSD). Thus, in this case, non-HSD proportion is 50% (so A is not \"large\" proportion), and C is true (most certs are HSD).\n\nIf H is 49, then C_non-HSD can be up to 51 -49 =2. So non-HSD certs can be 0,1, or 2. So non-HSD proportion is 51%, which is a large proportion (over 50%). So A would be true here.\n\nWait, so when H is less than 50, then non-HSD are more than 50%, making A true. When H is exactly 50, non-HSD are 50%, which may not be considered a \"large\" proportion. So whether A holds depends on whether H is less than 50 or exactly 50.\n\nThe problem states \"nearly all\" HSD have certs. If H=50, then H_CERT is 49 (h=1). That's \"nearly all\" (98% is nearly all). So that's acceptable.\n\nIn this case, the non-HSD are 50% of the workers. The question is whether \"a large proportion\" includes exactly half or not. The term \"large proportion\" is a bit ambiguous, but in LSAT or logical reasoning terms, it might mean more than half. So if it's exactly half, then A isn't true. However, if \"large proportion\" just means a significant portion, then 50% might qualify, but I think in such contexts, \"large proportion\" is often more than half.\n\nSo in the scenario where H=50, A would not hold, but that scenario is allowed by the premises. Therefore, A doesn't have to be true.\n\nHmm, so perhaps the correct answer is C? But earlier example where H=20 and C=30 (non-HSD certs) makes C false. But in that case, is that scenario possible?\n\nWait in that case, H=20, non-HSD certs are 30. But the non-HSD workers are 80, so 30 out of 80 have certs. The problem doesn't restrict non-HSD from taking the course, so yes, that's allowed.\n\nTherefore, since there exists a scenario where C is false while premises are true, C isn't necessarily true.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Let me check options again.\n\nOption B: \"A large proportion of students with high school diplomas have evening obligations...\" But the problem says that the HSD without the cert are those with evening obligations. Since \"nearly all\" HSD have the cert, the number without is small. So the proportion of HSD with evening obligations is small. So B is false.\n\nOption D: \"No factory worker without a high school diploma has completed the course and earned the certificate.\" The problem doesn't say that non-HSD can't take the course. It only mentions that HSD who didn't get the cert have evening obligations. There's no info restricting non-HSD from taking the course. So maybe some non-HSD workers did take it and got certs. So D is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but the question is which must be true. Since in some scenarios non-HSD have certs (like the example where H=20 and C=30), D is false there. So D isn't necessarily true.\n\nSo now I'm stuck. The options are A, B, C, D. B and D can be false, so not them. A and C can be true or false depending on scenarios. But the question says \"must be true\".\n\nWait, maybe I made an error in assuming that the total certs can be as high as 49 when H is low. Wait, let's think of another angle. Let's try to see if C must be true.\n\nThe question is, can the majority of certs be non-HSD? To do that, the number of non-HSD certs must be more than the HSD certs.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nHSD_CERT = H - h \u2248 H (since nearly all HSD have it)\n\nNon-HSD_CERT = C.\n\nTo have Non-HSD_CERT > HSD_CERT \u2192 C > H - h.\n\nBut total certs: (H - h) + C < T/2 (since most don't have certs).\n\nLet me suppose T=100 again.\n\nIf C > H - h \u2192 C > H (approximately).\n\nSo then, (H - h) + C \u2248 H + C <50.\n\nBut since C > H, then H + C > 2H \u2192 so 2H <50 \u2192 H <25.\n\nSo if H is less than 25, then it's possible. For example, let H=20, h=1, then HSD_CERT=19. Let C=20 (non-HSD certs). Then total certs=39 <50. So in this case, C=20 >19 (HSD_CERT), so the majority of certs are non-HSD. Thus C would be false here.\n\nBut can H be 20? Yes, as above.\n\nThus, there is a scenario where C is false. So C isn't necessarily true.\n\nWhat about A? When H is 25, then non-HSD is 75% (if H=25), so A holds. When H is 50, non-HSD is 50%, which may not count as \"large\". But in most cases where H is less than 50, A holds. However, since the maximum H can be is 50, and in that case A doesn't hold, then A isn't necessarily true.\n\nHmm. So none of the options A or C must be true in all scenarios. But the question says \"must be true\". Maybe I missed something.\n\nWait let me re-express the premises:\n\n1. Nearly all HSD employees have the certificate.\n\n2. HSD employees without the certificate have evening obligations.\n\n3. Most factory workers (overall) don't have the certificate.\n\nTherefore, the majority of workers are either non-HSD or HSD with evening obligations.\n\nWait, the non-HSD workers plus the HSD without certs must