tainer does. The question is which is \"most strongly supported.\" Since B is a direct inference from the reasoning given, whereas A is also correct but more of a restatement. Let me see the options again.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of it in terms of logical structure:\n\nPremise: Egg cartons made of recycled Styrofoam require less cleaning because eggshells prevent food contact.\n\nConclusion options:\n\nA says Not every container type is designed to prevent contact. This must be true because egg cartons do, but others don't. So A is true.\n\nB says the main reason for cleaning is to remove food contact residues. The premise supports this because the difference in cleaning is due to whether the new container has food contact. Thus, the cleaning's necessity is because of the food contact. So B is also true.\n\nHmm, so both A and B are true? But the options are single choice. Perhaps I made an error.\n\nWait, looking back at the problem's exact wording:\n\nThe question says: \"Based on the above information, which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?\"\n\nIf both A and B are supported, but the question requires choosing between them, I might need to re-examine.\n\nWait, perhaps the difference is in the scope.\n\nOption A is about the containers made from recycled Styrofoam. The premise says that egg cartons (a type) are among the easiest because their design prevents contact. So other containers made from recycled Styrofoam (like a bowl) would not prevent contact. Thus, \"not every\" is correct. So A is definitely true.\n\nOption B says the main reason for cleaning the styrofoam when recycled is to remove residual food that came into contact. The context's reasoning is that when the new container's food doesn't touch the Styrofoam (egg cartons), less cleaning is needed. This implies that when making containers where food does touch, more cleaning is needed to remove residues from previous uses where the Styrofoam had contact with food. So the cleaning is to remove residues from prior food contact. Hence, B is also correct.\n\nWait, but the problem's context says \"used Styrofoam need not be as thoroughly cleaned when made into egg cartons as when made into other food containers.\" So the \"used\" Styrofoam's cleaning level depends on the new container's design. The reason given is that in egg cartons, the food (egg) doesn't touch the Styrofoam. Therefore, the cleaning required for the recycled Styrofoam is because of the new container's design's contact with food. Wait, that's a different angle.\n\nWait, perhaps I'm misunderstanding the timing. The Styrofoam is used (previously used for something else), then recycled. When making new containers, if the new container is an egg carton, you don't need to clean the recycled Styrofoam as much because the egg won't touch it. But if the new container is a soup container, you need to clean more because the soup will touch the Styrofoam. So the cleaning needed is because of the new container's design's contact with food, not the previous use's contact. Wait, that might not make sense.\n\nWait, maybe the cleaning of the used Styrofoam (when recycling it) is to remove residues from its previous use. For example, if the original Styrofoam was used as a bowl that had soup (food contact), then recycling it would require cleaning to remove the soup residue. But if it was used as an egg carton before, maybe it didn't have food contact, so less cleaning was needed then. But the problem's context is about when making new containers: when making a new egg carton from recycled Styrofoam (regardless of its previous use), you don't need to clean it as much because the new container's design prevents contact. So the cleaning is related to the new container's design, not the previous use's contact.\n\nWait, this is a key point. The context says that the reason egg cartons require less cleaning is because their design prevents food contact in the new container, not because the previous use didn't have contact. So the cleaning required for the recycled material is due to the new container's design's need for the food not to touch it. Therefore, the cleaning is to ensure that the new container's food doesn't touch contaminated Styrofoam. So if the new container's design allows food to touch the Styrofoam, then the recycled Styrofoam must be cleaned to remove any previous residues (from its prior use's contact with food). Thus, the cleaning is to remove residues from prior uses where the Styrofoam had contact with food, so that when the new container has food contact, it's clean.\n\nTherefore, the necessity for thorough cleaning when making other containers (non-egg) is precisely because the new container will have food contact, thus requiring the recycled Styrofoam to be cleaned of any prior residues. Hence, the main reason for cleaning is to remove residues from when the Styrofoam was in contact with food in previous uses. So B is correct.\n\nTherefore, B is correct because the cleaning is needed to remove residues from prior food contact, which would otherwise come into contact in the new container. Thus, B is supported.\n\nA is also correct, but perhaps B is the stronger conclusion because it's directly tied to the reasoning given in the context. The context's main point is explaining why egg cartons require less cleaning, which hinges on the necessity of cleaning to prevent food contact in the new container. Hence, the necessity of cleaning for other containers stems from the need to remove residues that would come into contact with food in the new container, which supports B.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is B.\n\nWait, but now I'm confused again. Let me try to see which one is more directly supported.\n\nThe context says that egg cartons don't need as much cleaning because the eggshell prevents contact. This implies that for containers where food does touch the Styrofoam, the Styrofoam must be cleaned more. The reason for needing to clean more in those cases must be to remove any residues from previous uses where the Styrofoam had food contact. So the cleaning is done to remove residues from when the Styrofoam was in contact with food. So the main reason for cleaning during recycling is to remove such residues. Thus, B is correct.\n\nA is also correct, but it's a more general statement that doesn't go into the reason for cleaning, just the fact that not all containers prevent contact. Since the question is about the strongest support, and B directly addresses the reasoning given (the cleaning's purpose), B is better.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is A. Let me think of an example.\n\nSuppose someone says: \"Egg cartons are easy because the eggs don't touch the Styrofoam. Therefore, not every food container stops the food from touching it.\" That's exactly what A says. So A is a direct conclusion.\n\nB requires an extra step: the cleaning is because of prior food contact. The context says that the reason for less cleaning in egg cartons is because the new container doesn't have food contact. So the cleaning needed for other containers is because the new container will have food contact, which requires the recycled material to be clean. So the cleaning is to ensure that when the new container has food contact, the material is clean. So the cleaning is to prevent contamination in the new use, not necessarily because of prior contamination. Wait, that's a different angle.\n\nWait, maybe the cleaning is to make sure that the new container's material is clean so that when food touches it, there's no contamination from the material itself. So the reason to clean when making a container that has food contact is to make the material clean enough for food contact. The prior use's food contact might have left residues, so you need to clean it to remove those residues before using it in a new container where food will touch it.\n\nTherefore, the necessity to clean the recycled 