      "text": " B.\n\nWait, let me check again.B says the roots of rice grow in the direction of more fertilizer.I think normally roots grow towards water and nutrients. So that's a positive tropism (like chemotropism). It's a directional growth movement caused by external stimulus (fertilizer concentration). So yes, that fits. The other options: C is movement but not growth. D is about preferring shade but not directional movement. A is animal. So B is correct.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **B) The roots of rice always grow in the direction of more fertilizer in the soil**.\n\n### Reasoning:\n1. **Definition Alignment**: Directional movement, as defined, refers to **plant growth movements** caused by **external stimuli in a single direction**, and it is irreversible.  \n2. **Analysis of Options**:  \n   - **A)** Involves an animal (puppy) and a behavioral response (drooling), not plant growth. **Eliminated**.  \n   - **B)** Rice roots growing toward fertilizer-rich soil is a growth movement (chemotropism) driven by an external directional stimulus (nutrient concentration). This fits the definition of directional/growth movement. **Valid**.  \n   - **C)** Petals opening/closing with sunlight is a **rapid, reversible movement** (nyctinasty), not irreversible growth. **Eliminated**.  \n   - **D)** Preference for shade describes environmental adaptation, not a directional growth response to a unidirectional stimulus. **Eliminated**.  \n\nThus, **B** is the only option that matches the criteria of directional growth movement in plants caused by an external directional stimulus. \n\n**Answer: B**",
