t the options are separate, and the problem is from a test, perhaps the answer is not D because when only one is true, the only possibility that doesn\'t involve B is D. But I\'m not sure.\n\nWait, but let\'s think of it this way: the problem says that only one of the two assertions is true. Since both assertions include "pulled into smaller orbits" (A), if A were true, both assertions would be true, which violates the condition. Therefore, A must be false. So A is false.\n\nNow, the two assertions reduce to:\n\nS1: B.\n\nS2: ¬B.\n\nThus, exactly one of B or ¬B must be true, which is always the case. So if S1 is true (B=T), then S2 is false (¬B=F), which is valid. So the scenario is B=T, A=F → option B.\n\nIf S2 is true (¬B=T → B=F), then S1 is false (B=F), so the scenario is A=F, B=F → option D.\n\nTherefore, the possible answers are not B or D. Since the question asks which can be inferred, meaning which must be true, but neither must be necessarily true (either could be the case), the problem must have a unique answer.\n\nWait, but maybe the problem\'s second conclusion is different. Let me read the original problem\'s context again:\n\n"the two researchers made two different conclusions: first, Jupiter\'s gravity either pulled them into smaller orbits or expelled them from the solar system; second, Jupiter\'s gravity either pulled them into smaller orbits or drove them out of the solar system."\n\nAh! Wait a second. The first says "expelled them from the solar system", and the second says "drove them out of the solar system." But maybe "drove them out" is different from "expelled them from". Perhaps "expelled" means pulled out, while "drove them out" could mean something else, but in English, they are synonyms. Alternatively, maybe "drove them out" of the Oort cloud into the solar system, but the first is expelled from the solar system out into space. That would make them opposites.\n\nIf that\'s the case, then:\n\nLet me redefine:\n\nLet B = expelled from the solar system (i.e., out of the solar system).\n\nC = drove them out of the solar system (same as B).\n\nWait, no. Alternatively, maybe "drove them out" means into the solar system, so:\n\nLet B = expelled from the solar system (out).\n\nLet C = drove them into the solar system (in).\n\nThen the two conclusions are:\n\nResearcher1: A or B (pull into orbits or out).\n\nResearcher2: A or C (pull into orbits or in).\n\nNow, the two conclusions are different.\n\nNow, if only one of these is true:\n\nCase1: Researcher1 is true, Researcher2 is false.\n\nResearcher1: A∨B → true.\n\nResearcher2: A∨C → false → A=F and C=F.\n\nThus, A=F, C=F. But Researcher1 is true → B must be T.\n\nSo the scenario is A=F, B=T, C=F.\n\nThus, Jupiter didn\'t pull into orbits, expelled them (B=T), and didn\'t drive them in (C=F). But the options don\'t mention C, so this scenario would match option B ("did not pull into orbits but drove them out"), assuming that "drove them out" corresponds to B (expelled).\n\nCase2: Researcher2 is true, Researcher1 is false.\n\nResearcher2: A∨C → true.\n\nResearcher1: A∨B → false → A=F and B=F.\n\nThus, A=F, B=F, and C must be T (since Researcher2 is true).\n\nSo Jupiter didn\'t pull into orbits, didn\'t expel them (B=F), but drove them into the solar system (C=T). This scenario isn\'t covered in the options because the options only mention "drove them out of" (which would be B). Option B says "drove them out", which would be B=T, but in this case B=F and C=T (drove them in). So this isn\'t an option. Thus, this approach may not work.\n\nHmm, this is getting too convoluted. Perhaps the correct answer is not D, as in the case where neither happened, making only the second statement true (if the second is A∨¬B).\n\nGiven that in the initial analysis where S1=A∨B and S2=A∨¬B, the only valid answers are not B or D, and the options include both, but since the problem is likely structured to have a unique answer, and given that when A must be false, and considering the options, D is the only one that doesn\'t involve B being true, but actually requires B to be false. However, since both are possible, but in the options, D is an option, and B is also, but the problem may require that the answer is D because it\'s the only one that allows the second conclusion to be true when the first is false.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 30%|###       | 181/600 [1:54:34<1:36:41, 13.85s/it]
