      "text": " D. Wait, hold on, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait the original text says \"the problem of children concealing their own situation from their parents is not only worse solved, but also not given enough attention.\" So \"not given enough attention\" would mean that parents aren't paying enough attention to it. So D says \"has not attracted the attention of parents,\" which is a bit different because \"not given enough\" implies some attention is there but not enough, whereas D says it hasn't attracted attention. But maybe the original Chinese wording might use a term that would translate to \"has not attracted attention,\" making D correct. Alternatively, the options may have nuances.\n\nLooking at the options again:\n\nD) Children's behavior of concealing their own situation has not attracted the attention of parents.\n\nThe original says \"not given enough attention,\" so it's not that it's not attracted any attention, but not enough. However, the options may require us to choose the closest one. Alternatively, perhaps the translation here is precise. Let me check the exact wording of the original context again:\n\n\"...while the problem of children concealing their own situation from their parents is not only worse solved, but also not given enough attention.\"\n\nSo the problem is not only worse solved (i.e., less effectively addressed) but also not given enough attention. So \"not given enough attention\" means that parents are not paying sufficient attention to it. So the statement in D says that the behavior \"has not attracted the attention of parents,\" which might be an overstatement. However, the options are A, B, C, D. Let me see the other options again.\n\nOption D says \"has not attracted the attention of parents.\" The text says \"not given enough attention.\" So maybe the answer is D because the problem is not getting enough attention, so it hasn't attracted sufficient attention. But the wording in the option is absolute, but maybe in the context of the options, that's the closest.\n\nLooking at the options again:\n\nA) Parents think that children's failure to report information is more worrying than lying --> Text says lying is what parents are most worried about, so A is wrong.\n\nB) Parents believe that lying is the most unforgivable moral problem --> The text says it's what they're most worried about, not necessarily the most unforgivable moral issue. So B might not be correct.\n\nC) Most parents are more than willing to solve the problem of children lying --> The text says \"many have not solved it well, but at least most should pay attention to it.\" Paying attention doesn't mean they are willing to solve it. \"More than willing\" is too strong. So C is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, D is the correct answer even though it's phrased as \"has not attracted the attention,\" because the text says it's not given enough attention, which implies it hasn't attracted sufficient attention. The option D says it hasn't attracted attention, which is accurate in that it's not getting enough, so the attention is insufficient. Maybe the answer is D.\n\nWait but the wording: the problem of concealing is \"not given enough attention,\" so parents are not paying enough attention to it. So the statement D is \"has not attracted the attention of parents\" \u2014 that would mean that parents haven't noticed it at all. But the text's wording is that they are not giving enough attention, which could mean they are paying some but not enough. So maybe the correct option is D because the text says it's not given enough attention, so it's not attracting sufficient attention, but D's wording is absolute. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe the answer is D because the problem is not given enough attention, so the correct answer is D.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I'm overcomplicating. Let me check again. The question asks which is true of the original text.\n\nThe options are:\n\nD) Children's behavior of concealing their own situation has not attracted the attention of parents.\n\nThe original text says that the problem of children concealing is \"not given enough attention.\" So the behavior hasn't attracted enough attention, which aligns with D's statement that it hasn't attracted attention. The wording in D is \"has not attracted\" which is a bit strong, but maybe the intended answer is D because the text says it's not given enough attention, meaning that parents aren't paying attention to it as much as they should. So D is correct.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nD) Children's behavior of concealing their own situation has not attracted the attention of parents.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**  \n1. **Analyze the Context:** The text states that while parents are most worried about lying, the issue of children concealing their situations is \"not only worse solved, but also not given enough attention.\"  \n2. **Evaluate Options:**  \n   - **A** claims parents find concealment more worrying than lying, but the text explicitly states lying is their \"most worried\" issue. **Incorrect.**  \n   - **B** asserts lying is the \"most unforgivable moral problem,\" but the text only mentions it as a major concern, not a moral judgment. **Incorrect.**  \n   - **C** suggests most parents are \"more than willing\" to address lying, but the text says they \"have not solved it well\" despite paying attention. This overstates their willingness. **Incorrect.**  \n   - **D** states concealment \"has not attracted the attention of parents.\" The text clarifies it is \"not given enough attention,\" implying insufficient focus, which aligns with D's assertion. **Correct.**  \n\n**Answer:** D",
