ce bureau (government) set max prices. So that's national pricing. So C is subject to it, so not the answer.\n\nD: The bus charges are set by the government. So D is also under national pricing. So only A is not. So the answer is A.\n\nWait, but in the options, the discount is a temporary promotion. So the government didn't set that price. The original price might have been under national pricing? Or is the question about whether the pricing is set by the government?\n\nWait, the definition says \"commodity prices and charging standards set by the government\". So if an item is subject to national pricing, then its price is set by the government. So for items not subject to national pricing, the business can set their own prices.\n\nSo in A, the cashmere sweaters are high-end and imported. Maybe the government doesn't regulate those. So the mall can set their own prices. So this is not subject to national pricing. So A is the correct answer.\n\nYes, so the answer should be A.\n\nBut let me check the other options again.\n\nOption D: The bus fares are clearly set (like 1 yuan per ride), so that's government-set. So D is subject.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is option A.\n\nWait but in the options, the problem states that national pricing is set by government departments. So any pricing that's not set by them is not subject. So A is correct. I think I'm confident now.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **A**. \n\n**Explanation:**  \nNational pricing involves government-set prices or standards. Here's the breakdown:  \n- **Option A:** The shopping mall\u2019s 30% discount is a voluntary business decision, not mandated by the government. Thus, it is **not subject to national pricing**.  \n- **Option B:** The education bureau (a government body) sets tuition fees, making this subject to national pricing.  \n- **Option C:** The municipal price bureau (government) approves maximum prices, so this is under national pricing.  \n- **Option D:** Bus fares are clearly regulated by the government, so they fall under national pricing.  \n\nSince **A** involves a private business pricing decision, it is the correct answer.  \n\n**Answer:** A",
