      "text": " D) The same painkiller works better for women than for men.\n\nYes, that seems correct. Because the painkiller's effectiveness (both in terms of the degree of pain relief and duration) is better in women, so D is correct. B is a specific aspect of the duration part, but D is the broader conclusion.\n</think>\n\nD) The same painkiller works better for women than for men\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Context Analysis:**  \n   The passage states that men who underwent the same surgery and took the same painkillers experienced **less pain relief** and the painkillers were **less effective for less time** compared to women. This implies two key points:  \n   - Painkillers provided weaker relief in men.  \n   - The duration of effectiveness was shorter for men.  \n\n2. **Option Evaluation:**  \n   - **A)** Incorrect. The text does not claim painkillers are **ineffective** on men entirely; they just work **less effectively**.  \n   - **B)** Plausible but incomplete. While shorter effectiveness duration could mean pain returns sooner for men, this focuses on timing alone, not the overall comparative effectiveness.  \n   - **C)** Irrelevant. The text discusses painkiller efficacy, not pain tolerance (i.e., how much pain someone can endure).  \n   - **D)** Correct. Directly summarizes the findings: the same painkiller provides better pain relief (both in magnitude and duration) for women than for men.  \n\n3. **Conclusion:**  \n   The most accurate and comprehensive interpretation of the text is **D**, as it encapsulates both the reduced pain relief and shorter duration of effectiveness in men compared to women.",
