audi, Petroleum, and China were all visited (assuming the Oil pavilion is not part of the options here). Since the first affirmation requires S and O, but O is not mentioned, perhaps the problem considers the Oil pavilion as part of the Petroleum pavilion in the options. So the \"petroleum pavilion\" in option B includes the Oil pavilion (O) and Petroleum Museum (P). So if the Petroleum pavilion refers to both, then:\n\nOption B: S (Saudi), Petroleum pavilion (O and P), and C (China). Thus, all four are visited, which matches the requirements. But the options don't have such a phrasing.\n\nAlternatively, the problem might have a mistake in the options, but given the choices, the only one that satisfies the third's affirmation (C and P) and first's (S and O) is not listed, but the closest is B, which includes S, P, C. So maybe O is not required in the options' answer, so the correct is B.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the second suggestion's negation requires that he does not visit both P and C, but that contradicts the third's affirmation. Thus, the only way this works is if the second suggestion's negation does not contradict the third's.\n\nWait, maybe the second suggestion's negation allows both P and C. So the second suggestion was \"You must not visit both P and C,\" so the negation is you can visit both, which is the third's affirmation. Thus, the negation of the second is the same as the third's affirmation. Thus, all is okay.\n\nThus, the correct answer is that he visited S, O, C, P. Since the options don't have that, but option B includes S, P, C (assuming O is allowed), but the options don't have that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first suggestion's negation is S \u2227 O, so he did visit both. The third's negation is C \u2227 P. So the combination is S, O, C, P.\n\nNone of the options include all four, so perhaps the options are mistyped. Alternatively, perhaps the \"petroleum pavilion\" in option B refers to both Oil and Petroleum Museum. Then:\n\nB) Saudi, Petroleum (both Oil and P), and China.\n\nThus, all four are visited. So the answer is B.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we need to determine which option is correct given that Wang Gang rejected all three suggestions. Each suggestion must therefore be false, so we negate them and derive the necessary conditions:\n\n1. **First Suggestion**: \"If you visit Saudi Arabia Pavilion (S), you won\u2019t visit the Oil Pavilion (O).\"  \n   - **Negation**: \\( S \\land O \\).  \n   - Wang must visit **both Saudi Arabia and Oil Pavilion**.\n\n2. **Second Suggestion**: \"The Petroleum Museum (P) and China National Pavilion (C) are optional.\"  \n   - Assuming \"optional\" means \"you can choose to visit either or both,\" the negation implies he cannot visit both. However, since this conflicts with the third suggestion\u2019s negation, we reinterpret it as \"you must not visit both P and C.\"  \n   - **Negation**: \\( P \\land C \\).  \n   - Wang must visit **both Petroleum Museum and China National Pavilion**.\n\n3. **Third Suggestion**: \"Not both China National Pavilion (C) and Petroleum Museum (P).\"  \n   - **Negation**: \\( C \\land P \\).  \n   - Wang must visit **both C and P**, aligning with the second suggestion\u2019s negation.\n\n**Combined Requirements**:  \n- Wang must visit **Saudi Arabia (S)**, **Oil Pavilion (O)**, **China National Pavilion (C)**, and **Petroleum Museum (P)**.  \n\n**Analysis of Options**:  \n- **A**: Visits S and P but not C \u2192 **Invalid** (must visit both C and P).  \n- **B**: Visits S, P, and C \u2192 **Valid** (though O is not explicitly listed, the Oil Pavilion is logically required from the first suggestion).  \n- **C**: Does not visit S, P, or C \u2192 **Invalid** (must visit all four).  \n- **D**: Does not visit S, O, or C \u2192 **Invalid** (must visit S and O).  \n\n### Answer: **B**  \n**Explanation**: While the Oil Pavilion (O) is not explicitly mentioned in Option B, accepting \"Petroleum Pavilion\" as encompassing both Oil and Petroleum Museum aligns with the derived requirements. Thus, **B** is the best fit.  \n\n\\boxed{B}",
