messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nInvisible curriculum refers to the informal and unconscious learning experience that is not clearly specified in the school's planned curriculum.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following is not an invisible curriculum?\n# Options:\nA) Colleges and universities organize students to participate in two-day military training to cultivate and exercise students' willpower.\nB) When children learn teaching courses, they cultivate their interest in learning and teaching.\nC) A university set up a university culture festival to let students know the school motto and style of study.\nD) Teachers and students participate in extracurricular activities together to establish an equal and harmonious relationship between teachers and students.", '<think>\nOkay, let\'s tackle this question step by step.The user wants to know which option is NOT an example of the invisible curriculum based on the given definition..\n\nFirst, I need to recall what the invisible curriculum is.The context says it\'s the informal and unconscious learning that\'s not part of the school\'s planned curriculum.So, it\'s the stuff students pick up unintentionally or through the environment, not the explicit lessons or planned activities.\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nOption A: Military training organized by colleges to cultivate willpower.Hmm, military training is a structured, planned activity.The school is intentionally organizing this to build willpower.That sounds like it\'s part of the formal curriculum, so maybe this is the answer?But wait, sometimes even planned activities can have invisible aspects.Need to think more.\n\nOption B: Kids learn teaching courses and develop interest in learning/teaching.Wait, the wording here is a bit confusing.If they\'re taking teaching courses, maybe that\'s part of their formal education.But the interest in learning and teaching might be an unintended byproduct.Or is the course itself part of the curriculum?The question is whether the interest is part of the planned curriculum or an invisible outcome.Not sure yet.\n\nOption C: University culture festival to let students know the school motto and style.This seems like a planned event.The school is intentionally promoting their motto and values.But the invisible curriculum might include the underlying values or ethos students absorb without being explicitly taught.If the festival is a formal event meant to convey these things, maybe it\'s part of the planned curriculum.But the motto and style might be part of the school\'s culture that\'s absorbed informally.Hmm, tricky.\n\nOption D: Teachers and students do extracurricular activities to establish equal relationships.Extracurricular activities can be planned, but the relationships formed might be an unintended outcome.If the activity is meant to build relationships, then it\'s intentional.Wait, the question says "to establish an equal and harmonious relationship"—so the school\'s intention is explicit here.But maybe the actual relationships formed are part of the invisible curriculum because they emerge from interactions rather than being directly taught?.\n\nWait, the key is whether it\'s part of the planned curriculum.The invisible curriculum is the unplanned, informal learning.So, if an activity is organized with a specific goal (like military training for willpower), that\'s part of the formal curriculum.The invisible curriculum would be the unintended lessons, like social norms, values, or attitudes picked up through environment or interactions.\n\nLooking again:.\n\nA) Military training is a planned activity with the explicit purpose of cultivating willpower.So that\'s part of the formal curriculum, not invisible.So this would be the correct answer because the question asks which is NOT an invisible curriculum.\n\nB) If the students are in teaching courses (formal), but their interest in teaching is an outcome not necessarily part of the course\'s stated goals.Wait, maybe the interest is an unintended byproduct, making it part of the invisible curriculum.Alternatively, if the course is designed to cultivate interest, then it\'s planned.The question says "when children learn teaching courses, they cultivate their interest"—maybe it\'s a side effect, hence invisible.\n\nC) The culture festival is planned to make students know the motto and style.The explicit goal is to teach the school\'s values, so that\'s part of the formal curriculum.But the actual absorption of the school\'s culture might be invisible.Wait, but the question states the activity\'s purpose is to let them know the motto.So maybe the activity itself is part of the planned curriculum, but the learning of the motto is the intended outcome.Therefore, the motto isn\'t part of the invisible curriculum.But the question is about which is NOT an example.Hmm, conflicting thoughts here.\n\nD) Extracurricular activities meant to establish relationships.If the school plans these to create equality, then that\'s a formal goal.But the relationships formed might be part of the social environment, so maybe that\'s invisible.Wait, the question says "to establish an equal.." so the intention is explicit.Therefore, this might be part of the formal curriculum\'s goals, making it not invisible.But I\'m confused now.\n\nWait, let\'s clarify the definition again: Invisible curriculum is informal and unconscious, not specified in the planned curriculum.So any activity that\'s part of the planned curriculum (even if it has side effects) would not be part of the invisible curriculum.The invisible curriculum is the unintended stuff.\n\nSo for each option:.\n\nA) Military training is a planned activity with a clear goal (willpower).The willpower cultivation is the intended outcome, so that\'s part of the formal curriculum.Therefore, this is NOT an invisible curriculum.So A would be the answer.\n\nB) Learning teaching courses (formal) but developing interest in teaching might be an unintended consequence.Or maybe the course is meant to teach content, and the interest is a byproduct.So that\'s invisible.\n\nC) The culture festival is planned to teach the motto and style.So the explicit goal is to make students know these things.Therefore, that\'s part of the formal curriculum.Wait, but the school\'s motto and style might be part of the hidden curriculum.Wait, maybe the question is considering that the culture festival is a planned event, so the information about the motto is part of the formal curriculum.So C would also not be invisible?But the options are only one correct answer.\n\nHmm, now I\'m confused between A and C. Let\'s think again.\n\nThe key is whether the learning experience is specified in the planned curriculum.The invisible curriculum is the unspoken or unintended lessons.For example, the values conveyed through school rules, social interactions, or unspoken norms..\n\nMilitary training (A) is a planned activity with an explicit goal (willpower).So that\'s part of the formal curriculum.So A is not an example of invisible curriculum, making it the correct answer.\n\nOption C: The festival is planned to let students know the motto and style.The motto and style are part of the school\'s culture, but if the festival is an organized event to explicitly teach them, then it\'s part of the formal curriculum.Therefore, C is not invisible.Wait, but maybe the motto and style are part of the school\'s ethos that students absorb through daily interactions, not through a festival.If the festival is a formal way to teach them, then it\'s not invisible..\n\nBut the question asks which is NOT an invisible curriculum.So if both A and C are not invisible, but the options only have one correct answer, perhaps I made a mistake.\n\nLooking back at the options again:.\n\nThe question states "which is not an invisible curriculum"—so the correct answer is the one that IS part of the planned curriculum.\n\nA is a planned activity (military training) with the explicit goal of cultivating willpower.So that\'s part of the formal curriculum, hence not invisible.So A is the answer.\n\nC\'s activity (festival) is also planned to let students know the school motto.So that\'s part of the formal curriculum as well.Wait, but the school\'s motto and style might be considered part of the hidden curriculum if they are learned through the environment, but if it\'s taught through an organized event, then it\'s formal.So C could also be not invisible.But since the options are single choice, perhaps the answer is A.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the difference is that in C, the students are being made aware of the motto through the festival, which is a planned activity, so it\'s formal.Whereas in A, the military training\'s purpose is to build willpower, which is a personal quality, perhaps part of the intended curriculum.So both A and C are formal.But the options can\'t have two answers..\n\nWait, the options are A, B, C, D, so only one is correct.Let me check again.\n\nThe definition says "informal and unconscious learning experience that is not clearly specified in the school\'s planned curriculum.".\n\nSo the key is whether the learning is part of the planned curriculum..\n\nA) The military training is part of the planned curriculum (since the school organizes it to cultivate willpower).So the willpower aspect is part of the formal curriculum.Therefore, this is not an invisible curriculum.So A is the answer.\n\nC) The festival is part of the planned curriculum to let students know the motto and style.So the knowledge of the motto is part of the formal curriculum.Thus, C is also not an invisible curriculum..\n\nHmm, this is conflicting.But maybe the difference is in how the learning occurs.The invisible curriculum is more about the unintended lessons.For example, if the school\'s motto is taught through the festival (planned), then it\'s formal.But if students absorb the school\'s values through daily interactions (like seeing teachers act in a certain way), that\'s invisible..\n\nThe question\'s options are about which is NOT an example.So between A and C, which is more clearly part of the formal curriculum?The military training\'s explicit goal is willpower, which is part of the intended curriculum.The festival\'s explicit goal is to teach the motto, so that\'s also formal..\n\nWait, maybe the answer is C because the school motto and style are part of the institutional culture, which is part of the hidden curriculum.Wait, but if the school is explicitly organizing an event to teach them, then it\'s formal..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the "invisible curriculum" includes things like social norms, values, attitudes that students pick up without being directly taught.So the military training\'s goal is to build willpower, which is an explicit goal, so that\'s not invisible.The festival\'s explicit goal is to teach the motto, so that\'s also not invisible.So then, perhaps the answer is A or C, but the options might have only one correct choice..\n\nAlternatively, maybe option B is part of the invisible curriculum.Let me re-examine B:.\n\nB) When children learn teaching courses, they cultivate their interest in learning and teaching..\n\nIf the teaching courses are part of the formal curriculum, but the interest is a side effect, that\'s invisible.So B would be an example of invisible curriculum..\n\nC) The festival is planned to let students know the motto.So the motto is part of the formal curriculum..\n\nD) Extracurricular activities to establish relationships.The activity is planned, but the relationship building might be the intended goal, so that\'s part of the formal curriculum.Wait, but the question states the activity is to establish the relationship.So that\'s intentional, so part of the formal curriculum.Thus D is not invisible.The visible curriculum examples are clear and well-defined, such as explicitly stated academic goals or planned activities with specific objectives.The invisible curriculum, on the other hand, consists of hidden or unintended lessons that are not part of the formal educational structure.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
