ance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury (so the method works in the target plants)\n\nThe argument's conclusion is based on the experiment in Arabidopsis, and wants to apply that to vegetables. The key missing link is that the transgenic vegetables will indeed have the same response (enhanced fragrance when injured). The problem's context already says that in Arabidopsis, when the larvae eat the leaves (injury), the fragrance increases. But if they are applying this to vegetables, they need to assume that in the vegetables, the transgene will also cause the same effect (i.e., injury leads to enhanced fragrance). So B is a necessary assumption. If B isn't true, then the method wouldn't work in vegetables, so the conclusion fails. \n\nMeanwhile, A is about the current use of pesticides. If pesticides are not mainly for pests, then reducing pests might not reduce pesticide use. But the conclusion is about the potential reduction if this method is applied. The conclusion's validity (the logical connection between the premise and conclusion) requires that the method's effect (reducing pests) would lead to less pesticide use, which requires that pesticides are used for pests. Hence A is also needed. \n\nWait, but the problem is asking which premise must be added to make the conclusion valid. The conclusion is \"if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced\". The \"research result\" refers to the method where the plant's fragrance increases when injured, attracting parasitoids. \n\nTherefore, the argument assumes:\n\n1. The method works in vegetables (B is needed).\n\n2. Pesticides are used primarily to control the pests that this method targets (A is needed).\n\nSo both premises are needed. But the options are A, B, C, D. The question is which must be added. \n\nHowever, in the context given, the conclusion is about reducing pesticide use in general. The argument as stated might not