      "text": "4. The internet allows more accurate targeting. Because if they can target the right audience more precisely, even a smaller audience can generate higher revenue per view. So even with a smaller audience, each impression is more valuable. That would explain higher revenue from internet. So D is the answer.\n</think>\n\nThe paradox arises because the Internet audience is much smaller yet generates more revenue. To resolve this, we need a reason why the smaller Internet audience yields higher revenue. \n\n**Option D** states that Internet ads allow more accurate targeting. This makes sense because precise targeting can increase ad effectiveness, leading to higher engagement or conversion rates per viewer. Even with a smaller audience, each impression might be more valuable, thus generating more revenue. \n\nOther options fail to address the paradox as directly:  \n- **A** (identical ads) would exacerbate the contradiction, as TV\u2019s larger audience should dominate revenue.  \n- **B** (more TV experience) doesn\u2019t explain why Internet revenue is higher.  \n- **C** (cost differences) involves expenses, not revenue generation.  \n\nThus, **D** is the correct answer.  \n\n**Answer: D**",
