ger cars (regardless of company) are all overloaded (per A\'s correct statement).So if a company like Ruyi has a passenger car, it would be overloaded.But the problem doesn\'t specify whether Ruyi and Jixiang have passenger cars.Hmm, the options refer to their passenger cars.The problem doesn\'t state whether the companies have both buses and cars, but perhaps the question assumes that Ruyi and Jixiang have both?Or maybe the passenger cars mentioned in the options are separate from the buses in C\'s statement.\n\nWait, the options are about the passenger cars of Ruyi and Jixiang, which are different from the buses mentioned by C. So in this case, if A is correct, then all passenger cars (including Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s passenger cars) are overloaded.So in this scenario (B wrong, A and C correct), the passenger cars of both companies would be overloaded.So looking at the options:.\n\nOption B says both Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s passenger cars were overloaded.That would be true in this scenario.Option A says they are not overloaded, which would be false.Option C and D would also be false.However, we need to check if this is the only scenario where B is the wrong one, or also other possibilities.\n\nWait, let\'s check the third possibility: suppose that C is the wrong one.Then A and B are correct.\n\nIf C is wrong, then it\'s not true that both Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s buses are overloaded.So at least one of them is not overloaded.\n\nSince A is correct, all passenger cars are overloaded.\n\nB is correct, so all passenger buses are not overloaded.Therefore, Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s buses (as passenger buses) are not overloaded.Therefore, C\'s statement that both are overloaded is indeed wrong, which is consistent.So in this scenario:.\n\n- All passenger cars are overloaded (so Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s passenger cars, if any, are overloaded).All buses (including those two) are overloaded.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
