ction. So option A is impossible.\n\nOption C says Ruyi's cars are overloaded, Jixiang's not. But in both scenarios where only one is wrong, in both cases, the passenger cars of both companies are overloaded, so this is false.\n\nOption D says Jixiang's cars are overloaded, Ruyi's are not. That's also false for the same reason.\n\nTherefore, the only option that must be true in both valid scenarios is option B.\n\nWait, but let me check again the case where C is wrong. In that case, A is correct (so all passenger cars are overloaded, including both Ruyi and Jixiang's). So their passenger cars are both overloaded, so option B holds.\n\nIn the case where B is wrong (so C is correct), then Ruyi and Jixiang's buses are overloaded (so buses are overloaded), but A is correct so their cars are overloaded. So again, passenger cars of both are overloaded. So yes, B must be true in both cases.\n\nBut what if the scenario where C is wrong? Then C's statement is wrong, so at least one of the two buses isn't overloaded, but since B is correct, both aren't. So Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are not overloaded, but their cars are. So the passenger cars are overloaded regardless, so B holds.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be option B.\n\nWait, but the options mention \"passenger cars\" in all options, while C's statement was about buses. So the problem is about the cars' overloads, not buses. The options are all about passenger cars of the companies, so the statements about buses by B and C don't directly affect those options except through the validity of the statements.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that option B must be true.\n</think>\n\nTo determine which option must be true given that only one of the three statements (A, B, or C) is wrong, we analyze each scenario where one statement is incorrect while the others are correct:\n\n### **Scenario 1: A is wrong (B and C are correct)**\n- **A is wrong**: Not all passenger cars are overloaded (at least one is not).  \n- **B 