ait, so let me re-express the statements formally.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nC = criminal acts\n\nI = illegal acts\n\nS = acts sanctioned by criminal law.\n\nPremises:\n\n1. All C are S. (All criminal acts are sanctioned)\n\n2. Some I are C. (Some illegal acts are criminal acts)\n\n3. Huang Tao's acts (H) are I. (H is a subset of I)\n\nNow, the question is which must be true.\n\nOption A: Some I are S.\n\nBecause from 2 and 1, since some I are C, and all C are S, then those some I that are C are also S. Therefore, there exists some I that are S. Therefore, A is true.\n\nOption D: All S are C. Is this necessarily true?\n\nPremise 1 says All C are S, but not the converse. The converse would be that All S are C. But the premise does not state that. Therefore, S could have more elements. So D is not necessarily true. So D's statement is not necessarily true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer must be A.\n\nWait but the options also include B and C. Let's check those again.\n\nOption B: Huang Tao's acts are criminal (i.e., H is subset of C). But we only know that H is subset of I, and that some I are C. So H could be in the part of I that is not C. So B is not necessarily true.\n\nOption C: Huang Tao's acts are sanctioned (H is subset of S). Well, since H is subset of I, and unless H is part of the I that are C, then they would be sanctioned. But if H is not in the C part of I, then they might not be sanctioned. Therefore, C is not necessarily true.\n\nSo the only one that must be true is A. Therefore the correct answer is A.\n\nWait, but I need to make sure. Let me think of an example.\n\nSuppose:\n\nCriminal acts are all acts that are both illegal and involve violence. So some illegal acts (the violent ones) are criminal. Other illegal acts (like jaywalking) are not criminal.\n\nHuang Tao's acts are illegal, say he jaywalked. Then his acts are illegal but not criminal, so not sanctioned by criminal law. But in this case, A would still hold because the some illegal acts (the violent ones) are sanctioned. So A holds. But Huang Tao's acts (C) would not be sanctioned. So in this case, A is true. If there's at least one illegal act that is criminal, then A holds. Since the premise says some are, so yes.\n\nTherefore A is correct. Then the answer is A. So why did I initially think D? Because maybe I misread the first premise. But the first premise says all criminal are sanctioned, but doesn't say that only criminal acts are sanctioned. So D is not necessarily true. So the answer is A.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again. The options are A, B, C, D. The user says to pick the correct one. So according to this reasoning, A must be true. Therefore the answer is A. But I need to confirm once more.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is in Chinese, but the translation seems okay. So yeah, I think A is the correct answer. So the answer should be A.\n\nWait, but the user's options are:\n\nA) Some illegal acts will be punished by criminal law\n\nB) Huang Tao's behavior is a crime\n\nC) Huang Tao's actions will be sanctioned by criminal law\n\nD) All actions sanctioned by criminal law are criminal actions.\n\nWait, so D is saying that sanctioned actions are all criminal. But the first premise is that all criminal are sanctioned, but sanctioned could include more. So D is the converse and not necessarily true. So A is correct.\n\nTherefore the correct answer is A.\n\nWait, but the question is phrased as \"must be true\". So A must be true, while others may not be. So I think A is correct.\n\nBut wait, let me look again. The first premise says all criminal acts are sanctioned by criminal law. So the sanctioned set includes all criminal acts. The second premise says that some illegal acts are criminal. So those some illegal acts are in the criminal set, hence are sanctioned. Thus, those illegal acts are sanctioned. Hence, some illegal acts are sanctioned. Therefore A holds.\n\nTherefore the correct answer is A.\n\nWait, but the first time I thought maybe D was correct. But no, D's statement is not necessarily true. So the correct answer is A. So the answer should be option A.\n\nHmm. I think I've gone through this thoroughly. So I'll go with A.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{A}\n</think>\n\nGiven the context:\n1. All criminal acts will be sanctioned by the criminal law.\n2. Some illegal acts are criminal acts.\n3. Huang Tao's acts are illegal acts.\n\nWe need to determine which of the options must be true.\n\n**Analysis of each option:**\n\n- **Option A: Some illegal acts will be punished by criminal law.**\n  - From premise 2, some illegal acts are criminal acts. From premise 1, all criminal acts are sanctioned by criminal law. Therefore, the some illegal acts that are criminal will be sanctioned. This makes A true.\n\n- **Option B: Huang Tao's behavior is a crime.**\n  - Huang Tao's acts are illegal, but only some illegal acts are criminal. Since we don't know if Huang Tao's illegal acts are among those that are criminal, B is not necessarily true.\n\n- **Option C: Huang Tao's actions will be sanctioned by criminal law.**\n  - Similar to B, Huang Tao's illegal acts might not be criminal. Without confirmation that his acts are criminal, C is not necessarily true.\n\n- **Option D: All actions sanctioned by criminal law are criminal actions.**\n  - Premise 1 states that all criminal acts are sanctioned, but it does not restrict sanctioned acts to only criminal ones. Therefore, D is not necessarily true.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe only option that must be true based on the given premises is A.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
