he reverse.The application is about Shimada\'s criticism, so this might not be relevant.Unless the principle applies to both, but the question is specifically about the given application, so maybe C is not it.\n\nOption D: Both are regularly tardy, but Shimada criticizes McFeney without criticizing his own.So here, both are tardy.The principle says if you don\'t criticize yourself or vow to stop, you can\'t criticize others.In this case, since Shimada is tardy, and he doesn\'t criticize himself (as per the scenario, he doesn\'t criticize his own), and he also hasn\'t vowed to stop (the application\'s condition is that he doesn\'t vow to stop).Wait, the application\'s condition is specifically about not vowing.Wait, the application\'s exact wording is "if Shimada does not vow to stop being tardy himself, he should not criticize McFeney.." So the principle requires either criticizing oneself OR vowing to stop.The application is focusing on the vow part..\n\nIn option D, the scenario is both are tardy.Shimada criticizes McFeney\'s tardiness without criticizing his own.So that means Shimada is not criticizing himself (so that part of the condition is met) and also, since the application\'s premise is that he doesn\'t vow to stop (the "if" part of the application\'s statement is about not vowing), then in this scenario, if Shimada hasn\'t vowed to stop, then according to the principle, he shouldn\'t criticize.Therefore, this scenario would be exactly the case where the principle is applied, so D would justify the application..\n\nWait, but in the options, does D mention anything about Shimada not vowing?The option says "without criticizing his own" but doesn\'t mention the vow.Hmm, the principle requires either criticizing oneself OR vowing to stop.The application\'s condition is specifically about the vow.The question is which option, if true, justifies the application.The application is an instance of the principle.To justify the application, the scenario must fit the principle\'s conditions.So in D, if Shimada is tardy (so the behavior is present in himself), he doesn\'t criticize himself (so one part of the condition is met), and if he also hasn\'t vowed to stop (the other part), then the principle applies.The application\'s conclusion is that he shouldn\'t criticize.So the scenario in D would fit the principle\'s conditions..\n\nLooking at the options again, D says "both are regularly tardy, but Shimada criticizes McFeney\'s tardiness without criticizing his own."The lack of criticizing his own means that the first part of the principle\'s condition (not criticizing oneself) is met.The application\'s condition is specifically about not vowing to stop.Wait, but the principle\'s condition is either not criticizing oneself OR not vowing.Wait, the principle says "if one does not criticize a form of behavior in oneself OR vow to stop it, then.." So the negation of (criticize OR vow) would be (not criticize AND not vow).Wait, actually, the principle is phrased as "if not (criticize OR vow), then shouldn\'t criticize others."Wait, actually, the principle is phrased as "If one does not criticize a form of behavior in oneself OR vow to stop it, then one should not criticize that form of behavior in another."Wait, hold on, the wording is a bit ambiguous.Let me parse the principle again:.\n\nPrinciple: If one does not criticize a form of behavior in oneself OR vow to stop it, then one should not criticize that form of behavior in another.\n\nWait, the structure is: If (not (criticize oneself) OR not (vow to stop)), then should not criticize others.Wait, actually, the wording is "If one does not criticize.. OR vow to stop.." So actually, it\'s (¬criticize) OR (¬vow).Wait, no, let me parse it again.\n\nOriginal principle: "If one does not criticize a form of behavior in oneself or vow to stop it, then one should not criticize that form of behavior in another.".\n\nWait, the phrasing is a bit ambiguous.The "or" could be connecting "criticize oneself" and "vow to stop it", so the negation applies to both?Like "If one does not (criticize oneself OR vow to stop it), then.." That would make the principle: If one does neither criticize oneself nor vow to stop it, then shouldn\'t criticize others.\n\nAlternatively, it could be parsed as "If one does not criticize oneself, or [if one] does not vow to stop it, then..", which would be the same as the first interpretation.Because "does not A or B" is equivalent to "¬A ∨ ¬B" in logic.Wait, actually, in English, "does not A or B" can be ambiguous.But the standard interpretation would be that "does not (A or B)" is equivalent to "¬A ∧ ¬B", but actually no, "does not (A or B)" would be the same as "¬A ∧ ¬B", but the way it\'s written is "does not A or B", which might be interpreted as "does not A" OR "does not B".\n\nWait, this is crucial.Let me think again.The principle is written as: "If one does not criticize a form of behavior in oneself or vow to stop it, then one should not criticize that form of behavior in another.".\n\nIn logical terms, this is: If ¬(CriticizeSelf) OR ¬(VowToStop), then ¬(CriticizeOther).\n\nWait, actually, the structure is: "If (¬ CriticizeSelf) OR (¬ VowToStop), then ¬ CriticizeOther".So the principle is that if either you don\'t criticize yourself or you don\'t vow to stop, then you can\'t criticize others.That means that to be allowed to criticize others, you must do at least one of: criticize yourself or vow to stop.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive would be: If you criticize others, then you must have either criticized yourself or vowed to stop.\n\nSo applying this to the application: If Shimada does not vow to stop (so ¬VowToStop is true), then he shouldn\'t criticize others.But according to the principle, it\'s only if either ¬CriticizeSelf OR ¬VowToStop.So even if he does vow to stop, but doesn\'t criticize himself, he still can\'t criticize others.Alternatively, if he doesn\'t vow, but does criticize himself, he can.\n\nThe appli