h ¬C and ¬V, then the application would have to mention both.Since the application only mentions ¬V, that suggests the principle is using the OR condition, so that either is sufficient to trigger the conclusion.\n\nTherefore, the principle is: If either you don\'t criticize yourself OR you don\'t vow to stop, then you can\'t criticize others.\n\nTherefore, in the application, the scenario is that Shimada does not vow to stop (¬V), so regardless of whether he criticizes himself or not, he can\'t criticize others.But the application is stated as "if he does not vow to stop..", so that\'s exactly the case where ¬V is true, so the principle applies.\n\nNow, the question is which option, if true, justifies the application.So the application is an instance where the principle is applied.To justify it, the scenario must fit the principle\'s conditions.So the correct option should present a case where Shimada is in the situation described by the application (doesn\'t vow to stop) and thus the principle applies.\n\nLooking at the options again:.\n\nOption D says: Both are regularly tardy, but Shimada criticizes McFeney\'s tardiness without criticizing his own.\n\nSo in this case, Shimada is tardy (so the behavior is present in himself), he doesn\'t criticize himself (so ¬C is true), and the application\'s condition is that he doesn\'t vow to stop (¬V).Since the principle requires only that either ¬C or ¬V to prohibit criticism, then even if he had vowed, but didn\'t criticize, it would still apply.But the application specifically mentions the ¬V case, so perhaps the scenario must include that he didn\'t vow.\n\nWait, but the options don\'t mention the vow.Option D says "without criticizing his own", which covers ¬C, but doesn\'t mention the vow.However, the application\'s premise is that he doesn\'t vow.So perhaps the correct answer must include that he didn\'t vow, but none of the options mention the vow except maybe option A?.\n\nWait, looking again:.\n\nOption A: "criticizes himself for occasional tardiness"—so he does criticize himself (so C is true), so ¬C is false.Therefore, the principle\'s antecedent would require ¬C or ¬V.If he does criticize himself, then unless he also didn\'t vow (¬V), the antecedent would not hold.But the application is when he doesn\'t vow.Hmm, but in option A, he does criticize himself, so even if he didn\'t vow, the antecedent (¬C ∨ ¬V) would still be true because ¬V would be true.Wait, but the application is when he doesn\'t vow, so maybe in that case, even with C being true (he criticizes himself), if he doesn\'t vow, then the antecedent is still true because ¬V is true, so he still can\'t criticize others?Wait no, because the antecedent is ¬C OR ¬V.If he does criticize himself (so ¬C is false) but doesn\'t vow (so ¬V is true), then the antecedent is true (since one of the terms is true).Therefore, he still can\'t criticize others.But the application is about when he doesn\'t vow, regardless of whether he criticizes himself.So the application is correct in that scenario.But the question is which option, if true, justifies the application.The application is an instance of the principle.So the scenario in the option must be one where the principle\'s conditions are met, leading to the conclusion that Shimada shouldn\'t criticize.\n\nBut the question is asking which option, if true, would make the application valid.That is, which scenario would show that the application is correctly applying the principle.\n\nLooking at D: Both are tardy, and Shimada criticizes McFeney without criticizing his own.So in this case, Shimada is engaging in the behavior (so the behavior is present in himself), he doesn\'t criticize himself (so ¬C is true), so the principle\'s antecedent is met (¬C OR ¬V), so he shouldn\'t criticize others.The application\'s conclusion is that if he doesn\'t vow to stop (¬V), then he shouldn\'t criticize.But in D, even without knowing about the vow, since ¬C is true, the principle already appl