      "index": 0,
      "text": " C?\n\nWait, let me recheck the passage again. Wait the last sentence says \"one of the reasons alternative medicine is free of such side effects is that it does not have any effects at all.\" Wait, that's a bit confusing. So if alternative medicine has no effects, then it can't have side effects, but also can't help. So, alternative medicine is ineffective for everything, but people turn to it when orthodox fails. So for the trivial ailments, orthodox is ineffective, so people turn to alternative medicine, which also doesn't help. Similarly for the serious diseases. Therefore, there are some conditions (the trivial ones and the serious ones) where neither is effective. So that would make B correct?\n\nWait, this is conflicting.\n\nThe problem is the wording of the last sentence: \"One of the reasons alternative medicine is free of such side effects is that it does not have any effects at all.\"\n\nSo, the lack of side effects is because it has no effects. That would mean that alternative medicine has no effects, so it is ineffective. So, alternative medicine is ineffective for everything?\n\nIf that's the case, then for the trivial and serious illnesses, both orthodox and alternative are ineffective. Hence, B would be correct: there are some medical conditions (those mentioned) where neither is effective.\n\nAlternatively, the question is about the options given.\n\nLet me go through each option again:\n\nOption B says: There are some medical conditions for which no orthodox or alternative treatment is effective.\n\nIf for the trivial illnesses, orthodox is ineffective and alternative is also ineffective, then that would satisfy B. Similarly for the serious diseases. Therefore, B is correct.\n\nOption C says: There are some trivial illnesses that can be treated effectively by alternative medicine.\n\nBut if alternative has no effects, then it can't treat anything, so C would be incorrect.\n\nWait, so the key is the last sentence. The passage says alternative medicine is free of side effects because it has no effects at all. So, that would mean that alternative medicine has no effects: neither good nor bad. So it doesn't help and doesn't harm. So, it's ineffective. Therefore, for the trivial illnesses, when orthodox doesn't work, alternative also can't help, so those conditions are not treatable by either. Therefore, B is inferred.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B.\n\nWait but the options given are:\n\nB) There are some medical conditions for which no orthodox or alternative treatment is effective.\n\nC) There are some trivial illnesses that can be treated effectively by the methods of alternative medicine.\n\nSo if alternative medicine is ineffective, then C is false, and B is true. \n\nTherefore, B is the correct answer. But let me check other options again.\n\nOption D: Practitioners are acting in bad faith. The passage does not say anything about their intentions. The author might imply that alternative medicine is a placebo, but the text doesn't state that practitioners are dishonest. So D is not inferable.\n\nOption A is about the future, which we can't infer.\n\nTherefore, B is correct.\n\nWait but in the problem statement, the options are labeled A, B, C, D. Let me confirm the original options again:\n\nOptions:\n\nA) Orthodox medicine will eventually produce a solution for the diseases that are currently incurable.\n\nB) There are some medical conditions for which no orthodox or alternative treatment is effective.\n\nC) There are some trivial illnesses that can be treated effectively by the methods of alternative medicine.\n\nD) Practitioners of alternative medicine are acting in bad faith.\n\nYes, so B is correct because the passage states that orthodox is ineffective for those conditions (trivial and serious), and alternative has no effects, so it's also ineffective. Therefore, the conditions for which orthodox is ineffective (both trivial and serious) are also ineffective for alternative, so B can be inferred.\n\nBut wait, the first sentence says \"at the more trivial end, orthodox medicine is largely ineffective\". \"Largely ineffective\" might mean that it's ineffective for many, but maybe not all? But the question is about if there exists some conditions where neither works. Since the passage says that for trivial ailments, orthodox is ineffective, and alternative is ineffective (since it has no effects), then yes, those trivial ailments are conditions where neither works. Hence B is correct.\n\nSo the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nBased on the context provided, we can infer the following:\n\nThe passage states that orthodox medicine is ineffective for both trivial ailments (e.g., aches, allergies) and serious diseases (e.g., advanced cancer, lupus). Additionally, it asserts that alternative medicine is free of side effects because it \"does not have any effects at all.\" This implies that alternative medicine is also ineffective for all conditions since it lacks therapeutic effects. \n\n### Analysis of Options:\n- **A)** Suggests orthodox medicine will eventually cure currently incurable diseases.  \n  *The passage does not provide evidence about future outcomes; it only states that orthodox medicine has \"yet to produce a cure.\" This prediction cannot be inferred.*  \n\n- **B)** Claims there are medical conditions where neither orthodox nor alternative medicine is effective.  \n  *The passage explicitly states that orthodox medicine is ineffective for trivial ailments and serious diseases. Since alternative medicine has \"no effects at all,\" it cannot treat these conditions either. Thus, there are conditions (e.g., those listed) where neither approach works.*  \n\n- **C)** States that some trivial illnesses are treatable by alternative medicine.  \n  *The passage directly contradicts this by stating alternative medicine has \"no effects at all,\" meaning it cannot effectively treat any condition, trivial or otherwise.*  \n\n- **D)** Alleges that practitioners of alternative medicine act in \"bad faith.\"  \n  *The text does not address practitioners' intentions or ethics, making this unsupported.*  \n\n### Conclusion:\n**B** is the only logically inferable option. The context confirms that both orthodox and alternative medicine fail for the listed trivial and serious conditions.  \n\n**Answer: B**",
      "logprobs": null,
