      "text": "\u9009\u9879D\u4e0d\u8d28\u7591\u7edf\u8ba1\u63a8\u65ad\u7684\u53ef\u9760\u6027\uff0c\u56e0\u4e3a\u5b83\u8ba8\u8bba\u7684\u662f\u6210\u672c\u95ee\u9898\uff0c\u800c\u5176\u4ed6\u9009\u9879\u90fd\u6d89\u53ca\u6570\u636e\u6837\u672c\u7684\u6709\u6548\u6027\u3001\u6280\u672f\u51c6\u786e\u6027\u6216\u7ed3\u8bba\u7684\u6b63\u786e\u8868\u8ff0\u3002\u56e0\u6b64\uff0c\u6b63\u786e\u7b54\u6848\u662fD\u3002\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **D) The cost of paternity testing is too high.**  \n\n### Analysis:  \nThe question asks which option does **not** question the reliability of the statistical inference (i.e., the claim that \"15% of fathers raise children for others\" based on the 15% exclusion rate in paternity tests). The key is to identify which option does not challenge the validity of the data, its interpretation, or the generalization made.  \n\n- **Option A** questions the **title's wording**, pointing out that the 15% refers only to fathers who underwent testing, not all fathers. This challenges the generalization and thus questions reliability.  \n- **Option B** highlights **selection bias**: the sample includes only individuals who already suspected paternity issues, making the 15% rate non-representative of the broader population. This undermines the inference's reliability.  \n- **Option C** casts doubt on the **accuracy of the tests themselves**, implying the 15% exclusion rate might stem from technical errors rather than true non-paternity. This directly questions the data's reliability.  \n- **Option D** focuses on the **cost of testing**, which is unrelated to the statistical validity of the inference. While high costs might affect who chooses to take the test (introducing bias), the option itself does not explicitly address this. Instead, it raises a logistical or economic concern that does not inherently challenge the reliability of the statistical conclusion drawn from the tested group.  \n\nThus, **D** does not question the reliability of the statistical inference.",
