nd disease (from the survey). To support the prediction that not taking measures will lead to more cases, you can either strengthen the idea that exposure will continue (B) or strengthen the causal link (A). Since the survey already shows that exposure leads to cases, perhaps the key is ensuring that continued exposure will happen. So B is about the exposure continuing if no measures are taken, which directly supports the prediction. However, the expert's opinion already assumes that if measures aren't taken, the pollution remains. So B is providing additional info that even with measures, it's slow, but if you don't take them, then it's even worse. Wait, maybe B is more about the necessity of measures but not directly the consequence. Alternatively, A gives a reason why the diseases would increase, explaining the mechanism, making the experts' claim more plausible. So maybe A is better because it explains why the pollution leads to more cases, making the experts' conclusion stronger. The survey showed that high pollutants cause 10-15% to get the disease, but if the particles damage the eyes over time (A), then prolonged exposure would lead to higher rates. So A is a better support. B is more about the feasibility of improving air quality, which is about whether the measures can be effective, but the expert's point is about not taking measures leading to more cases. So B supports the idea that without measures, the pollution stays, which is part of the premise. The conclusion is \"if not taken measures, then incidence increases\". The survey already links pollution to incidence, so the support would be something that says that without measures, pollution stays high. But B says that improving takes time and people are in polluted environment. Maybe B is more about that. Wait the question is asking for the option that supports the experts' opinion. The experts are saying if you don't take measures, the incidence will go up. So the key is to show that without measures,