rt, both could be considered, but which is better? The question asks \"best supports\". Since the expert's argument is based on the premise that continued exposure (without measures) will lead to more cases, B directly addresses the continuation of exposure if no measures are taken. A supports the mechanism, which is already partially established by the survey. Therefore, B might be the better choice. Wait but I thought earlier that A was better. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the survey already shows that exposure causes the disease. The expert's prediction is that if measures aren't taken, the incidence will rise. To support this, you can either show that measures are needed to reduce exposure (so without them, exposure continues) or that the exposure indeed causes the disease. Since the first part is already covered by the survey, the missing link is that without measures, exposure continues. Therefore, B is necessary for that. Because if measures could be taken quickly even without urgency, then not taking urgent measures might still allow for later measures that reduce exposure. But B says that improvement can't be done quickly, so if you don't take urgent measures (i.e., delay), then the improvement won't come in time, so exposure remains. Hence, the incidence would increase. Therefore B supports that taking urgent measures is needed to prevent the continued exposure, and without them, the exposure stays, leading to more cases. \n\nTherefore, I think B is the better answer here. Wait, but initially I thought A. Now I'm confused. Let me see the options again.\n\nWait, the expert's opinion is that if urgent measures are not taken, the incidence will increase. So the key is that urgent measures are necessary to prevent the increase. The support would be a statement that if you don't act urgently, then the problem persists. \n\nOption B says that the improvement can't be done quickly, so many work in polluted areas. So if you don't take urgent measures (i.e., don't start now), the air quality won't improve quickly, and people remain in polluted areas. Hence, the exposure continues, leading to more cases. That directly supports the expert's point.\n\nOption A explains how the particles cause the disease, which is good, but the survey already provides the correlation. The expert's prediction is about the future increase, so ensuring that exposure continues (without measures) is the key. Thus, B is better. So I think I made a mistake earlier. The answer should be B?\n\nWait, but the question says \"if urgent measures are not taken to improve air quality\". So the measures are to improve air quality. If the measures are not taken, then the air quality won't improve. B says that improvement can't be done in a short period, but many have to work in polluted environment. So even if you take measures, it won't help quickly, so people are still exposed. But if you don't take measures, then the improvement won't happen even more. So in that case, the air quality remains bad for longer, leading to more cases. Thus, B is supporting the idea that without taking measures, the problem remains, leading to higher incidence. \n\nAlternatively, A is a direct causal link, which is important but maybe already somewhat covered by the survey. The survey already says that exposure causes the disease, so A is an explanation for that, making the survey's findings more credible. But the question is about supporting the expert's opinion that not taking measures will lead to increase. So the key is to link the inaction to continued exposure. Therefore B is better. \n\nWait, but maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me think of it this way: the expert's conclusion is that if we don't take urgent measures, the incidence will go up. The support must show that not taking measures will lead to higher incidence. \n\nOption B: If you don't take measures (since even with measures it takes time), then the air quality won't improve quickly, so people remain in polluted environ