easures, the problem continues. \n\nYes. So B is correct. \n\nHmm, so I think I made a mistake in my initial thought. The correct answer should be B. But I'm not 100% sure. Wait, let me try to think again. \n\nAlternatively, maybe A is better because it directly explains why the exposure leads to more cases over time. The expert is talking about the incidence increasing, which would require that the exposure causes cumulative damage. So A says that long-term contact affects lacrimal glands, which is a reason why the incidence would rise with continued exposure. \n\nThe survey says that continuous exposure causes 10-15% to get it. If you have continuous exposure over time, then perhaps more people get it beyond the initial 10-15%? Or maybe the existing 10-15% have worsening conditions. \n\nThe question mentions \"incidence of these diseases and related complications will increase.\" So the incidence (number of new cases) might increase because more people are exposed over time. \n\nA explains that long-term exposure damages the eyes further, so the complications would be more severe or the diseases more prevalent. \n\nBut the survey already links exposure to the diseases. The key is whether the exposure continues. \n\nHmm. \n\nAlternatively, if A is true, then the mechanism exists, so the experts' claim is more plausible. \n\nB says that without measures, the exposure continues, so the effects will accumulate. \n\nBoth support, but which is better? \n\nIn logical reasoning questions, usually, an option that directly addresses the cause leading to the effect in the conclusion is better. Here, the conclusion is that not taking measures will lead to increased incidence. \n\nB provides a reason why not taking measures leads to continued exposure, which causes the increase. \n\nA provides a reason why exposure causes the diseases, which the survey already showed. \n\nTherefore, the best support is B because it connects the lack of measures to the continuation of the cause