      "index": 0,
      "text": " D is the correct answer because it mirrors the fallacy of division, where a property of the collective is incorrectly ascribed to an individual member.\n</think>\n\nThe logical fallacy in Xiao Li's argument is the **fallacy of division**, where he incorrectly assumes that because the green space belongs to all community owners collectively, it must belong to him individually. This is a mistake in reasoning because properties of the whole (community ownership) do not necessarily apply to each individual part (Xiao Li's personal claim).\n\n### Analysis of Options:\n- **A** argues that \"since all behavior should be responsible, Xiao Wang\u2019s irresponsibility implies his behavior is correct.\" This is a **contradiction** rather than a fallacy of division, as it misapplies the premise to conclude the opposite.\n\n- **B** assumes buying a flower at the expo must be orchids because all orchids were ordered out. This is a **hasty generalization** or **false premise**, as the removal of orchids doesn\u2019t guarantee they were the only flowers available.\n\n- **C** concludes \"The Three Musketeers is Dumas\u2019s work\" because both \"all Dumas\u2019s works\" and \"The Three Musketeers\" cannot be read in a day. This is a **non sequitur**, falsely linking unrelated premises to draw a conclusion about authorship.\n\n- **D** concludes \"King Yia was invincible\" because the army (composed of all mobi knights) was invincible. This directly mirrors Xiao Li\u2019s fallacy: attributing a collective property (army\u2019s invincibility) to an individual member (King Yia). This is the **fallacy of division**.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe most similar mistake to Xiao Li\u2019s is **D**, as both incorrectly apply a collective property to an individual.\n\n**Answer:** D",
      "logprobs": null,
