 is B.\n\nWait, looking back at the original context: the tax increase is both a percentage (from 5% to 11%) and a specific tax. The experts' reasoning is that the tax increase will lead to higher prices, which will reduce buyers.\n\nIf the output isn't affected by selling price (Option A), that would mean supply is inelastic, so prices might go up more, but demand could decrease. Not sure. So A probably supports the conclusion.\n\nOption C: other countries have lower tax rates. Doesn't directly affect the argument's logic.\n\nBetween B and D, I think D is a better answer because it introduces an alternative supply that allows buyers to avoid the tax, thus reducing the effectiveness of the tax in curbing purchases.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is D. However, I'm a bit torn between B and D. \n\nWait, but let's think again. The question asks which weakens the conclusion. The conclusion is that the tax will increase prices and reduce buyers. If B is true (people are less sensitive to price), then even if prices go up, they buy the same amount. So the conclusion's prediction of reduced buyers is wrong. That directly weakens.\n\nD says that private cigarettes proliferate. So maybe the legal prices go up, but people buy more from private, so their total purchases might not decrease. So the number buying (from any source) might not decrease. That also weakens.\n\nSince both are possible, but in the options given, D is an example of a real-world effect that undercuts the policy's effectiveness, so the answer is D.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the answer is B.\n\nWait, in the options, option D says \"proliferation of private cigarettes\"\u2014this would mean that even after the tax, people have access to cheaper cigarettes, so the effective price doesn't rise much for them. Hence, demand remains, so the tax doesn't reduce buyers. So D weakens.\n\nOption B: higher income makes people less sensitive to price. So even if price rises, they keep buying. So also weakens.\