would be P, M, H. But then H is in two departments.That\'s okay.Let\'s see:.\n\nNow, check all constraints:.\n\n- Each department has three: yes.\n\n- Each assistant is in at least one: F (D1), G (D2), H (D1, D3), I (D2), M (D3), P (all three).\n\n- The one in all three is P.\n\n- F and G are not in the same department: F is in D1 and D2?Wait no, F is only in D1.G is in D2.So they are not together.Good.\n\n- H and I are not together: H is in D1 and D3; I is in D2.So no overlap.Good.\n\n- F and M are not in the same department: F is in D1, M in D3.Good.\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nA) F and H are in the same department: Yes, they are in D1 together.So in this scenario, A is true.\n\nBut does this scenario require A to be true?Or is there another scenario where A is not true?.\n\nLet me see another possible arrangement.\n\nSuppose instead of putting H with F, we have another arrangement.\n\nLet me try making X as M. Wait, maybe that\'s better.Let me see.\n\nSuppose X is M. So M is in all three departments.\n\nThen each department has M plus two others.\n\nNow, the remaining assistants are F, G, H, I, P.\n\nEach must be in at least one department.\n\nConstraints:.\n\n- F and G can\'t be in the same department.\n\n- H and I can\'t be in the same department.\n\n- F and M can\'t be in the same department.Wait, but M is in all departments, so F cannot be in any department with M, which means F cannot be in any department.But that\'s a problem because every assistant must be in at least one department.Therefore, M cannot be the person in all three departments, because that would require F to not be in any department, which violates the "each is in at least one" condition.Therefore, M cannot be the one in all three departments.So X cannot be M.\n\nSo that scenario is invalid, so X cannot be M. So that\'s a key point.\n\nSimilarly, let\'s see if X can be F.\n\nSuppose X is F. Then F is in all three departments.But the constraint says F and G can\'t be in the same department.Since F is in all departments, G can\'t be in any department.But that\'s impossible because G must be in at least one.So X can\'t be F either.\n\nSame with G: If X were G, then F can\'t be in any department, which is invalid.So X can\'t be G.\n\nSimilarly, H and I: Let\'s see, if X is H, then H is in all three departments.Then H and I can\'t be together, so I can\'t be in any department with H, but since H is in all departments, I can\'t be in any department.So that\'s impossible.Similarly for I as X.\n\nTherefore, the only possible candidates for X are P or someone else.Wait, the remaining candidates are P and maybe M?But M can\'t be as above.Wait, so X must be P, because the other options are invalid.Let me confirm.\n\nWait, let\'s check if X can be someone else.Let\'s try X = I.\n\nIf X is I, then I is in all three departments.Then H can\'t be in any department with I.Since I is in all departments, H can\'t be in any department, which is impossible.So no.\n\nSimilarly for H: X=H would exclude I entirely, which is bad.So the only possible candidate for X is P. Because when X is P, then all other constraints can be satisfied.\n\nTherefore, X must be P. Because otherwise, some constraints make it impossible.\n\nTherefore, P is the one in all three departments.So every department has P, plus two others.\n\nNow, with that in mind, let\'s redo the analysis with P being the one in all three.\n\nNow, the other assistants are F, G, H, I, M. Each must be in at least one department, and the constraints are:.\n\n- F and G not together.\n\n- H and I not together.\n\n- F and M not together.\n\nSince P is in all departments, each department has P plus two others.So three departments, each with two non-P members.\n\nTotal non-P assignments: 3 departments × 2 = 6.We have five assistants (F, G, H, I, M) to assign to these 6 slots, so one of them has to be in two departments, and the others in one each.Because 5 people need to cover 6 slots: 5 +1 (double) =6.So one person is in two departments, others in one each.\n\nWait, but each assistant can be in multiple departments except for P, who is in all three.The others can be in up to two departments.Because if someone is in three, that would be P\'s role.So yes, one of them can be in two departments, the rest in one.\n\nSo let\'s see.Let\'s try to construct the departments.\n\nLet me consider the constraints again.F cannot be with G or M. So F can only be with H or I, but also H and I can\'t be together.So if F is with H, then I must be with someone else, and vice versa.\n\nAlso, since F can\'t be with M, M can\'t be in any department with F, but since F is only in one or two departments (since he can\'t be in all three), M can be in other departments as long as not with F.\n\nLet me try to make a valid arrangement.\n\nFirst, since F can\'t be with G or M, let\'s see where F can go.\n\nSuppose F is in two departments.Let\'s say he\'s in D1 and D2.But each department must have two non-P members.\n\nWait, let\'s try step by step.\n\nLet me start by assigning F to a department.Let\'s say F is in D1 with H. So D1: P, F, H.\n\nThen F can\'t be in any other department because he can\'t be with G or M. Wait, but maybe he can be in another department that doesn\'t have G or M. Wait, but if F is in another department, say D2, then the other member can\'t be G or M. So could be I or someone else.Wait, but let\'s see.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s try to minimize overlaps.\n\nOption 1:.\n\nD1: P, F, H.\n\nD2: P, G, I.\n\nD3: P, M, someone else.The someone else has to be one of the remaining.The remaining people are F is already in D1, G in D2, H in D1, I in D2, so only M is left?Wait, no.Wait, the five non-P are F, G, H, I, M. So in D3, besides P and M, we need another person.The available are F, G, H, I.But F can\'t be with M (since F and M can\'t be together), so can\'t put F with M. G can be with M?Let\'s see, G and M can be together because the only constraints are F-G, H-I, and F-M.So G and M are allowed.So D3 could be P, G, M. But wait, but G is already in D2.That\'s okay because G can be in multiple departments as long as constraints are met.So:.\n\nD1: P, F, H.\n\nD2: P, G, I.\n\nD3: P, G, M.\n\nCheck constraints:.\n\n- Each department has three: yes.\n\n- Each non-P is in at least one:.\n\nF: D1.\n\nG: D2 and D3.\n\nH: D1.\n\nI: D2.\n\nM: D3.\n\nAll good.\n\n- F and G not together: F is in D1, G is in D2 and D3.So they are not together in any department.Good.\n\n- H and I not together: H in D1, I in D2.Good.\n\n- F and M not together: F is in D1, M in D3.Good.\n\nNow, check the options:.\n\nA) F and H are in the same department: Yes, D1.So A is true here.\n\nB) F and I: F is in D1, I in D2.No.\n\nC) I and P: Well, P is in all departments, so yes, but the option says I and P. Wait, the question is if I and P are in the same department.Since P is in all departments, I is in D2 with P, so yes.But the option is C: I and P. But the question is which must be correct.Wait, the options are:.\n\nA) F and H same dept.\n\nB) F and I.\n\nC) I and P.\n\nD) M and G same dept.\n\nIn this arrangement, D3 has M and G together, so D is true here as well.\n\nWait, but the question is which must be correct.So if in some arrangement A is true and D is also true, but in another arrangement only one is true, then we need to see which is always true.\n\nWait, let\'s see another possible arrangement where maybe D is not true.\n\nLet me try another arrangement where D is not true (i.e., M and G are not together).\n\nLet me try:.\n\nSuppose:.\n\nD1: P, F, H.\n\nD2: P, G, M (since they can be together).\n\nWait, but then M is with G in D2.But let\'s see:.\n\nWait, let\'s try to have M not with G.\n\nSuppose:.\n\nD1: P, F, H.\n\nD2: P, G, I.\n\nD3: P, M, I.Wait, but H and I can\'t be together?Wait, H is only in D1, so in D3, I and M are together.But H and I are not in the same department, so that\'s okay.Wait, but I is in D2 and D3.That\'s okay.Let\'s check constraints:.\n\nF is with H in D1, okay.\n\nG is in D2 with I, okay (since H and I not together is only about H and I being in the same dept; G and I are okay).\n\nM is in D3 with P and I.Wait, but I is allowed with M?The constraints don\'t prohibit that.The constraints are F-G, H-I, F-M.\n\nSo F and M are not together (F is in D1, M in D3), good.\n\nH and I: H is in D1, I is in D2 and D3.Since I is with H only in D3?No, H is only in D1.So I and H are not together.So that\'s okay.In every scenario, option C is incorrect and not valid. P is not in all departments, so P is not with I in any department. Therefore, C is false.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
