\n\nHmm, but looking back at the initial problem's options, perhaps I made a mistake in assuming that P is the triple assistant. Let me re-examine that step.\n\nThe problem says \"there is and only one general manager assistant in charge of three departments at the same time.\"\n\nSo that person must be in all three departments.\n\nIf that person isn't P, could it be someone else?\n\nLet me try to see if another person can be the triple.\n\nSuppose the triple is F. But then, since F is in all three departments, G cannot be in any department (because F and G cannot share any department), but G must be in at least one department. Contradiction.\n\nSimilarly, if the triple is G, then F cannot be in any department, which is invalid.\n\nTriple is H: then I cannot be in any department, invalid.\n\nTriple is I: then H can't be in any department.\n\nTriple is M: then F cannot be in any department (since F and M can't be together), but F must be in at least one, so invalid.\n\nTriple is P: that works, as we saw.\n\nThus, only P can be the triple. So yes, P must be in all departments.\n\nTherefore, every department has P. Thus, any assistant who must be in a department must be with P there. Since I has to be in at least one department, I must be with P in that department. Hence, C must be true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.\n\nBut the options given include option C. So I think that is correct.\n\nWait, but the original question's options might have a different answer. Wait, the user's problem's options are A, B, C, D as listed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the problem's constraints.\n\nWait the problem says \"each department is just under the charge of three general manager assistants.\"\n\nDoes that mean each department is managed by exactly three, and each assistant is in at least one department.\n\nYes.\n\nSo the key is that since I must be in at least one department with P, so C must hold.\n\nThus, the answer is C.\n\nBut let me check again the options:\n\nC) I and P are in charge of the same department \u2192 must be true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.\n\nWait, but in my first arrangement where I had:\n\nD1: P,F,H.\n\nD2: P,G,I.\n\nD3: P,G,M.\n\nHere, option C is true because I and P are in D2.\n\nIn the arrangement where I is with H and M:\n\nD1: P,F,H.\n\nD2: P,G,I.\n\nD3: P,M,H.\n\nHere, I is in D2 with P, so C holds.\n\nAnother arrangement:\n\nD1: P,F,I.\n\nD2: P,G,H.\n\nD3: P,M,G.\n\nHere, I is in D1 with P, so C holds.\n\nThus, in all cases, C must be true.\n\nTherefore, the answer must be C.\n\nHowever, I want to check if any of the other options must also be true in all scenarios.\n\nFor example, in some arrangements, A or D might be true, but there are scenarios where they aren't. So only C is always true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.\n\nBut looking back at the problem statement, maybe I misread the constraints.\n\nWait the problem says: \"F and G are not in charge of the same department.\" So they can't be together in any department.\n\n\"F and M are not in charge of the same department.\"\n\nH and I also can't be together.\n\nSo, in trying to make sure that the only possible answer is C.\n\nYes, I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{C}\n</think>\n\nGiven the problem of determining which option must be correct based on the constraints provided, we analyze the following:\n\n1. **Constraints Analysis**:\n   - There are six assistants: F, G, H, I, M, and P.\n   - Each department has three assistants, and one assistant is in charge of all three departments (the triple assistant).\n   - Constraints:\n     - F and G cannot be in the same department.\n     - H and I cannot be in the same department.\n     - F and M cannot be in the same department.\n\n2. **Identifying the Triple Assistant**:\n   - The triple assistant must be someone who can be in all three departments without violating constraints. \n   - Testing each assistant:\n     - F, G, H, I, and M cannot be the triple assistant due to constraints making it impossible for others to be placed.\n     - P is the only viable candidate for the triple assistant as it allows other assistants to be placed without conflict.\n\n3. **Implications of P as the Triple Assistant**:\n   - P must be in all three departments.\n   - Each department includes P plus two other assistants.\n   - All other assistants (F, G, H, I, M) must be placed in at least one department without violating constraints.\n\n4. **Analyzing the Options**:\n   - **Option A (F and H in the same department)**: Not necessarily true as F could be paired with I instead.\n   - **Option B (F and I in the same department)**: Not necessarily true as F could be paired with H instead.\n   - **Option C (I and P in the same department)**: Since P is in all departments and I must be in at least one department, I must be with P in that department. This is always true.\n   - **Option D (M and G in the same department)**: Not necessarily true as M can be placed with others not including G.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - The only option that must be true in all valid scenarios is **C**, as I must be paired with P in at least one department.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
