      "text": " B.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The context says \"the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced\". The research result is that in Arabidopsis, when the larvae eat the leaves, the fragrance increases, attracting parasitoids, thus eliminating the larvae. So the conclusion is that applying this (the transgenic method) to vegetables would reduce pesticide use. The key assumption here is that the transgenic vegetables when injured will also emit the enhanced fragrance, thereby attracting parasitoids. Because the research was done on Arabidopsis, but the conclusion is about vegetables in general (maybe other species). So unless we assume that the transgenic vegetables (the ones they plan to apply this to) also have the same response (fragrance enhanced when injured), then the conclusion is not valid. So B is necessary.\n\nAs for A: the argument might already assume that pesticides are used for pests, otherwise the conclusion wouldn't make sense. The researchers' conclusion is based on reducing pests through this method, so if pesticides are mainly for other purposes, then the conclusion's reduction might not occur. So A is also a necessary premise. Wait, but the question asks for the premise that must be added. The problem is, sometimes in these questions, only one option is correct. Let me see the options again.\n\nOption A is \"At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests\". If that's true, then reducing pests via this method would reduce pesticide use. If it's false, then maybe pesticides are used for other reasons, so even with fewer pests, pesticide use remains. Therefore A is a necessary assumption. However, does the argument already include this? The context says that the purpose of the fragrance is to attract natural enemies to eliminate pests. So the researchers are addressing pest control. The conclusion is