correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nThe problem presents three conditional statements:\n\n1. **If absolute fairness (A) is implemented, it leads to distribution according to work (D).**  \n   Symbolically: \\( A \\rightarrow D \\).\n\n2. **If distribution according to work (D), there will be inequality (R).**  \n   Symbolically: \\( D \\rightarrow R \\).\n\n3. **Only by equalizing the rich and poor (\u00acR) can absolute fairness (A) be implemented.**  \n   This implies \\( A \\rightarrow \\neg R \\), as equalizing the rich and poor (\u00acR) is a necessary condition for A.\n\n### Logical Analysis:\n- Assume \\( A \\) is true (absolute fairness is implemented).  \n  - From \\( A \\rightarrow D \\), \\( D \\) must be true.  \n  - From \\( D \\rightarrow R \\), \\( R \\) (inequality) follows.  \n  - However, \\( A \\rightarrow \\neg R \\) requires \\( \\neg R \\) (no inequality) for \\( A \\) to hold.  \n\nThis creates a **contradiction**: \\( A \\) implies both \\( R \\) (via \\( D \\)) and \\( \\neg R \\). Therefore, **\\( A \\) cannot be true** without leading to inconsistency.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe contradiction proves that **absolute fairness cannot be implemented**. The correct answer is:\n\n**D) Can not implement absolute fairness**",
