iting until the inferior works are forgotten. So he might not agree that it's only possible for past art. So he might not agree with C. Rachel's position doesn't address this, so they might not disagree here either.\n\nTherefore, the only point of disagreement is D. So the correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nD) Contemporary art is of lower quality than earlier art\n\n**Explanation:**  \n- **Rachel** explicitly argues that the freedom from constraints has caused a \"decline in the quality of art,\" directly asserting that contemporary art is inferior to earlier art.  \n- **James**, however, disputes this by claiming that people\u2019s negative perception of contemporary art is a *mistake*. He argues that inferior past artworks have already been forgotten, while contemporary inferior works are still visible, skewing current judgments. Thus, he believes the *actual* quality of contemporary art is not inherently worse than earlier art.  \n\nThis creates a direct disagreement: Rachel affirms that contemporary art is of lower quality (D), while James denies this, attributing the perception to incomplete historical filtering. The other options either align their views (A aligns with Rachel; B aligns with James) or are not explicitly contested (C).  \n\n**Answer:** D",
