      "index": 0,
      "text": " D\n\nWait, let me check again. The question is which statement weakens the argument. The argument is that B. t. is preferable because they are specific and don't harm other insects. The weakness would be something that shows that despite their specificity, there's a problem.\n\nOption D says that insects build up resistance more readily to B. t. than to chemical. If that's true, then even though B. t. is better in specificity, it might become ineffective faster than chemical ones. So the advantage of specificity might be offset by the problem of resistance. That would weaken the argument that B. t. is preferable. So D is a good candidate.\n\nOption B says no particular B.t toxin is effective against all insects. Well, the argument already says the toxins have limited range, so this is just reinforcing that point. Since the argument's advantage is based on their limited activity, this doesn't weaken it. It might even support it.\n\nSo the answer is D. I think that's right. Let me just confirm again.\n\nThe conclusion is that B.t. is preferable because of specificity and not harming other insects. If D is true, then even though they are specific, they might become less effective over time compared to chemicals. So the advantage might be short-lived, making them not as preferable in the long run. That weakens the argument that they are preferable for pest management programs. So yes, D weakens the argument.\n</think>\n\nThe argument states that B. t. toxins are preferable to chemical insecticides because of their specificity, which spares non-target insects and avoids harming mammals or birds. To weaken this, we need a statement that undermines the advantages of B. t. toxins. \n\n**Option D** directly challenges the long-term efficacy of B. t. toxins. If insects develop resistance to B. t. more quickly than to chemical insecticides, the toxins\u2019 effectiveness diminishes faster, reducing their overall benefit. This introduces a critical drawback that weakens the claim that B. t. is \"preferable,\" as resistance could render them less sustainable in pest management programs over time. \n\nOther options either support the argument (A and C) or align with the premise without contradicting it (B). Thus, **D** is the correct answer.\n\n**Answer:** D",
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "stop_reason": null,
      "prompt_logprobs": null
    }
