the correct answer is C.\n\nWait, but let me check again.\n\nThe question asks which statement is consistent with the original text.\n\nOriginal text says: \"China's... development of green tea industry has obvious comparative advantages.\" So that implies that Chinese green tea has competitive strength, so option C is correct.\n\nYes, so the correct answer is C.\n\nBut wait the options are:\n\nC) Chinese green tea has competitive strength in the international market. That's exactly what the text says. So that's correct.\n\nThe other options:\n\nA: Not mentioned. The text doesn't talk about supply vs demand for black tea.\n\nB: The text says their green tea quality is not high. The international price of green tea is higher than black, but India's green tea quality is low. So their price would be lower than the average for green tea, so B is wrong.\n\nD: The context says that the three countries tried to change red to green but failed. So there was an attempt but not a successful trend. The option D says there is a trend of changing red to green. Since they failed, the trend is not there. So D is incorrect.\n\nSo the answer is C.\n</think>\n\nC) Chinese green tea has competitive strength in the international market\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Context Analysis:**\n   - The text states that high-quality green tea is produced in higher latitudes (25\u00b0\u201330\u00b0N), while black tea dominates lower latitudes.\n   - Green tea has a higher international price than black tea.\n   - India, Indonesia, and Sri Lanka (black tea producers) attempted to switch to green tea but failed due to low-quality results.\n   - China\u2019s green tea regions have abundant, low-cost labor, giving it a **\"comparative advantage\"** in the green tea industry.\n\n2. **Option Evaluation:**\n   - **A:** No mention of black tea supply exceeding demand. Irrelevant.\n   - **B:** The text explicitly states India\u2019s green tea quality is not high. Lower quality would likely mean prices below the international average, making this false.\n   - **C:** Directly supported by the text\u2019s emphasis on China\u2019s \"comparative advantages\" (i.e., competitive strength) in green tea production.\n   - **D:** The failed attempts by India, Indonesia, and Sri Lanka to switch to green tea (they \"ended in failure\") contradict a \"trend\" of successful shifts. Thus, this is incorrect.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**  \n   Only **C** aligns with the text\u2019s assertion that China\u2019s green tea industry has strong competitive advantages in the global market. \n\n**Answer:** C",
