      "index": 0,
      "text": " C. The unregulated market lacks moral attributes..\n\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check again. The original text says that when disposing of labor, the system must face the unity of person (body, psychology, morality). So the argument is that labor can't be treated as a regular commodity because it's tied to a person's humanity. The main point is that letting the market be the sole guide (i.e., unregulated) will destroy society. The labor part is an example supporting that. So the gist is A. But option C says \"The unregulated market lacks moral attributes.\" The text mentions that the system must consider the moral aspect of the person, so maybe the problem is that if the market is the only guide, it ignores those moral aspects, hence the market lacks them? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, option B says labor is a special commodity. The text does state that labor cannot be treated like a normal commodity because it's part of a person. But the question is asking for the gist of the entire statement, which starts by saying that letting the market be the sole guide leads to destruction. The labor example is supporting that claim. So the main point would be A. The answer is A?\n\nBut let me check the options again. The question is which is the gist. The first sentence is the thesis, and the rest explains why using labor as an example. So A is the main point. B is a supporting detail. C is maybe another point, but the text doesn't explicitly say the market lacks morals, but rather that when dealing with labor, the system must consider the moral aspects of the person. The problem is that the market doesn't account for that, leading to societal destruction. So the gist is that unregulated markets (since they don't consider these moral aspects) would destroy society. So A is the answer.\n\nWait, but option D says \"Labor cannot be commoditized.\" The text says labor cannot be arbitrarily driven and used as a commodity, but it's called a \"special commodity.\" So it's a commodity but with caveats, not that it can't be commoditized entirely. So D might be incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A. Unregulated markets will destroy society.\n</think>\n\nThe context begins by stating that allowing the market mechanism to be the sole guide of society, its environment, and purchasing power will lead to societal destruction. This is the central argument. Karl Polanyi uses labor as an example of a \"special commodity\" tied to human dignity (body, psychology, morality), emphasizing that treating labor purely as a market commodity ignores its inherent moral and social dimensions. While options B and D address labor's unique status, they serve as supporting details rather than the overarching point. Option C (\"The unregulated market lacks moral attributes\") is implied but not explicitly stated as the main thesis. The primary focus is on the **consequence** of unregulated markets\u2014societal destruction\u2014making **A** the correct answer.\n\n**Answer:** A) Unregulated markets will destroy society.",
