      "text": " Maybe D?\n\nWait, let me read again.\n\nThe question is asking which can be inferred.\n\nOption D: Eating health products may not promote your health.\n\nThe passage says that nucleic acids and other genetic foods in health care products are \"not very helpful to human health.\" So the author is saying they are not very helpful, which implies that they might not be beneficial, or may not promote health. Therefore, D is a possible inference.\n\nOption C: Illness is the result of malnutrition. The passage says that \"what the human body lacks is nutrition, and nucleic acid cannot be lacking.\" Wait, maybe the translation is tricky here. Maybe the original Chinese version says something like the body's deficiency is in nutrition, but not in nucleic acids. So the author is saying that when people have deficiencies, it's nutritional, not nucleic acid. But that doesn't mean that all illnesses are due to malnutrition. So C is too broad.\n\nOption B is about nucleic acid decreasing with age. The passage says that the ad's claim (that ability to make genes weakens when old/sick) is nonsense. So the author is saying that the body's ability to produce nucleic acids doesn't decrease with age/sickness. Therefore, B is false.\n\nOption A: All ads about health products are unscientific. The passage only criticizes specific ads that make claims about nucleic acids. So not all, so A is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D: Eating health products may not promote your health. Because the passage states these products are \"not very helpful,\" implying that they might not contribute positively to health, so they may not promote health. So D is inferred.\n</think>\n\nThe context states that nucleic acids in health products are not very helpful and refutes claims that the body needs exogenous nucleic acids or that aging/sickness reduces gene production capacity. The key inference centers on the ineffectiveness of such health products. \n\n**Analysis of Options:**  \n- **A)** Incorrect. The passage critiques *specific* ads about nucleic acids, not *all* health product ads.  \n- **B)** Incorrect. The passage explicitly dismisses the idea that aging weakens nucleic acid production, calling it \"nonsense.\"  \n- **C)** Incorrect. While the body may lack *nutrition*, the text does not equate all illnesses to malnutrition.  \n- **D)** Correct. The passage asserts that nucleic acid-based health products are \"not very helpful,\" implying they may not benefit health, thus supporting this inference.  \n\n**Answer:** D) Eating health products may not promote your health.",
