h supports the idea that the house's discovery is evidence of that.\n\nHmm. In standardized tests, often the correct answer is the one that directly supports the conclusion that the evidence presented (the ornaments) leads to the conclusion (ceremonial activities). So D would be the best.\n\nWait, but D says \"Only when ceremonial activities are held will the ornaments of processed antlers appear.\" So that means ornaments are only present during ceremonies. Therefore, finding the ornaments means ceremonies happened. That strengthens the archaeologists' point. So D is correct.\n\nAlternatively, if the ornaments could be used for other purposes, then the conclusion is weaker. So D strengthens it.\n\nYes, I think D is the answer. Wait, but the question is in Chinese? The user's instruction is in Chinese, but the problem is in English. The options are A to D.\n\nWait, but the user's instruction is in English, so I can proceed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe B is better. Let me check again:\n\nThe context says the house existed in 8500 BC, 500 years earlier than the previous oldest. The archaeologists' point would be that this indicates people were settled earlier than thought. So B says they had a stable address (i.e., settled) rather than being hunters. So B directly supports that the house is part of a settled community, which is the main point of the discovery's significance. The ornaments are an additional finding, but the main point is the house's age and settlement.\n\nTherefore, B is the best support.\n\nWait, but the question is asking for the best support for the \"above point of view\", which includes both the house and the ornaments. The problem might be structured so that the key conclusion is the house's age and what that implies about settlement. So B is better.\n\nAlternatively, the ornaments' presence is used to argue for ceremonies. The question might have the best support as D.\n\nHmm. I'm a bit torn, but since D directly links the ornaments to ceremonies, w