se states that have only moderately strict environmental regulations have higher growth than those with the least-strict regulations**  \nThis reinforces the gradient correlation between regulation strictness and growth, which aligns with the initial observation rather than explaining it away. It does not provide an alternative cause.\n\n**Conclusion**: **A** is the most logical choice. By identifying education and job training investments as a plausible alternative driver of growth, it explains why the observed correlation does not prove causation. \n\n**Answer: A**",
