on.  \n- **Option B** (struggles in parliament) explicitly involves political entities (parties, classes) competing for influence, aligning with the criteria.  \n- **Option C** (councillors preaching views) involves political actors (councillors) publicly advocating for their political stance, which qualifies as a form of political struggle.  \n\n**Option D**, however, describes a **private economic transaction** between individuals (buyer and seller) over a price. This lacks the **political subjectivity** and **public political power** elements central to the definition. Thus, it is not a political struggle.",
