      "text": "36% reserves but 97% production. The other countries have reserves but no production, so they can't blame China's controls because they aren't using their own resources, so the problem is their own inaction. That weakens the accusation. So D is the best choice.\n\nWait, let me think again. The countries are accusing China of monopolizing, which is bad for others. To refute that, you need to show that China's actions are justified or that the problem is not their control. \n\nOption C says that previously, China was over-exploiting, so now they are controlling to prevent that. That's a valid reason to control, so it's not monopolizing but managing resources properly. That would help refute the accusation. \n\nOption D shows that other countries have reserves but aren't producing. So they could produce their own but choose not to. Therefore, China isn't monopolizing because the resources are available elsewhere (though they aren't using them). So the problem is other countries not developing their own resources, not China's controls. \n\nSo both C and D could work. Let me see the options again.\n\nThe question asks which is \"most appropriate\" to refute. \n\nOption C is about past exploitation, which justifies China's current controls. That's a good point because it shows they're addressing environmental issues, not trying to monopolize.\n\nOption D points out that others have reserves but aren't producing. Thus, their accusation is misplaced because they have their own reserves but aren't using them. So the real issue is other countries' lack of production, not China's control. \n\nWhich is a stronger refutation? The question is about monopolizing. Monopolizing implies restricting supply to control the market. If other countries have reserves but aren't producing, then China's control might not be the main issue. They could develop their own resources. So D shows that China isn't the only one with resources, but others aren't using theirs. Hence, the problem isn't China's monopoly but others' inaction. \n\nAlternatively, C is about why China is controlling, which is a valid reason, so their actions are justified, not monopolizing. \n\nThe question is to refute the accusation that China's actions are monopolistic and destructive. \n\nThe best refutation would show that the accusation is wrong. \n\nD's data shows that other countries have reserves but aren't producing. Hence, if they had produced their own, they wouldn't depend on China. So China's export controls are not monopolizing because other countries could source elsewhere but choose not to. Therefore, the problem is their own failure to produce, not China's control. \n\nC supports China's actions as a necessary correction to past mistakes, which also justifies their control, making it not a monopoly but a regulatory action. \n\nBetween the two, D directly addresses the accusation by showing that the problem isn't China's monopoly, but other countries' lack of production despite having reserves. So D might be stronger. \n\nLooking at the options again:\n\nOption D: In 2009, China's reserves were 36%, US 13%, Russia 19%, Australia 5.4%. But production was 97% from China, others zero. \n\nSo even though others have significant reserves (like US 13%, Russia 19%), they aren't producing. So why are they blaming China? Because China is producing most, but the reserves exist elsewhere. So they could develop their own resources instead of accusing China. Hence, the accusers have resources but aren't using them, so China's controls aren't the real problem. \n\nTherefore, D is the best answer.\n</think>\n\nThe most appropriate statement to refute the accusations is **D**. \n\n**Explanation:**  \nOption D highlights that while China holds 36% of global rare earth reserves, it accounts for 97% of global production. In contrast, countries like the U.S., Russia, and Australia possess significant reserves (13%, 19%, and 5.4%, respectively) but produce none. This shows that **other countries have the reserves but choose not to exploit them**, implying their reliance on China\u2019s production is due to their own inaction rather than China\u2019s \"monopolistic\" actions. By pointing out that these nations could develop their own resources but do not, the statement undermines the accusation of monopolization. It shifts blame to the accusers\u2019 failure to utilize their own reserves, thereby refuting claims of China\u2019s destructive resource control. \n\n**Answer:** D",
