      "text": " The conclusion assumes that Nobel winners actually made the breakthroughs. So the answer would be C.\n\nWait, but the premise says \"because image thinking helps make major scientific breakthroughs\". The \"because\" here is explaining why they use it more. Wait, maybe the structure is: the conclusion is that Nobel Prize winners use image thinking more than ordinary scientists, because image thinking helps make breakthroughs. So the reasoning is that the reason for their using it more is that it leads to breakthroughs. To make that causal connection, the argument must assume that the Nobel winners have indeed achieved those breakthroughs. Otherwise, if their use of image thinking didn't lead to breakthroughs, then the reasoning would be flawed. So the assumption is that their use of image thinking actually contributed to breakthroughs, which requires that they did make breakthroughs. Hence C is the assumption.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the assumption is that the image thinking is the reason for their breakthroughs, so there is a causal link. But the options don't have that. B says they can make creative thinking through image. Maybe that's part of it, but C is more directly necessary.\n\nSo I think the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe conclusion states that Nobel Prize winners use image thinking more because it helps achieve major breakthroughs. This reasoning assumes a connection between their use of image thinking and the breakthroughs themselves. For the argument to hold, it must presuppose that Nobel winners have indeed made such breakthroughs. Without this assumption, the link between image thinking and breakthroughs (the basis for why they use it more) would be ungrounded. \n\n**Option C** directly addresses this necessity: if Nobel winners had not made major breakthroughs, the claim that image thinking facilitates breakthroughs as a reason for their usage would be unsupported. While other options touch on related ideas (e.g., structured reasoning in A or creativity in B), they are not foundational assumptions for the conclusion. **Option D** introduces irrelevant factors (intelligence and effort), which are not mentioned in the argument.\n\n**Answer:** C) Nobel prize winners have made major scientific breakthroughs.",
