    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": " D) Most of the investment in new oil fields in utrania is expected to come from foreign sources.\n\nWait, no. The conclusion is that it's premature to assume higher exports even with new fields. The reason would be a factor that prevents the new production from being exported. If the investment is foreign, maybe the foreign companies are required to sell the oil domestically, or the government imposes export restrictions?Alternatively, perhaps the investment is for domestic consumption. Hmm, maybe I need to reconsider. \n\nWait, maybe D is not the right answer. Let me think again. If the investment is foreign, then the foreign companies might want to sell the oil abroad, so that would increase exports. So that would not make the conclusion valid. So maybe D is not the right answer.\n\nBack to B: If domestic demand increases, then even if production increases, the exports might not, because they are using more. That makes sense. So B would be the correct answer. \n\nBut the context says that economic stagnation had caused the drop in exports, implying that maybe domestic consumption was low before, but now the economy is better, so more people can afford cars, increasing their own oil consumption. So the new production might be used domestically instead of being exported. So B is the best answer.\n\nHmm, I think I made a mistake earlier. So the correct answer is B. Because if domestic demand goes up, the extra production is used there, so exports don't increase. Therefore, the correct option is B.\n</think>\n\nThe argument states that while new oil fields will be rapidly developed due to improved economic conditions and relaxed regulations, it is too early to assume this will lead to higher exports. The missing premise must explain why increased production might not translate to increased exports despite the new fields.\n\n**Option B** is the most logical completion. If the improved economy leads to a surge in car ownership among Utranians, domestic oil consumption would rise significantly. This increased domestic demand could absorb the additional oil produced by new fields, leaving exports unchanged or even reduced. The argument hinges on the idea that higher production does not necessarily mean more exports if domestic usage offsets the gains.\n\nOther options are less relevant:\n- **A** discusses old fields, not new ones, and does not address exports directly.\n- **C** (stable prices) would likely encourage exports rather than hinder them.\n- **D** (foreign investment) does not inherently prevent exports; foreign investors might prioritize exporting the oil, which would counter the conclusion.\n\nThus, **B** directly addresses a factor that would divert new production away from exports and toward domestic use, making it the correct answer.\n\n**Answer: B**",
