      "text": " C or D?\n\nWait, D states that if incumbents are rightly blamed, they shouldn't be returned. But in the first scenario, if all are blamed equally, then each incumbent is \"rightly\" blamed, so they shouldn't be returned, but the context says they are. Hence D contradicts the first scenario. So D is wrong.\n\nTherefore, C is the correct one. Let me check again.\n\nThe principle in C: When scandals are less the responsibility of individual incumbents than of the parties, then the responsible party must be penalized when possible.\n\nIn first case: All parties are blamed equally, so each party is responsible. But since you can't penalize all parties at once (because you have to vote for someone), maybe voters can't penalize any, so they keep incumbents. Alternatively, the principle says \"when possible\". If all parties are responsible, then you can't penalize all, so it's not possible, so perhaps they don't penalize. But the principle says \"must be penalized when possible\". So when possible, i.e., when only one party is responsible, then they do penalize. So the principle holds because when the responsible party is only one, you can penalize them (so their incumbents lose). When it's all parties, you can't penalize all (since you have to vote for someone), so you don't penalize any, so they stay. That works.\n\nOption C's principle would account for the contrast.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe context describes a contrast in voter reactions to political scandals: when all parties are equally blamed, incumbents are re-elected, but when only one party is blamed, its incumbents lose. The correct principle must explain this difference.\n\n**Option C** states that when scandals are attributed to parties rather than individuals, voters penalize the responsible party when possible. This aligns with the scenarios:  \n- **When all parties are blamed equally**, voters cannot penalize all parties simultaneously (as they must still vote for representatives). Thus, incumbents remain in office.  \n- **When only one party is blamed**, voters can penalize that party by defeating its incumbents.  \n\n**Option D** incorrectly suggests that incumbents \"rightly blamed\" should not be re-elected. However, in the first scenario, all incumbents are equally blamed, yet they are re-elected, contradicting D.  \n\n**Options A and B** are irrelevant or unsupported by the context.  \n\n**Answer: C**",
