a typo, but based on their wording:\n\nNegative image is when you see the original object's opposite? Or the original object's color?\n\nWait according to the user's definition:\n\nPositive image: image of the original object color is retained.\n\nNegative image: visual image is colored with the original object after the stimulation stops. Wait, that's the same as positive? That can't be.\n\nWait maybe there's a mistranslation here. Alternatively, perhaps \"negative image\" is when the afterimage is the opposite (complementary) color to the original.\n\nAssuming that the user might have intended that, even if the wording is a bit off.\n\nTherefore, in that case:\n\nOption C says after staring at red, when looking at white, you see green. Green is the complement of red, so that would be a negative afterimage. So C is the correct answer.\n\nOption B is the positive afterimage (seeing red after red).\n\nThus, the answer is C.\n\nWait the options:\n\nC) After staring at red for a long time, the vision moves quickly to white, not white, but green.\n\nThat's the classic afterimage where the complementary color appears. So that's negative image. So the answer is C.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is C, which is option C.\n</think>\n\nC) After staring at red for a long time, the vision moves quickly to white, not white, but green.\n\n### Explanation:\nAccording to the definitions provided:\n- **Positive image** retains the original object's color temporarily after the object is removed (e.g., seeing red after staring at a red object and looking away, as in option B).\n- **Negative image** involves a temporary afterimage in a color *opposite* to the original object's color. \n\nIn option C, after prolonged exposure to red, the visual system fatigues the red-sensitive photoreceptors. When shifting focus to a white surface, the remaining green-sensitive receptors dominate, creating a green afterimage (the complement of red). This matches the description of a *negative image* because it shows an opposing color to the original stimulus. \n\nOther options:  \n- **A** refers to motion perception, unrelated to color afterimages.  \n- **B** is a positive image (retaining red).  \n- **D** describes simultaneous contrast, not afterimage effects.  \n\nThus, **C** is correct.",
