creased despite their alcohol consumption remaining unchanged. Option D resolves this by suggesting a shift in *where* they drink: under-21 individuals now consume less alcohol in bars/restaurants and more at home. Drinking at home reduces the likelihood of driving under the influence compared to leaving bars or restaurants, where drunk driving is more common. Thus, even with the same total consumption, fewer incidents of drunk driving (and thus fewer traffic deaths) occur because the setting of the drinking has changed. Other options either address unrelated demographics (A), broader population trends (B), or fail to directly link drinking habits to reduced driving risks (C).",
