scientific achievements or accurate understanding of science) as a catalyst for innovation. Option D captures this idea, highlighting how their philosophical perspectives aid scientific progress. Other options misinterpret the text:  \n- **A** incorrectly attributes enlightenment to their \"understanding of the nature of science,\" which the passage explicitly calls \"naive and full of mistakes.\"  \n- **B** references \"predecessors' achievements,\" which the sages lacked.  \n- **C** introduces \"sublating,\" a concept not mentioned in the text, whereas the passage stresses inspiration through questioning rather than a dialectical process.  \n\nThus, the purpose is to argue that ancient philosophical thought supports scientific innovation, making **D** the correct choice.",
