c 30 or 31?) Wait, the excluded days are Dec 31 and the leap day (probably Feb 29). So Dec 30 is part of the week. So people with birthdays on Dec 31 would have their birthday on an excluded day every year. Maybe they can't schedule events on those days because they are not part of the weekly structure? Or maybe they have to adjust their plans because those days are not part of the regular weeks. Alternatively, maybe they have to deal with their birthday always being on an excluded day, which might not be part of any week, so their birthday doesn't fall on a weekday, which could cause issues with work or events. So the answer is B.\n\nAlternatively, if the proposal makes the last day of the year (Dec 31) not part of any week, then that day doesn't have a weekday assigned. So, for example, if someone's birthday is on Dec 31, their birthday is not on a Monday to Sunday, so maybe they can't plan events around it as easily. Similarly for leap day. So their anniversaries are on those days which are outside the weekly cycle, so they might have conflicts because their birthday isn't on a regular day. So yes, option B is correct.\n\nOption D: If holidays are fixed on specific days, then three-day breaks can be planned because the holidays are always on the same weekday. So if a holiday is on a Monday every year, then the three-day weekend would be the same every year, so maybe that would reduce conflicts. So D's conflicts would be resolved. Thus, the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe proposal aims to fix the calendar so that each year starts on the same day (e.g., Sunday) by excluding the last day of the year (December 31) and the leap day from weekly cycles. This ensures anniversaries fall on consistent weekdays. However, those with birthdays or anniversaries on December 30 or 31 would face conflicts because:\n\n- **December 31 and the leap day are excluded from weeks**, meaning these days do not align with regular weekdays. Birthdays on these dates would not fall within the structured weekly cycle, complicating scheduling (e.g., planning events or observances on non-weekday days).\n\nOther options are less likely:\n- **A** (religious observances every seventh day): The proposal maintains a consistent weekly cycle, so observances would align annually.\n- **C** (long-term planners): The fixed calendar simplifies long-term scheduling.\n- **D** (three-day breaks): Holidays would always fall on the same weekday, eliminating unpredictability.\n\n**Answer: B) People who have birthdays or other anniversaries on December 30 or 31**",
