\nSet boys_S=25, girls_S=26 (total 51).\n\nTotal boys:20+25=45,.\n\nTotal girls:30+26=56 → boys < girls → no.\n\nTo get boys_total≤ girls_total:.\n\nDon\'t need boys_S +20 >30 + girls_S.\n\nAlso, S_total = boys_S + girls_S >50.\n\nLet me set boys_S=30,.\n\ngirls_S=21 (total 51).\n\nTotal boys:20+30=50,.\n\nTotal girls:30+21=51 → still boys (50) < girls (51).\n\nHmm.Don\'t need boys_S=31,.\n\ngirls_S=20 (total 51).\n\nTotal boys:20+31=51,.\n\nTotal girls:30+20=50 → now boys (51) ≤ girls (50).This works.\n\nIn this case, boys_S=31, girls_LA=30,.\n\nso boys_S (31) ≤ girls_LA (30).So C holds.\n\nAnother example where boys_S is exactly equal to girls_LA:.\n\nSuppose girls_LA=30,.\n\nboys_S=30,.\n\nthen we need to see if possible.\n\nLet’s set LA_total as girls_LA + boys_LA =30 + B_L,.\n\nScience total must be > LA_total.\n\nSuppose boys_S=30,.\n\ngirls_S= G_S,.\n\nso S_total =30 + G_S.\n\nTo have boys_total (B_L +30) ≤ girls_total (30 + G_S).\n\nAlso, S_total must be > LA_total (30 + B_L).\n\nLet me pick B_L=0,.\n\nso LA_total=30,.\n\nso S_total must be at least 31.\n\nSet S_total=31,.\n\nso G_S=1.\n\nTotal boys=0+30=30,.\n\nTotal girls=30 +1=31 → boys < girls → invalid.\n\nTo get boys_total≤31,.\n\nneed 30 + B_L >30 +1 → B_L >1.\n\nLet’s set B_L=2,.\n\nso LA_total=32,.\n\nthus S_total must be at least 33.\n\nSet boys_S=30,.\n\ngirls_S=3 → total S=33.\n\nTotal boys=2+30=32,.\n\nTotal girls=30+3=33 → boys (32) <33 → nope.\n\nNeed boys_total≤33,.\n\nso 2+30=32 <33 → need more.\n\nSet B_L=3,.\n\nLA_total=33,.\n\nso S_total must be at least 34.\n\nSet boys_S=30,.\n\ngirls_S=4,.\n\nTotal boys=3+30=33,.\n\nTotal girls=30+4=34 → still 33 vs34.\n\nHmm, this is a pattern.To get boys_total ≤ girls_total,.\n\nLet’s set B_L=5,.\n\nLA_total=35,.\n\nS_total must be at least36.\n\nSet boys_S=30,.\n\ngirls_S=6,.\n\nTotal boys=5+30=35,.\n\nTotal girls=30+6=36 → still 35 vs36.\n\nContinuing,.\n\nB_L=6,.\n\nLA_total=36,.\n\nS_total=37,.\n\nboys_S=30,.\n\ngirls_S=7,.\n\nTotal boys=6+30=36,.\n\nTotal girls=30+7=37 → 36 vs37.\n\nHmm, it seems that to have boys_S=30 (equal to girls_LA=30), the boys_total can never exceed girls_total unless we increase boys_S beyond 30.\n\nWait, if we set boys_S=31,.\n\nthen let\'s try:.\n\ngirls_LA=30,.\n\nB_L=0,.\n\nLA_total=30,.\n\nS_total=31 (minimum).\n\nSet boys_S=31,.\n\ngirls_S=0,.\n\nTotal boys=0+31=31,.\n\nTotal girls=30+0=30 → now boys (31) ≤ girls (30).\n\nHere, boys_S=31 ≤ girls_LA=30,.\n\nso C holds.\n\nAlternatively, trying to have boys_S=30 (equal to girls_LA=30):.\n\nAs above, it\'s impossible to get boys_total ≤ girls_total without making boys_S exceed girls_LA.\n\nTherefore, in all possible scenarios where the given conditions hold, boys_S must be less than or equal to girls_LA\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
