nTotal boys:10+40=50,\n\nTotal girls:50+21=71 \u2192 boys (50) < girls (71).Doesn\u2019t work.\n\nHmm.\n\nAnother try:\n\ngirls_LA=50,\n\nboys_S=30.\n\nLA_total=50 girls and 30 boys \u2192 LA_total=80.\n\nScience must have >80, say 81.\n\nSet boys_S=30,\n\ngirls_S=51 \u2192 total 81.\n\nTotal boys=30+30=60,\n\nTotal girls=50+51=101 \u2192 boys (60) < girls (101).No.\n\nHmm.\n\nAnother try with higher boys_LA:\n\ngirls_LA=50,\n\nboys_S=30.\n\nLA_total=50 girls and 50 boys \u2192 LA_total=100.\n\nScience must have >100, say 101.\n\nSet boys_S=30,\n\ngirls_S=71 \u2192 total 101.\n\nTotal boys=50+30=80,\n\nTotal girls=50+71=121 \u2192 boys (80)<121.\n\nStill no.\n\nHmm.\n\nPerhaps it's impossible to have boys_S \u2264 girls_LA while still having boys_total > girls_total.\n\nWait let me think of the extreme case where boys_S=0.\n\nSuppose girls_LA=100,\n\nboys_S=0,\n\nLA_total=100 girls and 0 boys (LA_total=100).\n\nScience must have >100 students.\n\nSet science has boys_S=0,\n\ngirls_S=101 \u2192 total 101.\n\nTotal boys=0+0=0 < girls_total=201.\n\nNo good.\n\nAnother example with boys_S=0 but adding boys in LA:\n\ngirls_LA=50,\n\nboys_S=0,\n\nLA_total=50 girls and 100 boys (total 150).\n\nScience must have >150 students.\n\nSet science has 0 boys and 151 girls \u2192 total 151.\n\nTotal boys=100,\n\nTotal girls=50+151=201 \u2192 100 <201.\n\nStill no.\n\nHmm.\n\nWhat if we set boys_LA very high?\n\ngirls_LA=50,\n\nboys_S=40 (\u226450),\n\nLA_total=50 girls + 100 boys (total 150).\n\nScience must have >150 \u2192 say 151.\n\nSet science has 40 boys and 111 girls (total 151).\n\nTotal boys=100+40=140,\n\nTotal girls=50+111=161 \u2192 boys (140) <161.\n\nStill no.\n\nHmm.\n\nWait, maybe it's impossible to have boys_S \u2264 girls_LA and still have boys_total > girls_total.\n\nTherefore, boys_S must be greater than girls_LA.\n\nThus, option D must hold.\n\nSo the correct answer is D.\n\nWait let me confirm with an example where D holds.\n\nSuppose:\n\ngirls_LA=50,\n\nboys_S=51,\n\nLA_total=50 girls +0 boys (LA=50).\n\nScience has 51 boys and 0 girls (total 51).\n\nTotal boys=51,\n\nTotal girls=50 \u2192 satisfies B_total >G_total.\n\nAlso S_total=51>LA_total=50.\n\nHere, D says boys_S (51) > girls_LA (50).Yes.\n\nAnother example:\n\ngirls_LA=20,\n\nboys_S=21,\n\nLA_total=20 girls +5 boys,\n\nScience:21 boys + 1 girl (total 22>LA's 25? Wait LA is 25: 20+5=25,\n\nso science needs to be \u226526.\n\nSet science to 26: boys_S=21, girls_S=5,\n\nTotal boys:5+21=26,\n\nTotal girls:20+5=25 \u2192 boys>girls.\n\nD holds:21>20.\n\nAnother case where girls_LA=0:\n\nSuppose there are no girls in liberal arts.\n\ngirls_LA=0.\n\nThen, option D requires boys_S >0.\n\nSince there must be more boys than girls in the school.\n\nIf girls_LA=0,\n\nthen all girls are in science.\n\nSuppose:\n\nLA has 100 boys (boys_LA=100),\n\ngirls_LA=0.\n\nScience has, say, 50 boys and 49 girls (total 99).\n\nTotal students in science 99 < LA's 100 \u2192 violates S_total > LA_total.\n\nThus, science must be at least 101.\n\nSet science to 101: boys_S=51, girls_S=50.\n\nTotal boys=100+51=151,\n\nTotal girls=0+50=50 \u2192 boys>girls.\n\nD holds: boys_S (51) > girls_LA (0).\n\nYes.\n\nTherefore, in all valid scenarios, boys_S must exceed girls_LA.\n\nTherefore D must be correct.\n\nThus the answer is D.\n</think>\n\nD) The total number of boys in science class is more than that of girls in liberal arts class\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\nGiven the conditions:\n1. Total boys (B) > total girls (G) in the school.\n2. Total students in science classes (S_total) > total students in liberal arts classes (LA_total).\n\n**Analysis of Options:**\n\n- **Option A (Boys in LA > Girls in LA):**  \n  Not necessarily true. If all boys are in science and all girls in liberal arts (e.g., LA has 0 boys and 100 girls, science has 101 boys), boys_LA = 0 < girls_LA = 100. Thus, A can be false.\n\n- **Option B (Boys in Science > Girls in Science):**  \n  Not necessarily true. Suppose science has 50 boys and 50 girls (total 100), and liberal arts has 50 boys (total LA = 50). Total boys (100) > girls (50), but boys_S = girls_S. Thus, B can be false.\n\n- **Option C (Boys in LA > Girls in Science):**  \n  Not necessarily true. If LA has 0 boys and 50 girls (total LA = 50), science has 70 boys and 30 girls (total S = 100). Total boys (70) > girls (80), but boys_LA (0) < girls_S (30). Thus, C can be false.\n\n- **Option D (Boys in Science > Girls in LA):**  \n  **Must be true.**  \n  Assume the opposite: boys_S \u2264 girls_LA.  \n  Then, total boys = boys_S + boys_LA \u2264 girls_LA + boys_LA.  \n  Total girls = girls_LA + girls_S.  \n  To satisfy B > G:  \n  boys_S + boys_LA > girls_LA + girls_S  \n  \u21d2 (boys_S - girls_LA) + (boys_LA - girls_S) > 0.  \n  Since boys_S \u2264 girls_LA, the first term is \u22640.  \n  The second term (boys_LA - girls_S) must compensate for this deficit.  \n  However, given S_total > LA_total:  \n  boys_S + girls_S > boys_LA + girls_LA.  \n  Rearranged: (boys_S - boys_LA) > (girls_LA - girls_S).  \n  Combining these inequalities shows contradictions unless boys_S > girls_LA.  \n  Thus, boys_S must exceed girls_LA for B > G to hold.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Only **D** must be true.  \n\n**Answer:** D",
