ditional: \"if this research is applied... then pesticides can be reduced\". The conclusion's validity as a logical argument requires that if the research is applied (i.e., the transgenic plants are used, which would have B's property), then pesticides can be reduced. The \"if\" part includes applying the research, which would presumably include the plants having the property in B. So perhaps B is already part of the \"applied\" condition. Therefore, the missing premise might be A. Wait, no. Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, the conclusion is that applying this method (the gene insertion) to vegetables would reduce pesticide use. The argument's logic is that the method works in Arabidopsis, so it should work in vegetables, leading to less pest damage, thus less need for pesticides. The unstated premise is that the vegetables will actually exhibit the same response (B), so B must be added. Additionally, that pesticide use is for pests (A). But since the question asks for the premise that \"must be added\", perhaps the answer is B. Because the argument's main gap is that the experiment was on Arabidopsis, so applying to vegetables requires that the same effect occurs there. The other assumption (A) might be considered part of the common knowledge or not as critical. Alternatively, maybe A is not required because the conclusion's \"greatly reduced\" could mean that even a partial reduction would be considered \"great\" if the method is effective, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the answer is B. The question is from logical reasoning, so the key missing link is that the transgenic vegetables will actually have the enhanced fragrance when injured. Otherwise, the conclusion doesn't hold. So B is the necessary premise. A is more about the current pesticide usage, but the argument's conclusion is about the potential of the method, not about current usage. The conclusion could still hold even if pesticides are used for other reasons, as long as the method reduces pest-related pesticide use sufficiently to make the total reduction \"great\". But if pesticides are not mainly used for pests, then the overall reduction might not be great, so the conclusion's claim of \"greatly reduced\" would require that pests are a major target of pesticides. Thus, A is also necessary.\n\nBut since the options are separate and the question asks \"must be added\", perhaps the answer is B. Because without B, the conclusion is invalid. Without A, maybe the conclusion is still possible but not necessarily \"greatly\". Since the question is about the conclusion's validity, which requires that the conclusion must follow. If the conclusion states that pesticide use can be \"greatly\" reduced, then assuming that pesticides are mainly used for pests (A) is necessary. Otherwise, even if the method works, the reduction might not be great.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Let me see the options again:\n\nThe options are:\n\nA) Current pesticides mainly for pests.\n\nB) Transgenic veggies' fragrance increases when injured.\n\nC) Green fragrance is a defense weapon (not necessary for the argument's logic).\n\nD) Legal permission (not about validity, just implementation).\n\nThe conclusion's validity requires B, because the experiment was on Arabidopsis, but the conclusion is about vegetables in general. The argument assumes that when this gene is applied to vegetables, they will have the same response (enhanced fragrance upon injury). If that's not true, the conclusion fails. So B is necessary.\n\nA is also a premise that might be needed, but perhaps the argument's structure is such that even if pesticides are used for other things, reducing pests would still reduce pesticide use somewhat. The conclusion says \"can be greatly reduced\"\u2014so the \"greatly\" part requires that pesticides are mainly for pests. Therefore, A is also needed. However, in logical reasoning questions like this, sometimes the key missing link is the one that directly connects the experiment to the real-world application, which would be B. The question is asking for the premise that must be added. If you have to choose between A and B, I think B is more directly necessary for the conclusion to hold, because without B, the method doesn't work. Without A, maybe the reduction is still possible, just not as much, but the conclusion says \"can be greatly reduced\"\u2014so \"greatly\" depends on A. But the question is about the conclusion being valid. If the conclusion is \"can be greatly reduced\", then the premise that pesticides are mainly for pests (A) is necessary to make that \"greatly\" part valid. Without A, the conclusion might only support \"reduced\", not \"greatly\".\n\nTherefore, both A and B are needed? But the options don't allow multiple answers. The question must have one correct answer.\n\nAlternatively, let me think of it as the conclusion is based on the mechanism working in vegetables (B) and that pesticides are used for pests (A). Since the problem is about a logical premise to be added, perhaps B is the direct assumption needed for the mechanism to work, while A is more of an external condition. Since the question is about making the researcher's conclusion valid (i.e., ensuring that the logic holds), the key missing link is B. The argument states that the gene was introduced into Arabidopsis and worked there. The conclusion is about applying it to vegetables. So the assumption is that the transgenic vegetables will also exhibit the same response (B). Therefore, B is the answer.\n\nI think I'll go with B as the necessary premise.\n</think>\n\nThe researcher's conclusion hinges on the assumption that the transgenic vegetables will exhibit the same response as Arabidopsis when injured, i.e., enhanced fragrance attracting parasitoids to reduce pests. Without this assumption, the mechanism's effectiveness in vegetables cannot be guaranteed. While Option A addresses pesticide usage reasons, the conclusion's validity primarily depends 