      "text": " C is the contrapositive, so correct.\n\nWait, but let me parse option B again:\n\n\"Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.\" The structure here is \"Truth cannot be shut out (\u00acQ) cannot happen unless P (closing doors).\" So \"unless P, you can't have \u00acQ\"? Or \"unless you do P, truth can't be shut out\"? Maybe I'm getting confused.\n\nAlternatively, \"Truth can be shut out only if you close all wrong doors.\" Which would mean Q \u2192 P, which is converse. But the original is P \u2192 Q.\n\nAlternatively, \"Truth cannot be shut out unless all doors are closed\" could be interpreted as \"To prevent truth from being shut out (i.e., have \u00acQ), you must close all the wrong doors (P).\" So \u00acQ requires P. Which would be \u00acQ \u2192 P. Which is equivalent to \u00acP \u2192 Q, which is the same as the original P \u2192 Q? Wait, no. Wait, \u00acQ \u2192 P is equivalent to \u00acP \u2192 Q. Because the contrapositive of \u00acQ \u2192 P is \u00acP \u2192 Q. Which is exactly the original statement P \u2192 Q's contrapositive? Wait, no.\n\nWait, original is P \u2192 Q. The contrapositive is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP. So if \u00acQ \u2192 P were true, that would be different. Hmm, perhaps I'm overcomplicating.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of the options:\n\nOriginal: If P then Q.\n\nOption C: If not Q, then not P. Which is contrapositive, so equivalent. So C is correct.\n\nOption A: If Q then P (converse). Not equivalent.\n\nOption B: If not P, then not Q? Which would be equivalent to P or not Q, which is the same as original.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me clarify:\n\nOriginal: P \u2192 Q is equivalent to \u00acP \u2228 Q.\n\nOption B: \"Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.\" \"Unless P\" means \"if not P\". So \"Truth can't be shut out (\u00acQ) unless P\" \u2192 So \"\u00acQ can only be true if P is done?\" Or \"If you don't do P (close doors), then truth can't be shut out?\" Wait, maybe that is \"If not P, then \u00acQ is not possible?\" Hmm, perhaps it's more precise to parse it as \"Truth cannot be shut out (\u00acQ) unless P\" \u2192 which would mean \"If you want \u00acQ, then you must have P\". So \u00acQ \u2192 P. Which is equivalent to \u00acP \u2192 Q.\n\nWait, the original is P \u2192 Q. So \u00acP \u2192 Q would not be equivalent. Because P \u2192 Q allows for Q to be true even if P is false. But \u00acP \u2192 Q would mean that whenever P is false, Q is true, which is a stronger statement.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"Truth cannot be shut out unless all doors are closed\" is saying that the only way for truth to be shut out is if you close all wrong doors. Which would be Q \u2192 P. Which is the converse.\n\nHmm, this is getting confusing. Let's think of examples.\n\nSuppose \"If it rains (P), the ground gets wet (Q).\" The contrapositive is \"If the ground isn't wet, it didn't rain.\" Which is equivalent.\n\nSimilarly, the original statement is \"If you close all wrong doors (P), then truth is shut out (Q).\" So contrapositive is \"If truth is not shut out (\u00acQ), then you didn't close all wrong doors (\u00acP).\" Which is exactly option C.\n\nTherefore, option C is the correct answer. The other options are not equivalent. Option B is tricky but seems to involve a converse or something else.\n\nWait, but let me check option D again: \"To accept the truth, we must reject wrong.\" The original is about closing doors leading to truth being excluded, so accepting truth would require not closing those doors? Maybe that's a possible interpretation, but it's more of a paraphrase rather than a logical equivalent. Since the question asks for the closest in meaning, which would be the contrapositive, so C is better.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe original statement is \"If you close all the wrong doors (P), truth will be shut out (Q),\" which is logically represented as **P \u2192 Q**. To determine the closest match among the options, we analyze their logical implications:\n\n- **Option A** (\"If truth is shut out, then you closed all wrong doors\") translates to **Q \u2192 P**, the converse of the original. This is not equivalent.\n- **Option B** (\"Truth cannot be shut out unless all wrong doors are closed\") implies **\u00acQ \u2192 P** (to prevent truth from being shut out, you must close all wrong doors). This is not equivalent to **P \u2192 Q**.\n- **Option C** (\"If truth is not shut out, then all wrong doors were not closed\") translates to **\u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP**, which is the **contrapositive** of the original statement. The contrapositive is logically equivalent to **P \u2192 Q**, making this the correct answer.\n- **Option D** is a broader philosophical statement and not a direct logical translation.\n\nThus, the closest match is **C**, as it preserves the original implication through its contrapositive.\n\n**Answer:** C",
