be with their product, not the air.\n\nAlternatively, if the problem is the air, then all ACs in the area would have the same problem. If other ACs work fine, then the engineer's argument is wrong.\n\nBut none of the options mention other brands. Option C is a general statement. Hmm.\n\nOption D says all ACs should be guaranteed to work in the area before being sold. If that's true, then the ACs should have been tested and designed to handle the local air quality, so the engineer's excuse of bad air is invalid because they should have made them to work there. So D would refute the engineer's argument by implying that the company failed to meet the required standards for the area's conditions. So D could be a good answer.\n\nWait, but the wording of D is \"should be guaranteed to work properly in the area before they can be sold.\" So if that's true, then the company's ACs were sold without meeting that requirement, meaning they should not be blamed for poor performance because they didn't meet the standard. Wait, no, actually. If the requirement is that all ACs must be guaranteed to work properly in the area before being sold, then the company's ACs would have failed that requirement. So the fact that they are sold in the area would mean they must have met that requirement. But if they are not working, then the problem is the company's fault. Because they should have been guaranteed to work, but they didn't. So the engineer can't blame the air because the ACs should have been designed to handle it. So D would refute the engineer's argument by saying that the company's ACs were supposed to work in the local area, so the problem must be their fault.\n\nAh, that's a better analysis. So D would be a good refutation.\n\nWait, but I need to make sure. Let me go through again.\n\nThe engineer says the problem is the air, not the ACs. If D is true (that ACs must be guaranteed to work in the local area before being sold), then the ACs should have been tested and certified to work despite the local air. Therefore, the engineer can't blame the air because the ACs were supposed to handle that. So the problem must be with the ACs themselves, thus refuting the engineer.\n\nSo D is a good refutation.\n\nAlternatively, B: If the brand has not had many complaints elsewhere, that suggests that the problem is specific to this area. Which could mean it is the air quality here. So B might support the engineer.\n\nWait, but if the brand has no complaints elsewhere, maybe the problem is indeed the local air. So B doesn't refute, it supports.\n\nSo back to A: if in areas with good air their ACs work well, then that supports the engineer's claim that air quality is the issue. So A would support, not refute.\n\nC says not all ACs are affected by air quality. So if that's true, maybe the brand's ACs are affected, so the problem is their own quality. Wait, but the engineer is blaming air quality. If some ACs are not affected, then the brand's ACs could be among those affected, meaning that the issue is their design. So the engineer is wrong because other ACs aren't affected by the same air, so the problem is the brand's ACs. But the option doesn't say that the brand's ACs are affected. It just says \"not all\" so there exist some that aren't. So if there exist ACs that aren't affected, then the engineer's excuse is invalid because the brand's ACs could be the ones that are affected, so it's their fault. Therefore, C would refute the engineer by showing that the problem isn't inherent to all ACs, so it must be this brand's fault.\n\nAlternatively, if the engineer is saying that air quality is the issue, then if some ACs are not affected by air quality (as C says), then in this area, those unaffected ACs would work fine. Therefore, the problem here must be the brand's ACs, since their ACs are affected by the bad air, but others aren't. Hence, the engineer's defense is invalid because proper ACs don't have this issue.\n\nSo C is also a refutation.\n\nNow I'm confused between C and D.\n\nWait, let me think again. The question is which would most effectively refute the engineer's argument.\n\nThe engineer's argument is that the ACs are not working because of the local air quality. To refute that, we need to show that the air quality isn't the cause.\n\nOption C says that not all ACs are affected by air quality. Therefore, there exist some ACs that can work even with bad air. If that's true, then the problem with the brand's ACs is not the air, but their own design. Thus, the engineer's excuse is invalid.\n\nOption D says that all ACs should be guaranteed to work in the area before being sold. So if that's true, the ACs should have been designed to handle the local air. Therefore, the fact that they're not working here means they failed to meet the required standard, so it's the company's fault, not the air. So D also refutes.\n\nSo both C and D could be contenders. Need to see which is better.\n\nThe question is which \"most effectively\" refutes.\n\nOption D attacks the requirement: the AC should have been designed for the local conditions. Therefore, if they didn't, then the problem is theirs, not the air. So it's a direct refutation by saying that the air shouldn't be an issue because they should have accounted for it.\n\nOption C points out that some ACs aren't affected, implying that this brand's is, so it's their fault. But it requires the assumption that other ACs in the area are working fine. But the option doesn't state that. It just says \"not all\" are affected, which could mean that in some places, but not necessarily in this area. So maybe it's weaker.\n\nAlternatively, if \"not all\" means that in the same area, other ACs are working, then that's a strong refutation. But the option doesn't specify that.\n\nTherefore, D might be a better answer because it's a general requirement that directly addresses the engineer's point. The engineer is saying it's the air, but D states that they should have been made to