      "text": " D.\n</think>\n\nThe argument uses Somalia's rising per capita GDP to claim its people's living standards are comparable to other African nations. However, it relies solely on **one economic indicator**\u2014per capita GDP\u2014while ignoring other critical factors that define living standards, such as income inequality, access to healthcare, education, security, and basic infrastructure. \n\n**Option D** directly addresses this flaw by stating that judging living standards based on a single index (GDP per capita) is unreliable. While Option A and B highlight potential issues with wealth distribution or the source of GDP growth, they are narrower critiques. Option D, however, strikes at the core of the argument\u2019s methodology, emphasizing the overreliance on an incomplete metric. Option C, meanwhile, introduces an unsupported causal claim about \"economic freedom,\" which is not central to the argument\u2019s reasoning. \n\nThe most serious flaw is the **reliance on a single index**, making **D** the correct answer.\n\n**Answer: D**",
