hat typhoons derive their energy from sunlight heating the ocean surface. If this were not true, blocking sunlight via increased cloud reflection would be ineffective. \n\n**Key analysis of options:**  \n- **A** discusses droplet regrouping, which is tangential to the energy mechanism.  \n- **B** focuses on rainfall in adjacent areas, which is irrelevant to the core argument.  \n- **C** describes typhoon characteristics (e.g., wind/rainstorms) but does not address the energy source.  \n- **D** directly states that the typhoon\u2019s driving force comes from solar-heated seawater, which is essential for the proposed method to work.  \n\n**Answer:** D) Driving force of typhoon comes from the heat generated by sunlight on the surface of sea water.",
