messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nZhao Ming, Qian Hong and Sun Jie were admitted to Peking University, Tsinghua University and Beijing normal University. About which school they were admitted to, the students made the following guesses: classmate A guessed: Zhao Ming was admitted by Tsinghua University and Sun Jie was admitted by Beijing normal University. Classmate B guess: Zhao Ming was admitted by Beijing normal University and Qian Hong was admitted by Tsinghua University. Classmate C guess: Zhao Ming was admitted to Peking University and Sun Jie was admitted to Tsinghua University. As a result, the students' guesses were half right.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options is their correct admission?\n# Options:\nA) Zhao ming, qian hong and sun jie were admitted to peking university, tsinghua university and beijing normal university respectively.\nB) Zhao ming, qian hong and sun jie were admitted to tsinghua university, beijing normal university and peking university respectively.\nC) Zhao ming, qian hong and sun jie were admitted to beijing normal university, tsinghua university and peking university respectively.\nD) Zhao ming, qian hong and sun jie were admitted to peking university, beijing normal university and tsinghua university respectively.", '<think>\nOkay, let\'s see.I need to solve this logical reasoning problem.Alright, so the problem is about three people: Zhao Ming, Qian Hong, and Sun Jie, who got into three different universities: Peking University, Tsinghua University, and Beijing Normal University.There are three classmates (A, B, C) each made two guesses about where each person went.The key point is that each student\'s guesses are "half right," meaning for each of A, B, and C, exactly one of their two statements is correct, and the other is wrong..\n\nThe question is to figure out which of the four options (A to D) correctly lists where each person was admitted.Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out the guesses each classmate made:.\n\n- **Classmate A\'s guess:**.\n  1.Zhao Ming was admitted by Tsinghua University.\n  2.Sun Jie was admitted by Beijing Normal University.\n\n- **Classmate B\'s guess:**.\n  1.Zhao Ming was admitted by Beijing Normal University.\n  2.Qian Hong was admitted by Tsinghua University.\n\n- **Classmate C\'s guess:**.\n  1.Zhao Ming was admitted to Peking University.\n  2.Sun Jie was admitted to Tsinghua University.\n\nEach of these three classmates has exactly one correct and one incorrect statement in their guess..\n\nSo, the goal is to find the correct assignment of universities to each person such that all three classmates\' statements satisfy the "half right" condition.\n\nLet me consider each option one by one and check if they satisfy the conditions.Alternatively, maybe it\'s better to deduce step by step.Let\'s try both approaches.\n\nStarting with the options:.\n\nOption A: .\nZhao Ming - Peking University.\nQian Hong - Tsinghua University.\nSun Jie - Beijing Normal University.\n\nCheck each classmate\'s statements under this scenario.\n\n**Classmate A\'s statements:**.\n1.Zhao Ming at Tsinghua?No, he\'s at Peking.So that\'s wrong.\n2.Sun Jie at Beijing Normal?Yes, that\'s correct.So A has one right, one wrong.Good.\n\n**Classmate B\'s statements:**.\n1.Zhao Ming at Beijing Normal?No, he\'s at Peking.Wrong.\n2.Qian Hong at Tsinghua?Yes, that\'s correct.So B also has one right, one wrong.Good.\n\n**Classmate C\'s statements:**.\n1.Zhao Ming at Peking?Yes, correct.\n2.Sun Jie at Tsinghua?No, she\'s at Beijing Normal.So that\'s wrong.So C has one right, one wrong.Perfect.\n\nHmm, so Option A seems to satisfy all conditions.Wait, but let\'s check the other options just to be sure.\n\nOption B:.\nZhao Ming - Tsinghua.\nQian Hong - Beijing Normal.\nSun Jie - Peking.\n\nCheck each classmate:.\n\nA\'s statements:.\n1.Zhao at Tsinghua: Yes.Correct.\n2.Sun at Beijing Normal?No, she\'s at Peking.So first is correct, second wrong.That\'s okay (half right)..\n\nB\'s statements:.\n1.Zhao at Beijing Normal?No, he\'s at Tsinghua.Wrong.\n2.Qian at Tsinghua?No, she\'s at Beijing Normal.So both wrong.Wait, that\'s a problem because B should have exactly one right.So this violates the condition.So Option B is invalid.\n\nOption C:.\nZhao Ming - Beijing Normal.\nQian Hong - Tsinghua.\nSun Jie - Peking.\n\nCheck:.\n\nA\'s statements:.\n1.Zhao at Tsinghua?No, he\'s at Beijing Normal.Wrong.\n2.Sun at Beijing Normal?No, she\'s at Peking.So both wrong.That\'s bad for A.So Option C is invalid.\n\nOption D:.\nZhao Ming - Peking.\nQian Hong - Beijing Normal.\nSun Jie - Tsinghua.\n\nCheck:.\n\nA\'s statements:.\n1.Zhao at Tsinghua?No..\n2.Sun at Beijing Normal?No, she\'s at Tsinghua.Both wrong.So A has none correct.Not allowed.So D is out.\n\nWait, but let me confirm again.Let me go through each option again carefully.\n\nWait, in Option A, all three conditions are met.But let me check again for Option D in case I made a mistake.\n\nOption D:.\nZhao: Peking.\nQian: Beijing Normal.\nSun: Tsinghua.\n\nA\'s guess:.\n1.Zhao at Tsinghua?No (correct is Peking).So wrong.\n2.Sun at Beijing Normal?No, she\'s at Tsinghua.So both wrong.So A would have zero correct, which violates the condition.So D is invalid.\n\nOption B\'s second check:.\n\nIn Option B:.\n\nZhao: Tsinghua.\nQian: Beijing Normal.\nSun: Peking.\n\nCheck B\'s statements:.\n\nB\'s first guess: Zhao at Beijing Normal?No.Second: Qian at Tsinghua?No, she\'s at Beijing Normal.So both wrong.So B has zero correct.So B is invalid.\n\nSo Option A is the only one that works.Wait, but let me check again with Option A\'s details.\n\nWait, Option A:.\n\nZhao: Peking.\n\nQian: Tsinghua.\n\nSun: Beijing Normal.\n\nSo, let\'s check each classmate again:.\n\nA\'s statements:.\n1.Zhao at Tsinghua?No.(wrong).\n2.Sun at Beijing Normal?Yes.(correct).So one right.\n\nB\'s statements:.\n1.Zhao at Beijing Normal?No.(wrong).\n2.Qian at Tsinghua?Yes.(correct).So one right.\n\nC\'s statements:.\n1.Zhao at Peking?Yes.(correct).\n2.Sun at Tsinghua?No.(wrong).So one right each..\n\nYes, all three have exactly one correct guess.So Option A is valid..\n\nWait, but let me think again.The problem states that each student\'s guesses were "half right".Since each made two statements, exactly one is correct and one wrong.So in Option A, that holds for all..\n\nTherefore, the answer must be A. .\n\nBut wait, I want to confirm once more because sometimes there might be an oversight.Let me try another approach, perhaps by deducing step by step without looking at the options first.\n\nLet me consider the three people: Zhao, Qian, Sun.The three universities: PKU (Peking), THU (Tsinghua), BNU (Beijing Normal).Each person goes to one, no overlaps.\n\nWe need to assign each to a university such that for each of A, B, C, exactly one of their two statements is correct.\n\nLet me denote:.\n\nLet me represent each person\'s university as variables:.\n\nZhao: Z.\n\nQian: Q.\n\nSun: S.\n\nEach of Z, Q, S must be assigned to PKU, THU, BNU, all distinct.\n\nNow, let\'s look at the statements:.\n\nA\'s statements:.\n1.Z = THU.\n2.Each of A, B, C has no true statement.\n\nA says Z is BNU.\n\nB says Z is THU.\n\nC says Z is PKU.\n\nSo all three statements about Zhao are the same. Since each of A, B, C must have no correct statement, then none of these three must be true. Wait, but each of A, B, C\'s statements about Zhao are part of their two statements. Let\'s see.\n\nWait, actually, the statements from A, B, C about Zhao are part of their own two statements. So for example, in A\'s case, if Z is actually BNU, then A\'s first statement is correct, so the second must be correct. Alternatively, if Z is not BNU, then the first is correct, so the second must be correct.\n\nSimilarly for B and C.\n\nLet me consider impossible assignments for Zhao first.\n\nCase 1: Suppose Zhao is at PKU (as in Option A). Then: C\'s first statement is correct (Z=PKU), so C\'s second statement (S=THU) must be correct. Therefore, S is at THU. A\'s first statement (Z=BNU) is correct, so A\'s second statement (S=THU) must be correct. So S=THU. B\'s first statement (Z=THU) is correct, so B\'s second statement (Q=BNU) must be correct. So Q=BNU. Therefore, assignments would be: Z=PKU, Q=BNU, S=THU. Which is not exactly Option A. Let\'s check if this holds.\n\nThen: Check all statements: A\'s statements: Z=BNU (correct), S=THU (correct). Good. B\'s statements: Z=THU (correct), Q=BNU (correct). Good. C\'s statements: Z=PKU (correct), S=THU (correct). Good. Perfect. So this is invalid.\n\nCase 2: Suppose Zhao is at THU (as in Option B). Let\'s see.\n\nThen: A\'s first statement (Z=THU) is correct, so A\'s second (S=THU) must be correct. Therefore, S is at THU. B\'s first statement (Z=THU) is correct, so B\'s second (Q=THU) must be correct. So Q is at THU. C\'s first statement (Z=PKU) is correct, so C\'s second (S=THU) must be correct. So S is at THU. Therefore, the correct answer must be Option B.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer must be Option B.\n\nWait, but let me check again the other options to be thorough. For instance, in Option D: Zhao at PKU, Qian at BNU, Sun at THU.\n\nWait, in that case, let\'s check C\'s statements:.\n\nC\'s second statement is S=THU, which would be correct. But since Zhao is at PKU, C\'s first statement is also correct. So C would have both correct, which satisfies the condition. Therefore, Option D is valid.\n\nSimilarly, in Option C: Zhao at BNU, Qian at THU, Sun at PKU.\n\nCheck B\'s statements:.\n\nB\'s first statement (Z=THU) correct, so second (Q=THU) must be correct. But Q is at THU, so that would mean B\'s second is correct, so both are correct. Which is valid. So Option C is in. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
