      "text": " C.\n\nWait but let me confirm again.\n\nThe argument's conclusion is that imposing the fee at salvage time is better because it makes consumers keep appliances longer to avoid the fee. So the assumption is that the fee at the time of disposal successfully deters disposal, leading to less waste.\n\nThe weakener would be if that fee at salvage time doesn't actually lead to less waste for some reason. C says that people might just dispose improperly, so even though they don't get the fee, the waste isn't reduced. So the fee at salvage time leads to more improper disposal, which is worse than proper disposal with salvage. So total waste might be the same or worse.\n\nHence C weakens the argument.\n\nYes, so the answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe argument concludes that imposing a fee at the time of salvage (discarding appliances) would reduce waste more effectively than charging it upfront because consumers would delay discarding appliances to avoid the fee. To weaken this, we need a reason why the salvage fee might not achieve the intended effect.\n\n**Option C** states that increasing the cost of proper disposal (via the salvage fee) could incentivize improper disposal (e.g., illegal dumping). If consumers avoid the fee by disposing of appliances improperly, this undermines the goal of reducing waste, as improper disposal does not salvage materials and may even increase environmental harm. This directly weakens the argument by showing that the fee\u2019s deterrent effect could lead to worse outcomes than the original plan.\n\nOther options are less relevant:  \n- **A** discusses repair costs but does not address the fee\u2019s timing or consumer behavior.  \n- **B** focuses on manufacturer incentives, which are irrelevant to the argument about consumer behavior.  \n- **D**\u2019s timing of fee payments does not negate the core logic that a salvage fee delays disposal.  \n\nThus, **C** most seriously weakens the argument.  \n\n**Answer: C**",
