ut real treatment's efficacy being affected by belief, which is a different scenario. The explanation is about why fake treatments work, but C is about real treatments not working because of lack of belief. So does that support the explanation?\n\nThe explanation's mechanism is about expectations affecting the body's physiology. Whether the treatment is real or fake, if expectations influence the outcome, then it still supports the mechanism. So C does support the explanation.\n\nHmm. This is really tricky. Maybe I need to consider that the question is looking for an option that contradicts the explanation. Let me check each option again:\n\nA) The effect is due to many factors: doesn't contradict, so supports.\n\nB) Alzheimer's patients (can't anticipate) have no placebo effect: supports.\n\nC) Real treatment worsens when no belief: supports.\n\nD) Placebo causes endorphins via expectation: supports.\n\nSo all support except A? No, all support. But the question says one does not support. Therefore, I must have made a mistake.\n\nWait, maybe the answer is A. Because the explanation given is a specific one (expectation leading to brain changes). A says it's multiple factors, which doesn't support that explanation specifically. The question is which does NOT support the explanation, so A's statement doesn't support the explanation (as it's only one of many), so it's the answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is C. Let me read again: \"Some patients do not believe that the treatment will be effective, although the normal treatment, but their condition further worsened.\" \n\nWait, maybe the wording is confusing. \"although the normal treatment\"\u2014does that mean even though they received normal treatment, their condition worsened? If they received normal treatment but didn't believe it would work, their condition worsened. This shows that expectations (or lack thereof) can have a negative effect, which aligns with the explanation's mechanism. So it supports.\n\n