 first part says that people have proposed that adding MSG is harmful.The second part says research shows benefits.The question is which of the options is correct according to the materials..\n\nOption A is directly stated as a proposal, so it is a correct statement based on the materials.Option B is also part of the proposal (the "even cancer or other visceral diseases").So both A and B are mentioned in the materials as being part of the proposal.But maybe the options are structured so that only one is correct.Let\'s see the exact wording.\n\nThe context says: "Eating too much monosodium glutamate will lead to hair loss, vision loss, zinc deficiency, and even cancer or other visceral diseases."The wording here uses "will lead to," which is a stronger statement than "may cause."But the question is whether the options are accurate.\n\nOption B says "may cause cancer or other visceral diseases."The context states that eating too much "will lead to.. even cancer.." So "will lead to" is stronger than "may cause," but the option is still a correct statement because it\'s less strong.The materials include that possibility, so B is correct.\n\nBut the options might have A and B both correct, but in the context, the question is which is correct according to the materials.However, in the given options, perhaps the correct answer is C, but there\'s a mistranslation.Wait, let me check option C again.The user\'s option C is written as "improve people\'s food and martial arts," which must be a mistranslation.The original probably says "enhance appetite and improve the body\'s ability to absorb other things, and have a tonic effect."So maybe in the options, C should be "enhance people\'s appetite, improve the ability to absorb, and have a tonic effect."If that\'s the case, then C is correct.But in the user\'s text, the option C is written as "improve people\'s food and martial arts," which is wrong.Maybe "martial arts" is a mistranslation of "absorption" or "assimilation."If so, 