      "text": " C is correct?\n\nWait wait, wait. Wait, the problem states that the mice were exposed to the same dose and intensity of radiation. I misread that before. So all mice had the same radiation exposure. Then the difference was the diet. So the radiation was same for all.\n\nAh, so then the setup is: All 200 mice got the same radiation. Then split into two groups: one group could eat normally (unrestricted), and the other was restricted. The unrestricted group had 55 cases of leukemia, while the restricted had 3. So the difference in leukemia is due to the diet.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that if you control the food (restrict it), the mice are less likely to develop leukemia from that radiation exposure. So option C says exactly that. So C is correct.\n\nWait but 55 vs 3? Wait, the unrestricted group had more leukemia? Wait, that's the opposite. Wait in the problem statement: \"55 mice in the former group had leukemia, while only 3 mice in the latter group had leukemia.\"\n\nWait former group is the unrestricted. So unrestricted had 55, restricted had 3. That means that when they ate more food, they had more leukemia. So restricting the food led to fewer cases. Thus, restricting the food made them less likely to get leukemia from the radiation. So option C says controlling the food amount (so restricting) makes them less likely. So C is correct.\n\nBut option A says \"whether exposed to radiation or not, letting them eat normally increases likelihood\". But the problem is all were exposed, so the study can't address the \"not exposed\" scenario. Thus A is incorrect because it's making a claim about mice not exposed, which the study didn't test.\n\nOption B says leukemia has nothing to do with radiation. But in this experiment, since all had radiation, you can't say whether radiation is a factor. Because maybe without radiation, even with unrestricted food, they wouldn't get leukemia. But since all got radiation, the study can't say whether radiation is a factor. But the question is about the options given. The problem says they were all exposed. So B's statement says for any kind of mouse, leukemia has nothing to do with radiation. But since the experiment shows that when exposed, even with restricted food, there were 3 cases, so maybe radiation is a factor. But without comparing to unexposed, can't be sure. So B is not necessarily correct.\n\nOption D says \"mice exposed to some kind of radiation are bound to develop leukemia in the end\". But in the restricted group, only 3 out of 100 got it. So they didn't all develop it. So D is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be C. But wait the numbers. Because the restricted group had fewer cases. So C says controlling the amount (so restricting) makes them less likely. So that's accurate. So the answer is C.\n\nWait, but the first thought I had was conflicting. Initially, I thought maybe I misread but after correcting that all were exposed same radiation, then the conclusion is correct as C.\n\nSo the correct answer is C.\n\nBut let me check again. The problem says: \"the other half gave them the same food, but in a limited amount.\" So the restricted group had limited food. Then the leukemia in the restricted group was 3, which is much lower. So controlling the amount (limiting) reduces the likelihood. Therefore C is correct.\n\nYes. So the answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe experiment exposed 200 mice to the **same dose and intensity of radiation**, then split them into two groups: one unrestricted in food (55 leukemia cases) and one food-restricted (3 leukemia cases). The key conclusions are:\n\n- **All mice were exposed to radiation**, so the difference in leukemia rates stems from diet.\n- The **restricted diet reduced leukemia incidence** (3% vs. 55% in unrestricted), indicating that **controlling food intake mitigates the risk of radiation-induced leukemia**.\n\n**Analysis of options:**\n- **A:** Incorrect. The study does not compare mice exposed vs. unexposed to radiation, so it cannot generalize to \"whether exposed or not.\"\n- **B:** Incorrect. While radiation\u2019s role cannot be isolated, the experiment\u2019s design (all mice were exposed) does not support dismissing radiation\u2019s relevance entirely.\n- **C:** **Correct.** The restricted-diet group had far fewer leukemia cases, directly linking controlled food intake to reduced risk from radiation exposure.\n- **D:** Incorrect. The restricted group had only 3 cases (3%), so \"bound to develop leukemia\" is false.\n\n**Answer:** C) By controlling the amount of food these mice eat, they are less likely to develop leukemia from certain radiation exposure.",
