messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nRecently, a number of emotional intelligence education institutions have launched corresponding packages for different age groups. Emotional intelligence classes are popular among parents. Emotional intelligence is the ability to control and manage emotions, and it plays an important role in people's life and work. However, in the hearts of many people, the connotation of emotional intelligence has been alienated. The earliest concept of emotional intelligence is very different from the popular concept of emotional intelligence today. Many people's understanding of emotional intelligence is another way of saying sophistication and flattery. In fact, the core of emotional intelligence is not only the ability to recognize and control one's own emotions, but also the ability to communicate with others and integrate into the collective. The cultivation of these two abilities needs to be practiced in daily life. Whether children can develop good emotional control and social skills depends to a large extent on their parents, and no EQ training can replace the EQ training in daily life.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the followings is most likely to be described next?\n# Options:\nA) Emotional intelligence tutoring should arouse great attention from parents\nB) Parents' practice in family education\nC) Parents should view emotional intelligence training courses rationally\nD) Need to cultivate and improve parents' emotional intelligence", '<think>\nOkay, let me try to work through this problem step by step.The question is asking which option is most likely to be described next based on the given context..\n\nFirst, I need to understand the context thoroughly.The passage talks about emotional intelligence (EQ) education institutions offering packages for different age groups, and how EQ classes are popular among parents.It mentions that while EQ is about controlling emotions and its importance in life and work, there\'s a misunderstanding where people see EQ as just being sophisticated or good at flattery.The core of EQ is actually two abilities: recognizing and controlling one\'s own emotions, and communicating with others and integrating into a group.The passage then emphasizes that cultivating these abilities requires daily practice.Importantly, it states that whether children develop good emotional control and social skills depends largely on their parents, and no EQ training can replace the EQ training in daily life.\n\nThe question is asking what comes next.The options are A, B, C, D. Let me look at each option in light of the context.\n\nThe last sentence of the passage is crucial here: "Whether children can develop good emotional control and social skills depends to a large extent on their parents, and no EQ training can replace the EQ training in daily life."This seems to be leading towards a conclusion or a follow-up point about the role of parents in this process..\n\nOption B: "Parents\' practice in family education" – this seems to directly relate to the idea that parents play a big role and that daily life training is key.The passage mentions that parents are crucial, so maybe the next part would talk about how parents should practice this in family settings.\n\nOption C: "Parents should view emotional intelligence training courses rationally" – the passage does mention that the current understanding of EQ is alienated from the original concept, and that some see it as flattery.So maybe there\'s a caution against over-relying on courses, hence suggesting parents should be rational about the courses.The last sentence says that no EQ training (maybe referring to formal classes) can replace daily life training, so this could lead to advising parents to not rely solely on courses but to be rational about them..\n\nOption D: "Need to cultivate and improve parents\' emotional intelligence" – the passage says that children\'s EQ depends on parents, so maybe parents need to have their own EQ improved first.But the passage doesn\'t explicitly mention parents\' own EQ, just their role in providing the environment..\n\nOption A: "Emotional intelligence tutoring should arouse great attention from parents" – but the passage might be cautioning against just focusing on tutoring since it says that formal training can\'t replace daily practice.So maybe A is not the next point.\n\nNow, the last sentence contrasts the importance of parents with the inadequacy of EQ training programs.The author is likely to follow up by emphasizing the parents\' role in daily life, so maybe B or C. .\n\nThe original text\'s key point is that parents are crucial, and that daily life practice is essential, so the next sentence might elaborate on how parents should be practicing EQ in family education (B), or advising parents to not over-rely on courses (C)..\n\nThe passage\'s criticism of the current misunderstanding of EQ (seeing it as flattery) and the emphasis on parents\' role suggests that the next part could be a recommendation directed at parents.Since the last sentence says that no EQ training can replace daily life, it\'s possible the author is leading into the idea that parents need to take charge in their own family practices..\n\nAlternatively, if the author is pointing out that courses aren\'t enough, then maybe the next step is to tell parents to view these courses rationally (C)..\n\nHmm.The question is which is "most likely" to come next.The structure of the passage is: EQ education is popular, but misunderstood.The real core is two abilities that need daily practice.Then the conclusion is that children\'s development depends on parents, and formal training can\'t replace daily life..\n\nSo the logical next step would be to talk about what parents should do in their daily lives, hence B: Parents\' practice in family education..\n\nAlternatively, if the author is pointing out that parents are key, then maybe the next part is about improving parents\' own EQ (D), but the passage doesn\'t mention parents\' EQ, only their role in providing the environment..\n\nAlternatively, the author might argue that since EQ courses are popular but misunderstood, parents should be cautious (C).The last sentence says that courses can\'t replace daily training, so the author might be advising parents to not rely on courses but instead focus on their own role.Hence, C could be the answer..\n\nWait, the question is what is most likely to be described next.The flow is: the current state (courses popular, but misunderstood), the true definition, the need for daily practice, and the crucial role of parents.The next paragraph would logically discuss the parents\' role in family education (B) as an elaboration of the last point.Alternatively, the author might transition into advising parents to be rational about courses (C) because the previous paragraph ended by saying that courses can\'t replace daily practice..\n\nLooking at the options again, the last sentence is a strong statement that parents are key and formal training can\'t replace daily life.So the next part would probably elaborate on what parents should do in their daily lives.So B would be the next topic..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the author wants to caution against over-reliance on courses, so C would be the next point..\n\nHmm.Let me re-read the last sentence: "Whether children can develop.. depends to a large extent on their parents, and no EQ training can replace the EQ training in daily life."The "and" here connects two points: parents are crucial, and formal EQ training can\'t replace daily life.So the next sentence could address the parents\' role, so B. .\n\nAlternatively, the author might want to tell parents to not just rely on courses (C).Since the passage mentions that the connotation has been alienated, maybe the next point is to correct that misunderstanding by advising parents to view courses rationally..\n\nBut the last part specifically mentions that parents are key and formal training can\'t replace daily.So the next step would logically be discussing parents\' practices in family education (B)..\n\nAlternatively, the author might suggest that parents need to improve their own EQ first (D), but that\'s an assumption not directly stated..\n\nBetween B and C. The question is about what is most likely.The passage ends by emphasizing the importance of parents and daily practice, so the next topic would be about how parents can practice that in family settings.So B is the answer..\n\nWait, but option C says "Parents should view emotional intelligence training courses rationally."The passage does mention that the current understanding is alienated and that courses might be part of that misunderstanding.So if the author is pointing out that courses can\'t replace daily training, then advising parents to view courses rationally (not to overvalue them) would follow..\n\nHmm.Let me think again.The passage says that EQ classes are popular among parents, but the real EQ is different from what\'s popular.Then the author explains the true core, and says that parents are key, and courses can\'t replace daily practice.So the next logical step is to tell parents not to rely too much on courses and to focus on their own role.That would be option C. .\n\nAlternatively, the author could go into detail about what parents should do (B)..\n\nThe question is which is more likely.Since the last sentence contrasts the role of parents with the inadequacy of EQ training (courses), the author might next advise parents to be rational about the courses (C), because they are popular but not sufficient..\n\nAlternatively, the author might explain that parents need to practice in family education (B), which would be the solution..\n\nThe structure is: .\n\n1.EQ education is popular, but misunderstood..\n\n2.True EQ is about two abilities needing daily practice..\n\n3.Parents are crucial, courses can\'t replace daily life..\n\nThe next step would be to elaborate on how parents should act in daily life (B), or to caution about over-reliance on courses (C)..\n\nThe key is that the last sentence says "no EQ training can replace the EQ training in daily life".So the author is making a point against over-reliance on formal training.Therefore, the next sentence would likely be advising parents to view these courses rationally (C), because they are popular but not sufficient..\n\nAlternatively, the author might go into the parents\' practices (B)..\n\nHmm, perhaps the answer is C. Because the passage starts by saying that the emotional intelligence education institutions have launched packages, which are popular among parents.However, the author is critical of this because the real EQ requires daily practice and parents\' role.So the author would probably advise parents to not just follow the trend (the courses) but to be rational about them..\n\nTherefore, option C: Parents should view EQ training courses rationally..\n\nAlternatively, if the author is going to explain how parents should do it (B), but the transition from the last sentence would need to be more explicit..\n\nThe question is about what is most likely next.Since the last part ends with the importance of parents and the insufficiency of courses, the immediate next point would be advising parents to be rational about the courses (C)..\n\nAlternatively, the author might first explain what parents should do (B) as the next step..\n\nI think I\'m torn between B and C. Let me check the options again..\n\nOption B is "Parents\' practice in family education" – that\'s a statement, so perhaps the next paragraph would elaborate on that.The last sentence ends by saying parents are crucial, so the next part could discuss their practices..\n\nAlternatively, the author might first make a recommendation (C) before explaining how..\n\nThe flow of the original text is: .\n\n- Current popularity of EQ courses, but misunderstanding..\n\n- True EQ is X and Y, requiring daily practice..\n\n- Parents are key, courses can\'t replace daily life..\n\nTherefore, the next paragraph could start by saying "Parents should view EQ courses rationally.." (C) because the author is pointing out that courses aren\'t enough..\n\nAlternatively, the author could explain how parents should practice in family education (B)..\n\nIn terms of logical flow, after stating that parents are crucial and courses can\'t replace their role, the next step is to tell parents to not rely on courses (C) or to tell them how to practice (B)..\n\nThe question is which is more likely.Since the passage already mentions that the connotation has been alienated (people think EQ is flattery), and the author is correcting that, the next step could be advising parents to be rational about the courses (C), which are part of the alienated understanding..\n\nAlternatively, the author might proceed to discuss the parents\' role in detail.The author\'s critique of EQ courses being overvalued and the emphasis on parents\' crucial role in daily life suggest that the next step would likely involve cautioning against overreliance on courses (C) rather than emphasizing parents\' practices (B).. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
