      "text": " D\n\nWait, no, actually, the passage says Wittgenstein was considered the best because he had so many questions. Later, he became more successful than Russell because Russell had no problem. So the idea is that having questions (finding problems) is necessary for progress. So the premise is that finding problems allows you to keep progressing. Hence, the correct answer is D. But let me check the options again:\n\nA) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success.\n\nThe passage is about the importance of questioning and problem-finding leading to success. So both A and D are close. The key difference is that A is about doubting and questioning as the secret, while D says finding problems is the premise for progress.\n\nThe original story's conclusion is that Russell fell behind because he had \"no problem.\" The implication is that without having problems (i.e., not identifying problems to solve), one can't progress. So the main point is that finding problems is essential for progress, which is D.\n\nAlternatively, the \"endless questions\" part points to the questioning aspect, but the final line by Wittgenstein is about Russell having \"no problem.\" The phrase \"finding problems\" is directly related to that. So D seems more precise.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe paragraph emphasizes the importance of questioning and identifying problems for success. Moore identifies Wittgenstein as his best student because of his endless questions, and Wittgenstein attributes Russell's stagnation to his lack of problems (\"he has no problem\"). The key takeaway is that **finding problems is essential for continuous progress**, as opposed to complacency without inquiry. \n\n**Answer: D) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress.**",
