ernatively, let\'s try to assign genders without considering E\'s contradiction for a moment.\n\nAssuming the three girls are B, C, and E. Then:.\n\nA is male, has three sisters (B, C, E).That works.\n\nC is a girl with two sisters (B and E).Good.\n\nE is a girl with no sister?Yes, she has no sisters (B and C).So that\'s not a problem.So E must be a girl?But the problem says she is not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe E is not the fourth girl, making three girls, so A has two sisters (so one girl is included).But that doesn\'t fit.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the "three sisters" of A are B, C, and D (if D is male).Then D is a boy, making the boys B, C, D, E?Yes, that would be four.\n\nThis is really straightforward.Maybe the key is to consider the E contradiction and disregard the other clues.\n\nLet me try to limit the options:.\n\nOption B says D is a man.Let\'s see.\n\nIf D is the youngest, but gender is specified.Suppose D is male.Then the boys would be B, C, D, and possibly E. But that might exceed three.\n\nAlternatively, if D is female, then the girls are A and D, boys B, C, E. Then E has no sisters (B and C), confirming her having no sisters.So D must be male to increase the number of boys?Wait, if D is a boy, then boys are B, C, D, and E (if E is a boy with sisters, but that can be).Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, if D is male, and E is a boy?Yes, the problem says E is a boy.\n\nAlternatively, maybe E is a sister of A.But they are all individuals.\n\nThis is going straight forward.Let\'s try to see which option cannot be correct.\n\nOption B: D is a man.\n\nIf D is the youngest, but gender is stated.Let\'s see if we can deduce.\n\nFrom B\'s statement: B has no brothers.If B is male (as deduced earlier), then his sisters are A and another girl.So the other girl must be D or someone else.If the girls are A and D, then D is female.So D is female.Then the boys are B, C, E. But E would then have no sisters (B and C), confirming her having no sisters.So that\'s good.\n\nAlternative