e ignore E\'s statement, then three boys (B, C, and another), two girls.So boys are more.But the problem states E is a boy with no brother, which simplifies.If we proceed without considering that confirmation (maybe it\'s a mistake), then C could be incorrect.\n\nD) A ranks third among the six.Probably a typo for five, but even so, we don\'t have age info except D is not oldest.So can say third.\n\nThe question asks which cannot be concluded.If the problem\'s E is a confirmation and should be a girl, then E is female, making girls A and E (two), boys B, C, D (three).Then:.\n\n- A\'s two sisters: B, C.\n\n- B is male (has no brothers: A and E).\n\n- C is a boy with no sisters (B and D).\n\n- D is male.\n\n- E is female with no brothers?Wait, the problem says E is a boy.So that\'s not possible.\n\nAlternatively, if the last confirmation is about E having no siblings, but that can be.\n\nGiven the straightforwardness, maybe the answer is B) D is a man.\n\nWait, let\'s see:.\n\nIf D is male, then:.\n\nBoys: B, C, D, E (four).But A has two sisters, so that\'s not okay (he can have two of them).C has no sisters (B and D, for example), but E would have two sisters (B and C).So this works.\n\nAlternatively, if D is female:.\n\nBoys: B, C, E (three).Girls: A and D (two).Then E has no sisters (B and C), confirming her "no sister".\n\nTherefore, the only way E has no sisters is if she\'s the only boy, but then A would have no sisters, which confirms.\n\nTherefore, there must be a mistake in the problem\'s confirmations.\n\nPerhaps the correct answer is B) D is a man.Let me see:.\n\nIf D is male, then boys are B, C, D (three), and E is a boy with sisters.Wait, E would be the fourth boy, making him have two sisters, confirming.So yes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the "two sisters" of A are B, C, and E, making D a girl.Then:.\n\n- A is female, has B, C, E as sisters (three boys).\n\n- B is male (has sisters A and D).\n\n- C is a boy with no sisters (B and E).\n\n- E is a boy with no sisters (B and C).So he does have sisters, confirming his having none.\n\nAh!So the only way E can have no sister is if he is not part of the three sisters of A.But since A has two sisters, and there are five individuals, that would require E to be a sister, so he must be counted.This is possible.\n\nPerhaps the key is that "He has brother or sister" is referring to E. If E has neither, then he has no siblings.But he\'s part of five, so that\'s possible.Unless the "He" is a mistake and it\'s "E has brother or sister".So E has siblings, making him not an only child, but he\'s part of five siblings.This is possible, so the only conclusion is that the problem has no errors, but we have to choose the worst possible option.\n\nLooking at the options again:.\n\nOption B says D is a man.If D is male, then boys are B, C, D, E (four).Girls are A, so that\'s not okay (he could have B, C, D as sisters).Then C has no sisters (B and D), so that\'s not okay.E, being a boy, would have two sisters (B, C, D), but he is supposed to have no sisters.So that\'s not good.\n\nAlternatively, if D is female, then boys are B, C, E (three).Girls are A and D (two).Then E has no sisters (B and C), confirming his "no sister".\n\nTherefore, the only way to resolve this is if the "two sisters" of A include E, but he must be counted.\n\nWait, unless E is the one who has brother or sister.If the last confirmation is about E: "E has brother or sister", then E has siblings.But he\'s part of five, so that\'s not possible.So maybe the last confirmation is about someone else, like D?If D has siblings, then D is not an only child, but again yes.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the "two sisters" of A are B, C, and D (if D is male).Then E is a boy with sisters (but he would have two sisters: B, C, D).So no.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem\'s confirmation for E is that he has brothers, but has no sisters.Then he could be part of the three sisters of A, and have two sisters.But the problem says he has no sister.\n\nI think the key is to look for the answer that cannot be derived despite contradictions.Let\'s see:.\n\nOption B: D is a man.If we assume D is male, then boys are B, C, D, and E (four), but A has two sisters, so that\'s not okay (he could have B, C, D as sisters).Then C has no sisters (B and D), so that\'s not okay.E, being a boy, would have two sisters (B, C, D), but he is supposed to have no sisters.So that\'s not good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the "two sisters" of A are B, C, and D, making E a girl, but then E would have four sisters, contradicting.So no.\n\nAlternatively, the only way E has no sister is if he is not part of the four sisters of A, but he must be counted.\n\nTherefore, the answer cannot be B) D is a man?Or C) More boys than girls.\n\nWait, if girls are A and another (say D is female), then boys are three (B, C, E), making more boys (3 vs 2 girls).So option C would be incorrect.Even though E confirms, maybe the problem expects us to overlook that and go with the numbers.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is not C) There are more boys than girls.\n\nBut let me check again:.\n\nIf A has two sisters, so two boys.\n\nB has no sisters, so that\'s a problem.\n\nC is a boy with no sisters, so that\'s good.\n\nE is a boy with no sister → confirmation, but if we ignore that, then two boys and three girls → C is incorrect.\n\nThe question is which conclusion cannot be drawn from the description.Even with the confirmation, the numbers still suggest two boys and three girls, so C is incorrect.The confirmation might be a trick, but the options might not require all statements to be consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the "He" in the last confirmation is E, and it\'s a translation error, so E has siblings, making him not an only child, but then A\'s two sisters exist.So that can be. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
