      "text": " D\n\nWait, let me check again. The researcher's point is that if the survey is well-constructed (so questions don't indicate expected answers), then the respondents' desire to meet expectations doesn't affect the results. But the flaw here might be that even if the survey doesn't indicate the expectations, the respondents could still have beliefs (even if incorrect) about what the surveyor expects. For example, maybe they assume the surveyor wants a positive response, so they answer accordingly\u2014regardless of the wording. The argument assumes that if there's no indication, then their beliefs are correct (i.e., that there are no expectations), but maybe their beliefs are wrong but still influence their answers. That's exactly what option D says: \"The answers can be influenced by beliefs about the surveyor's expectations even if those beliefs are unfounded.\"\n\nYes, that makes sense. The researcher is assuming that if the survey doesn't provide indications, then the respondents' beliefs about the surveyor's expectations would be correct (or nonexistent), but the possibility is that people might still have incorrect beliefs. Hence, the argument overlooks D. \n\nOption B is about people trying to oppose, but the argument addresses the desire to meet expectations, not to oppose them. So even if some do the opposite, that's still influenced by their belief, but perhaps the researcher's point is that the desire to meet won't affect it. But if their belief is unfounded but still present, then their desire to meet (even based on wrong beliefs) would still impact the results, which the researcher didn't account for. So D is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe researcher's argument assumes that if a survey is well-constructed (i.e., questions do not indicate expected answers), respondents' desire to meet the surveyor's expectations will not influence results. However, this reasoning overlooks the possibility that respondents might still form beliefs about the surveyor's expectations **even if those beliefs are incorrect**. For instance, respondents could incorrectly assume what the surveyor expects (e.g., preferring socially acceptable answers) and adjust their responses accordingly, regardless of the survey's wording. The argument fails to consider that influence can arise from **unfounded beliefs**, not just explicit cues in the questions. \n\n**Correct Answer:**  \n**D) The answers of opinion-survey respondents can be influenced by beliefs about the surveyor's expectations even if those beliefs are unfounded**",
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "stop_reason": null,
      "prompt_logprobs": null
    }
  ],
