ct. Let me check the options again for any clues.\n\nThe question is in Chinese medical terminology maybe? \"Disturbance of perceptive synthesis\" might be a specific term in psychology or psychiatry. Let me think about possible psychological conditions.\n\nWait, the term \"perceptive synthesis\" might refer to the brain's integration of sensory information. A disturbance could mean that the overall object is recognized, but specific features (like size, shape) are misperceived. \n\nFor example, someone might see a car correctly as a car (whole object), but perceive it as being twice as large as it is. That would be a disturbance in perceptive synthesis for size.\n\nIn option B, the movement is the attribute. If the pendulum is swinging but perceived as still, that's a dynamic attribute. So that's a possible answer.\n\nOption D's color weakness is a static attribute. Since the definition includes color, D is also possible.\n\nBut maybe the question's options are structured so that only one is correct. Let me see if there's a better fit.\n\nThe question says the patient is correct as an objective whole. So in D's case, the person can see the object (e.g., an apple) as an apple but can't tell its color. That fits. But color weakness is a sensory issue. The question's definition doesn't specify that it's not due to sensory issues. So in that case, D would be correct.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is that \"perceptive synthesis\" refers to higher-level processing, so color weakness is a lower-level issue. But the question's definition explicitly lists color as an example attribute, so that would include it.\n\nTherefore, both B and D fit. But since it's a single-answer question, perhaps I made a mistake.\n\nWait, the options are in Chinese, but the translation might have nuances. Let me think again.\n\nThe problem states that the disturbance is in the perception of the attributes of the real thing. So for B, the pendulum is real and swinging, but the patient's perce